import{t as e}from"./links-ClIw6rLF.js";var t={id:`F4`,title:`Electronic Components for Inverter Engineering`,level:`beginner`,stage:`foundation`,summary:`Passive, semiconductor, magnetic, protection, control, sensing and thermal components: how they work, their ratings, how to test them and how to identify faults and replacements.`,prerequisites:[`F2`],recommended:[`F3`],status:`published`,phase:9,competency:`CU-04`,modules:[{id:`F4.M1`,title:`Passive components`,summary:`Resistors, capacitors and inductors: how each stores or dissipates energy, how to read their markings and ratings, and how to test each one out of circuit.`,lessons:[{id:`F4.M1.L1`,title:`Resistors: types, codes, power, tolerance`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Read a four-band and five-band resistor colour code to find value and tolerance.`,`Explain why a resistor's power rating, not just its resistance value, must be checked for a given circuit.`,`Compare the common resistor construction types (carbon film, metal film, wirewound) and where each is used in an inverter.`,`State the practical consequence of exceeding a resistor's power rating.`],introduction:`You met Ohm's law and resistance as ideas in F1. This lesson treats the [[resistance|resistor]] as a physical PART you will hold, read, select and test: how its value and [[tolerance|tolerance]] are marked, why its [[power-rating|power rating]] matters just as much as its ohm value, and which construction type belongs where in an inverter — from the gate-resistor on a MOSFET to the high-power bleeder across a DC bus.`,lecture:`### Reading the colour code

A resistor's value is almost always printed as coloured bands, not as text (it is too small to print legibly on a tiny part). The standard four-band code is: **band 1** (first digit), **band 2** (second digit), **band 3** (multiplier, ×10^n), **band 4** (tolerance). Each colour stands for a digit: black=0, brown=1, red=2, orange=3, yellow=4, green=5, blue=6, violet=7, grey=8, white=9. A five-band resistor (tighter, more precise tolerances) adds a third digit before the multiplier band. The tolerance band is usually gold (±5 %), silver (±10 %), red (±2 %) or brown (±1 %).

### Why power rating matters as much as resistance

A resistor's job is to dissipate the power P = I²R (or V²/R) as heat. Two resistors can share the same marked value and tolerance yet have very different power ratings (commonly 1/8 W, 1/4 W, 1 W, 5 W, 10 W, up to tens of watts for a bleeder or current-sense resistor) simply because they are physically different sizes — more surface area and often a larger body sheds heat faster. Fitting a resistor whose power rating is too low for the actual current it will carry is one of the most common, entirely preventable component failures: it overheats, drifts in value, discolours, and eventually opens or chars the board around it.

### Construction types and where each belongs

- **Carbon film**: cheap, general-purpose, modest tolerance (commonly ±5 %) and modest noise performance — fine for bias and pull-up/pull-down resistors in control circuitry.
- **Metal film**: tighter tolerance (±1 % common), lower temperature coefficient, lower noise — used wherever the actual value matters precisely, such as in a voltage-sensing divider (F4.M5) where accuracy directly sets measurement accuracy.
- **Wirewound**: a resistance wire wound on a former, able to dissipate real power (watts to tens of watts) in a small body — used for bleeder resistors, current-shunt resistors and gate resistors that must survive repeated switching pulses, though its own small self-inductance can matter at high switching frequencies.

### Resistors as gate resistors

A small series resistor between a gate driver (F4.M2.L6) and a MOSFET or IGBT gate — the "gate resistor" — slows the gate's charge and discharge rate, trading switching speed (and switching loss) against ringing and electromagnetic noise. Its own power rating is normally small, since gate charge/discharge energy is tiny, but its resistance VALUE is a real design choice, not an arbitrary round number.`,technical:`**Temperature coefficient of resistance (TCR).** Every resistor's value drifts slightly with temperature, quoted in ppm/°C. Metal film types have a far lower TCR than carbon film, which is why precision sensing circuits specify metal film even though both might show the same room-temperature tolerance band.

**Surface-mount (SMD) resistors** use a numeric code instead of colour bands (e.g. "103" = 10 × 10³ = 10,000 Ω = 10 kΩ; "4R7" = 4.7 Ω), printed directly on the tiny ceramic body where space allows, or left completely unmarked below a certain size — identification then relies on circuit position and an in-circuit or desoldered measurement.`,diagrams:[{id:`series-parallel`,caption:`Resistors in series and parallel, and how their combined value and power handling differ from a single equivalent resistor.`}],formulas:[{name:`Four-band value`,expr:`R = (10·d1 + d2) × 10^mult  Ω`,where:[[`d1`,`first digit`,``],[`d2`,`second digit`,``],[`mult`,`multiplier band exponent`,``]]},{name:`Power dissipated`,expr:`P = I²R = V²/R`,where:[[`P`,`power dissipated as heat`,`W`]],note:`Must stay below the resistor's rated power, with margin (derating, F3.M5/F4.M5.L3 thermal context).`}],examples:[{title:`Decoding a four-band resistor`,problem:`A resistor shows yellow, violet, orange, gold. Find its value and tolerance.`,steps:[`Yellow = 4, violet = 7 → "47"`,`Orange multiplier = ×10³`,`R = 47 × 1000 = 47,000 Ω`,`Gold tolerance band = ±5 %`],answer:`47 kΩ ± 5 % (44.65 kΩ to 49.35 kΩ).`},{title:`Checking a power rating is adequate`,problem:`A 100 Ω bleeder resistor is placed across a 400 V DC bus. Is a standard 2 W resistor adequate?`,steps:[`P = V²/R = 400²/100 = 160,000/100`,`= 1600 W`],answer:`No — 1600 W vastly exceeds a 2 W rating; the resistor would fail almost instantly. A bleeder at this voltage and resistance needs either a far higher resistance value (to limit power) or multiple high-power resistors in series/parallel, sized and derated correctly.`,check:`A 2 W part surviving 1600 W is off by nearly three orders of magnitude — a sanity check alone flags the mismatch before any detailed design.`}],applications:`Every reference you will use in the Component Library and in the Repair Workshop's resistor-related checkpoints assumes you can read a value off the part itself, not just look it up — exactly this skill.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating tolerance as "basically irrelevant" for a general-purpose resistor.`,correction:`Tolerance sets the real worst-case range of behaviour; in a sensing divider or timing circuit it directly limits the achievable accuracy.`},{mistake:`Assuming any resistor of the right ohm value will do, regardless of power rating.`,correction:`Power rating must be checked separately against the actual current/voltage in the circuit, with margin.`},{mistake:`Reading colour bands in the wrong direction.`,correction:`The tolerance band (gold/silver/red/brown) is always read last; start from the opposite end, nearer a lead, with the tolerance band itself usually spaced slightly apart from the value bands.`},{mistake:`Assuming a resistor that measures "roughly right" on a meter is definitely healthy.`,correction:`A resistor can drift within tolerance from age/heat and still mislead a quick check; compare against both the colour-code value and the circuit's expected behaviour (F4.M1.L4).`}],demo:{title:`Read resistors in the library`,text:`Open the resistor entry in the Component Library and compare marked values against the colour-code method above.`,link:e.lib(`Resistor`,`resistor`)},activities:[e.lib(`Resistor`,`resistor`),e.ckt(`Battery and resistor circuit`,`ckt-01`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Value (Ω) of a resistor coded brown, black, red?`,answer:1e3,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`10 × 10² = 1000 Ω.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A gold fourth band means tolerance of...`,options:[`±5 %`,`±1 %`,`±10 %`,`±20 %`],answer:0,explain:`Gold = ±5 %.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why might two resistors of the same value and tolerance have very different physical sizes?`,options:[`Different power ratings`,`Different colours`,`Different manufacturers always differ`,`There is no reason, they must be identical`],answer:0,explain:`A higher power rating usually needs a larger body to dissipate heat.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Metal film resistors are preferred over carbon film where...`,options:[`precise, stable value matters (e.g. a sensing divider)`,`maximum power dissipation is needed`,`cost must be minimised above all else`,`the resistor carries no current`],answer:0,explain:`Metal film gives tighter tolerance and lower temperature drift.`}],summary:[`A resistor's value and tolerance are read from colour bands (or a numeric code on SMD parts), not printed in full.`,`Power rating is a separate check from resistance value — both must suit the actual circuit.`,`Carbon film, metal film and wirewound constructions trade cost, precision and power handling differently.`,`Exceeding a resistor's power rating causes overheating, value drift and eventual failure — a common, preventable fault.`],homework:{id:`F4.M1.L1.hw`,title:`Resistor code and power practice`,brief:`Decode values and check power ratings.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Value (Ω) of red, red, brown?`,answer:220,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`22 × 10¹ = 220 Ω.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power dissipated by a 10 Ω resistor carrying 2 A (W)?`,answer:40,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`P = I²R = 4×10 = 40 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`A technician replaces a failed 10 Ω, 1 W current-sense resistor with another 10 Ω resistor pulled from a drawer of mixed parts, without checking its power rating. Explain the risk in this practice and how to avoid repeating the same failure.`,model:`The replacement resistor may have the correct resistance value but an inadequate power rating for the actual current in that circuit, in which case it will overheat and fail again (possibly damaging nearby parts or causing a fire risk) even though "the right value" was fitted. The correct practice is to check BOTH the resistance value and the power rating (with margin/derating) against the circuit's actual current and voltage, not just match the ohm value, and ideally to investigate why the original resistor failed before assuming a like-for-like replacement is sufficient.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M1.L2`,title:`Capacitors: electrolytic, film and ceramic; ESR; polarity`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Compare electrolytic, film and ceramic capacitors by construction, capacitance range, ESR and polarity.`,`Explain why reversing polarity on an electrolytic capacitor is dangerous, not just "wrong".`,`Define ESR and explain why it is the key aging indicator for electrolytic capacitors.`,`Identify which capacitor type belongs on the DC bus versus the AC/switching side of an inverter.`],introduction:`Capacitors appear everywhere in an inverter: smoothing the DC bus, filtering the switched output, decoupling control electronics. This lesson compares the three families you will meet in the Component Library and the Repair Workshop — [[electrolytic-capacitor|electrolytic]], [[film-capacitor|film]] and [[ceramic-capacitor|ceramic]] — and introduces [[esr|ESR]], the single most useful aging indicator for the type that fails most often: the electrolytic bus capacitor.`,lecture:`### The three families

- **Electrolytic**: achieves high capacitance (µF to many mF) in a compact can using a liquid or gel electrolyte. **Polarised** — the can is marked with a stripe showing the negative lead, and reversing it is not merely "wrong wiring", it can cause the electrolyte to break down, generate gas, and vent or rupture the can. Used for DC bus smoothing ([[bus-capacitor|bus capacitors]]) where bulk capacitance matters more than precision.
- **Film**: a thin plastic dielectric, not polarised, low ESR, stable value, long-lived — but larger per microfarad than an electrolytic. Used on the AC/switching side and in snubber and filter applications where low loss and long life matter more than raw capacitance per volume.
- **Ceramic**: a ceramic dielectric, not polarised, very low ESR, compact at small-to-moderate values — used for high-frequency decoupling on control boards, though some ceramic types lose a meaningful fraction of their capacitance as applied DC voltage rises (a datasheet characteristic, not a fault).

### ESR: the aging electrolytic's tell

An ideal capacitor has zero resistance in series with its capacitance. A real one has a small [[esr|equivalent series resistance (ESR)]], mostly from the electrolyte and internal construction. As an electrolytic capacitor ages — especially under heat and ripple current — its electrolyte slowly dries out, and its ESR rises steadily, long before its capacitance value itself falls far out of tolerance. Rising ESR means the capacitor heats itself more under the same ripple current (P = I²·ESR), which accelerates further drying in a feedback loop, and it becomes progressively less effective at absorbing the fast switching currents the DC bus needs — the classic, slow "aged electrolytic capacitor" fault you will meet directly in the Repair Workshop.

### Why polarity matters this much

Forward (correct) polarity on an electrolytic maintains a thin internal oxide layer that IS the dielectric. Reverse polarity (even briefly, at voltage) breaks this layer down electrochemically, generating heat and gas inside a sealed can — the mechanism behind the characteristic bulging top or vented case of a reverse-biased or over-voltage electrolytic failure, and the reason this fault carries its own safety handling (discharge and isolate before any suspect electrolytic is touched or removed).`,technical:`**Ripple current rating.** An electrolytic capacitor's datasheet specifies a maximum RMS ripple current it can carry continuously at a stated temperature and frequency — exceeding it heats the part internally via I²·ESR even if the applied DC voltage is well within its voltage rating. Bus capacitors are often chosen (or paralleled) as much for ripple-current capability as for bulk capacitance.

**Capacitors in series and parallel.** Paralleled capacitors add capacitance directly (C_total = C1 + C2 + …) and divide the total ripple current between them, which is why multiple smaller bus capacitors in parallel are common instead of one large one. Series capacitors add in reciprocal (like resistors in parallel) and divide the total voltage between them — occasionally used to reach a voltage rating beyond any single part, with balancing resistors to equalise the voltage split.`,diagrams:[{id:`rc-curve`,caption:`A capacitor charging and discharging through a resistance, and where ESR sits in the equivalent circuit of a real (non-ideal) capacitor.`}],formulas:[{name:`ESR heating`,expr:`P_ESR = I_rms² · ESR`,where:[[`P_ESR`,`heat generated inside the capacitor by its own ESR`,`W`],[`I_rms`,`RMS ripple current through it`,`A`]],note:`Rising ESR with age directly increases self-heating at the same ripple current — a feedback loop that accelerates further aging.`},{name:`Parallel capacitance`,expr:`C_total = C1 + C2 + …`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`ESR self-heating as a capacitor ages`,problem:`A bus capacitor carries 3 A RMS ripple. Compare its self-heating when new (ESR = 0.05 Ω) against aged (ESR = 0.4 Ω).`,steps:[`New: P = 3² × 0.05 = 9 × 0.05 = 0.45 W`,`Aged: P = 9 × 0.4 = 3.6 W`],answer:`Self-heating rises from 0.45 W to 3.6 W — eightfold — at the exact same ripple current, purely from ESR aging.`,check:`Power scales linearly with ESR for a fixed current, so an 8× ESR rise gives an 8× power rise; consistent with 0.4/0.05 = 8.`},{title:`Choosing a capacitor family for a DC bus`,problem:`A 400 V DC bus needs 1000 µF of bulk smoothing capacitance. Which family is appropriate, and why not ceramic?`,steps:[`Required capacitance (1000 µF) is far beyond what a ceramic capacitor can practically provide at this voltage in a sensible size`,`Film capacitors can reach this range but are physically large and costly at high µF values`,`Electrolytic capacitors are the practical, compact, cost-effective choice for bulk bus smoothing at this scale`],answer:`Electrolytic — it is the only family that reaches the needed bulk capacitance in a practical size and cost at this voltage, accepting the trade-off of polarity sensitivity and ESR aging as a known, managed limitation.`}],applications:`The Repair Workshop's "dried-out smoothing capacitor" scenario is built entirely around this lesson's ESR concept: the live reading you will take there is exactly an ESR-style comparison between a healthy and an aged bus capacitor.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a capacitor that still reads close to its rated capacitance on a basic meter is definitely healthy.`,correction:`Capacitance can stay close to nominal while ESR has risen significantly; ESR, not just capacitance, is the key aging indicator for electrolytics (F4.M1.L4).`},{mistake:`Treating "polarised" as just a wiring convention to get right, with no real consequence if reversed.`,correction:`Reverse polarity on an electrolytic can cause electrochemical breakdown, gas generation and venting — a genuine safety hazard, not only a non-functioning circuit.`},{mistake:`Using a ceramic capacitor wherever a film capacitor is specified, assuming "capacitor is a capacitor".`,correction:`The three families differ in polarity sensitivity, ESR, voltage-dependent capacitance and practical size per microfarad — the specified family is rarely interchangeable without re-checking the application.`}],demo:{title:`Compare capacitor types in the library`,text:`Open the capacitor entry and compare the electrolytic, film and ceramic families against this lesson's descriptions.`,link:e.lib(`Capacitor`,`capacitor`)},activities:[e.lib(`Capacitor`,`capacitor`),e.ckt(`Capacitor charging and discharging`,`ckt-03`),e.repair(`Aged/sulfated battery (ESR-style aging)`,`rsc-05`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which capacitor family is polarised and must never have reversed voltage applied?`,options:[`Electrolytic`,`Film`,`Ceramic`,`All of them equally`],answer:0,explain:`Electrolytic capacitors are polarised; film and ceramic normally are not.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`ESR is best described as...`,options:[`the small series resistance inside a real capacitor`,`the capacitor's voltage rating`,`a type of capacitor`,`the ripple frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Equivalent series resistance: a real-world non-ideality of a real capacitor.`},{id:`k3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`ESR self-heating (W) for 2 A RMS ripple through 0.2 Ω ESR?`,answer:.8,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`P = I²·ESR = 4 × 0.2 = 0.8 W.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`As an electrolytic capacitor ages under heat and ripple, its ESR typically...`,options:[`rises steadily, well before capacitance falls far out of tolerance`,`falls steadily`,`stays exactly constant until sudden failure`,`has no relationship to capacitance`],answer:0,explain:`Drying electrolyte raises ESR progressively — the classic aging signature.`}],summary:[`Electrolytic capacitors give high capacitance in a small size but are polarised and age via rising ESR.`,`Film capacitors are unpolarised, low-ESR and long-lived, but larger per microfarad.`,`Ceramic capacitors are unpolarised and very low-ESR, used for high-frequency decoupling at smaller values.`,`Rising ESR, not falling capacitance, is the primary real-world aging signature of a failing electrolytic.`],homework:{id:`F4.M1.L2.hw`,title:`Capacitor family and ESR practice`,brief:`Apply ESR heating and choose the right family.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`ESR self-heating (W) for 1.5 A RMS through 0.3 Ω ESR?`,answer:.675,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P = 1.5² × 0.3 = 2.25 × 0.3 = 0.675 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`A colleague suggests fitting a ceramic capacitor in place of a failed 2200 µF electrolytic bus capacitor because "ceramic capacitors are more reliable and don't age". Explain why this substitution is not practical, referring to what each family can actually provide.`,model:`Reliability is not the limiting factor here — capacity is. A ceramic capacitor cannot practically provide 2200 µF of capacitance at bus voltage in any reasonable physical size; the families differ fundamentally in how much capacitance per unit volume they can deliver at a given voltage. The correct fix is a like-for-like (or better-rated) electrolytic replacement, chosen for adequate ripple-current rating and voltage rating, not a substitution to a different capacitor family that cannot meet the bulk-capacitance requirement at all.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M1.L3`,title:`Inductors: cores, saturation, current rating`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain how an inductor stores energy in a magnetic field and resists a change in current.`,`Define saturation and explain its consequence for an inductor's effective inductance and the current it then draws.`,`Compare common core materials (iron powder, ferrite) and the trade-offs each makes.`,`State why an inductor's current rating, not only its inductance value, limits where it can be used.`],introduction:`An inductor is the "other" energy-storage component, alongside the capacitor, and the two are deliberately paired in the output [[lc-filter|LC filter]] that turns a switched waveform into something close to a clean sine (F3.M5, P3). This lesson covers the physical inductor itself: its core, its [[saturation|saturation]] limit, and why its current rating is just as critical as its inductance value.`,lecture:`### Storing energy in a magnetic field

A [[current|current]] flowing through a coiled [[winding|winding]] creates a magnetic field in and around the coil; energy is stored in that field (E = ½LI²). An inductor resists a CHANGE in current (the dual of a capacitor, which resists a change in voltage) — exactly the property an output choke uses to smooth the current drawn from a switching bridge into something closer to a steady DC or slowly-varying AC value.

### Core materials and saturation

Most practical inductors wind the coil around a magnetic core (iron powder, laminated steel, or [[ferrite|ferrite]]) to concentrate the [[magnetic-flux|magnetic flux]] and achieve far more inductance for the same number of turns than an air-core coil would. But every core material has a limit: above some flux density, the core material [[saturation|saturates]] — it can carry no more flux no matter how much more current flows, so the inductor's EFFECTIVE inductance collapses sharply, and current then rises steeply and uncontrollably for the same applied voltage, since the circuit has effectively lost its current-limiting inductance. A saturating output inductor under overload is a genuine fault mode, not just a design corner case: it is one reason output inductors carry an explicit current rating.

- **Iron powder cores**: moderate permeability, graceful ("soft") saturation behaviour, used widely for output chokes where some tolerance of overcurrent without catastrophic behaviour is valued.
- **Ferrite cores**: higher permeability for a given size (so a smaller part for the same inductance) but sharper saturation onset, and low loss at the high switching frequencies used in SPWM — the usual choice for the higher-frequency inductors inside a sine-wave inverter's filter stage.

### Current rating versus inductance value

An inductor's datasheet value (say, 1.2 mH) describes its behaviour only up to its rated current — beyond that, saturation changes the picture entirely. Selecting (or replacing) an inductor purely by matching the inductance value, while ignoring its current rating, is the same class of mistake as matching a resistor's ohm value while ignoring its power rating (F4.M1.L1): both ratings must independently suit the actual circuit.`,technical:`**DC resistance (DCR).** Every real winding has some resistance, causing I²·R copper loss and a small, usually unavoidable voltage drop under load — this is the same winding-resistance reading used in the inductor's own test procedure (F4.M1.L4) to detect an open or partially broken winding.

**Saturation current rating (I_sat) versus RMS current rating.** Datasheets often distinguish between the current at which the core begins to saturate (I_sat, sometimes defined as a stated percentage inductance drop) and the current the winding can carry continuously without overheating (I_rms, a copper-loss/thermal limit) — the lower of the two is the part's true practical limit in a given application.`,diagrams:[{id:`rl-curve`,caption:`Current rising in an inductor over time, and how the curve steepens sharply once the core saturates and the effective inductance collapses.`}],formulas:[{name:`Stored energy`,expr:`E = ½ L I²`,where:[[`E`,`energy stored in the magnetic field`,`J`],[`L`,`inductance`,`H`],[`I`,`current`,`A`]]},{name:`Copper loss`,expr:`P_cu = I_rms² · R_dc`,where:[[`R_dc`,`winding DC resistance`,`Ω`]]}],examples:[{title:`Stored energy in an output choke`,problem:`An output inductor of 1.5 mH carries 8 A. Energy stored?`,steps:[`E = ½ L I²`,`= ½ × 0.0015 × 64`],answer:`≈ 0.048 J (48 mJ).`},{title:`Why saturation causes a steep current rise`,problem:`Explain, in terms of effective inductance, why current rises much faster once a choke's core saturates under a fault that pushes current above its rating.`,steps:[`Below saturation, the core multiplies the coil's inductance substantially (high permeability)`,`Once saturated, the core can carry no additional flux, so the effective inductance collapses toward the much lower air-core value`,`With applied voltage roughly unchanged but inductance now far lower, di/dt = V/L rises sharply, so current rises far faster than before saturation`],answer:`The core's loss of permeability at saturation removes most of the inductor's current-limiting ability, so current rises steeply — exactly the uncontrolled-current signature of a saturating choke under overload.`}],applications:`The Repair Workshop's open-winding output-inductor scenario and this lesson's winding-resistance reading are the same measurement: a healthy low DC resistance versus an open (infinite) reading at the broken joint.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Choosing a replacement inductor by inductance value alone.`,correction:`Current rating (both I_sat and I_rms) must also suit the actual application; the lower of the two limits is what matters.`},{mistake:`"An inductor with a higher current rating is always a safe drop-in replacement."`,correction:`A higher-current, lower-inductance-tolerance part can still behave differently in the filter it sits in (corner frequency, F3.M5) — match inductance as well as rating, not rating alone.`},{mistake:`Assuming saturation means the inductor is "broken".`,correction:`Saturation is a normal physical limit of any core material reached under excessive current; the component itself may be completely healthy, and the real question is why the current reached that level.`}],demo:{title:`See the inductor entry`,text:`Compare core materials and current ratings in the Component Library.`,link:e.lib(`Inductor`,`inductor`)},activities:[e.lib(`Inductor`,`inductor`),e.repair(`Output inductor winding open`,`rsc-11`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy (J) stored in a 2 mH inductor carrying 5 A?`,answer:.025,tol:.02,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 0.002 × 25 = 0.025 J.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Saturation means...`,options:[`the core can carry no more magnetic flux, so effective inductance collapses`,`the winding has melted`,`the inductor has the wrong polarity`,`the inductor is overrated`],answer:0,explain:`A core physical limit, not necessarily a fault.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Ferrite cores, compared with iron powder, typically offer...`,options:[`higher permeability for a given size, with sharper saturation onset`,`lower permeability always`,`no saturation limit at all`,`only use at DC, never at switching frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Ferrite suits smaller, higher-frequency designs; iron powder saturates more gracefully.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Selecting a replacement inductor should check...`,options:[`both inductance value and current rating`,`inductance value only`,`physical size only`,`colour of the winding only`],answer:0,explain:`Mirrors the resistor power-rating lesson: value and rating are separate checks.`}],summary:[`An inductor stores energy in a magnetic field and resists a change in current.`,`Saturation is a core material limit: beyond it, effective inductance collapses and current rises steeply.`,`Iron powder and ferrite cores trade permeability, saturation behaviour and high-frequency loss differently.`,`Current rating (I_sat and I_rms) must be checked alongside inductance value, exactly as a resistor's power rating must be checked alongside its ohm value.`],homework:{id:`F4.M1.L3.hw`,title:`Inductor energy and rating practice`,brief:`Compute stored energy and reason about saturation.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy (J) stored in a 0.8 mH inductor carrying 10 A?`,answer:.04,tol:.02,unit:`J`,explain:`½ × 0.0008 × 100 = 0.04 J.`}],written:[{prompt:`An output choke rated at 1.2 mH, 6 A begins drawing a much larger, rapidly rising current under a sustained overload. Explain, using the idea of saturation, why simply fitting a core of the same inductance but with a higher saturation current rating is a better fix than fitting a larger-value inductor of the same current rating.`,model:`The problem under overload is that the core has run out of headroom before saturating, not that the inductance value itself is wrong for normal operation. A higher saturation-current-rated core of the same inductance keeps the filter's normal behaviour (corner frequency, ripple) unchanged while giving the extra margin needed to stay out of saturation under the overload condition. Simply raising the inductance value at the same current rating would change the filter's normal behaviour and still saturate at the same current, so it does not address the actual limitation.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M1.L4`,title:`Testing passive components`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`State the correct instrument and setting for testing a resistor, a capacitor and an inductor out of circuit.`,`Interpret a resistance, ESR and inductance reading against a healthy reference value.`,`Explain why passive components are normally tested OUT of circuit, isolated from the rest of the board.`,`Apply the Component Library's test procedures to a resistor, capacitor and inductor.`],introduction:`This lesson ties F4.M1.L1–L3 together into practice: how to actually confirm a resistor, capacitor or inductor is healthy on the bench, using the same test procedures built into the Component Library and relied on throughout the Repair Workshop.`,lecture:`### Why isolate the part first

Measuring a component while it is still connected into a circuit risks reading the PARALLEL combination of that part and everything else connected to the same two nodes, not the part alone — a resistor reading low could be perfectly healthy with another low-resistance path alongside it, or a capacitor's apparent capacitance could include a neighbouring capacitor on the same rail. Lifting at least one lead (or fully removing the part) before testing removes this ambiguity, which is why every test procedure in the Component Library begins with isolation (and, for capacitors, discharge) before any reading is taken.

### Resistors: ohms, compared against the colour code

A digital multimeter's resistance (Ω) range reads a resistor directly once isolated. Compare the reading against the value decoded from its colour code (F4.M1.L1), within its marked tolerance — a reading well outside that tolerance, or an open (no continuity) reading, confirms a fault.

### Capacitors: capacitance AND ESR

A basic meter's capacitance range confirms gross value, but (F4.M1.L2) ESR is the more sensitive aging indicator for an electrolytic. A dedicated ESR meter (or an LCR meter capable of measuring at the right frequency) reads this directly; compared against a healthy reference value for the same part, a significantly raised ESR confirms an aging or failing electrolytic even when its capacitance still reads close to nominal.

### Inductors: DC resistance AND inductance

An inductor's health needs two separate readings (F4.M1.L3): DC resistance (Ω range, or a 4-wire measurement for very low values) confirms the winding is continuous and not developing excess resistance; an LCR meter's inductance reading, compared against the marked value, confirms the winding has not partially shorted turns or that the core has not cracked — either of which can collapse the measured inductance well below its healthy value even while DC resistance looks normal.`,technical:`**4-wire (Kelvin) resistance measurement.** For very low resistances (a fraction of an ohm, typical of inductor windings or current shunts), the voltage drop across the test leads themselves becomes comparable to the reading — a 4-wire measurement uses separate current-carrying and voltage-sensing lead pairs to eliminate this lead-resistance error, giving an accurate low-ohm reading that a standard 2-wire measurement cannot.

**Comparing against a "healthy" reference, not a fixed universal threshold.** Component tolerances and typical aging ranges vary by part and application; the test procedures in this platform quote representative healthy and faulty readings for TEACHING, not a datasheet limit for one specific real part — always confirm against that part's own datasheet for a genuine repair decision.`,diagrams:[{id:`test-bench`,caption:`The out-of-circuit test bench setup: isolate, discharge where relevant, then measure with the correct instrument and setting for each passive component.`}],formulas:[{name:`Acceptable range from tolerance`,expr:`R_min = R_nom(1 − tol),  R_max = R_nom(1 + tol)`,where:[[`R_nom`,`nominal (colour-code) value`,`Ω`],[`tol`,`tolerance as a fraction`,``]],note:`A reading outside this range (or open/short) confirms a fault; within range is consistent with health but does not rule out a borderline drift.`}],examples:[{title:`Judging a resistor reading against tolerance`,problem:`A resistor coded for 1 kΩ ± 5 % reads 1080 Ω out of circuit. Is this within tolerance?`,steps:[`R_min = 1000 × 0.95 = 950 Ω`,`R_max = 1000 × 1.05 = 1050 Ω`,`1080 Ω > 1050 Ω`],answer:`No — 1080 Ω is outside the ±5 % tolerance band, indicating the resistor has drifted (likely from heat or age) and should be treated as suspect.`},{title:`Interpreting an ESR reading`,problem:`A healthy reference reading for a particular bus capacitor is 0.05 Ω ESR. A suspect capacitor of the same part number reads 0.6 Ω. What does this indicate, and what should be checked next?`,steps:[`0.6 Ω is twelve times the healthy reference value`,`This magnitude of ESR rise, with capacitance possibly still near nominal, matches the classic aging signature (F4.M1.L2)`],answer:`The capacitor is almost certainly aged/failing and should be replaced; its capacitance reading alone would likely not have revealed this, which is exactly why ESR is checked separately.`}],applications:`Every circuit-grounded Repair Workshop scenario that involves a passive component reports exactly these readings (resistance, ESR, or inductance) taken live from the real circuit engine — this lesson is the theory behind interpreting them correctly.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Measuring a component while it is still wired into the rest of the circuit.`,correction:`Isolate (lift at least one lead, or remove the part) before measuring, to avoid reading a parallel combination with other parts.`},{mistake:`Checking capacitance but not ESR on a suspect electrolytic.`,correction:`ESR rises well before capacitance drifts noticeably for most aging electrolytics — ESR is the more sensitive test.`},{mistake:`Treating any reading "in the right ballpark" as automatically healthy.`,correction:`Compare precisely against the colour-code/marked value and its tolerance (resistors), or a stated healthy reference (ESR, inductance) — "roughly right" can still hide a real fault.`}],demo:{title:`Run a real test procedure`,text:`Open the resistor test procedure in the Component Library and work through its steps and readings.`,link:e.proc(`Resistor Ω-range test`,`resistor`,`resistor-ohms`)},activities:[e.proc(`Resistor test`,`resistor`,`resistor-ohms`),e.proc(`Capacitor ESR test`,`capacitor`,`cap-esr`),e.proc(`Inductor L and DCR test`,`inductor`,`inductor-lcr`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why isolate a component before testing it?`,options:[`To avoid reading it in parallel with everything else connected to the same nodes`,`Because meters cannot be used in circuit at all`,`To discharge the battery`,`It is not necessary`],answer:0,explain:`A parallel path would give a misleading reading.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The most sensitive early indicator of an aging electrolytic capacitor is...`,options:[`rising ESR`,`falling capacitance`,`colour change`,`weight loss`],answer:0,explain:`ESR rises well before capacitance drifts far.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A 4-wire (Kelvin) resistance measurement is used specifically for...`,options:[`very low resistance values, to eliminate lead-resistance error`,`very high resistance values`,`AC measurements only`,`capacitor testing`],answer:0,explain:`Separate current and sense leads remove the lead-resistance error that matters most at low ohms.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Maximum acceptable resistance (Ω) for a 470 Ω, ±10 % resistor?`,answer:517,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`470 × 1.10 = 517 Ω.`}],summary:[`Passive components are tested out of circuit (isolated, and discharged where relevant) to avoid misleading parallel readings.`,`Resistors are checked against their colour-code value and tolerance.`,`Capacitors need both a capacitance check and an ESR check — ESR is the more sensitive aging indicator.`,`Inductors need both a DC resistance check (winding continuity) and an inductance check (core/turns integrity).`],homework:{id:`F4.M1.L4.hw`,title:`Interpreting passive-component readings`,brief:`Judge readings against tolerance and healthy references.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum acceptable resistance (Ω) for a 2.2 kΩ, ±5 % resistor?`,answer:2090,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`2200 × 0.95 = 2090 Ω.`}],written:[{prompt:`A junior technician measures a suspect bus capacitor's capacitance, finds it within 5 % of its marked value, and concludes the capacitor is healthy without checking ESR. A few weeks later the same capacitor is confirmed as the cause of excessive output ripple. Explain what the technician's check missed and why.`,model:`Capacitance staying close to its marked value does not rule out a failing electrolytic, because the primary real-world aging mechanism (drying electrolyte raising ESR) shows up in ESR well before it noticeably affects the bulk capacitance reading. By checking capacitance alone, the technician missed exactly the measurement most likely to reveal early-stage aging, and the capacitor's rising ESR — not its capacitance — was almost certainly the actual cause of the excessive ripple that surfaced later.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Value (Ω) of a resistor coded orange, orange, red?`,answer:3300,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`33 × 10² = 3300 Ω.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which resistor band gives the tolerance?`,options:[`The last band (commonly gold, silver, red or brown)`,`The first band`,`The multiplier band`,`There is no tolerance band`],answer:0,explain:`Tolerance is the final band.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which capacitor family is polarised?`,options:[`Electrolytic`,`Film`,`Ceramic`,`None of them`],answer:0,explain:`Electrolytic capacitors must not have reversed polarity.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`ESR self-heating (W) for 2.5 A RMS through 0.1 Ω ESR?`,answer:.625,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`2.5² × 0.1 = 6.25 × 0.1 = 0.625 W.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Saturation in an inductor core causes...`,options:[`effective inductance to collapse and current to rise steeply`,`inductance to rise sharply`,`the winding to open automatically`,`no measurable effect`],answer:0,explain:`Loss of permeability removes current-limiting ability.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Passive components are tested out of circuit mainly to avoid...`,options:[`reading a parallel combination with other connected parts`,`damaging the meter`,`the need for a reference value`,`exceeding the meter's voltage rating`],answer:0,explain:`Isolation removes ambiguity from parallel paths.`}]},assignment:{id:`F4.M1.assignment`,title:`Passive component selection and testing`,brief:`Apply resistor, capacitor and inductor selection and testing to short scenarios.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power (W) dissipated by a 22 Ω resistor carrying 1.5 A?`,answer:49.5,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P = I²R = 2.25×22 = 49.5 W.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`ESR self-heating (W) for 4 A RMS through 0.15 Ω ESR?`,answer:2.4,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P = 16×0.15 = 2.4 W.`},{id:`a3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A replacement inductor should match the original in...`,options:[`both inductance value and current rating`,`physical colour only`,`inductance value only, any current rating`,`current rating only, any inductance`],answer:0,explain:`Both ratings matter independently.`}],written:[{prompt:`A 1000 µF, 400 V electrolytic bus capacitor is replaced after confirmed ESR failure with a part of the same capacitance and voltage rating but a lower ripple-current rating than the original. Explain why this is still a risk, even though the two headline specifications (capacitance, voltage) match.`,model:`Ripple-current rating is a separate specification from capacitance and voltage rating, governing how much self-heating (via I²·ESR) the capacitor can tolerate continuously. A replacement with a lower ripple-current rating may overheat under the same operating conditions the original was designed for, even though its capacitance and voltage rating are identical on paper — all of a capacitor's relevant ratings, not just the two most commonly quoted, must be checked when selecting a replacement.`}]},practicals:[{id:`F4.M1.P1`,title:`Passive component rating calculations`,description:`Check resistor power ratings, capacitor ESR heating and inductor saturation margin against real operating figures. All answers must be correct.`,task:{id:`f4-passive-ratings`,kind:`calc`,title:`Rating a resistor, a capacitor and an inductor`,brief:`A 15 Ω bleeder resistor carries 3 A. A bus capacitor with ESR 0.25 Ω carries 2 A RMS ripple. An output choke of 1 mH is rated for a saturation current of 9 A.`,questions:[{id:`c1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power dissipated in the bleeder resistor (W)?`,answer:135,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P = I²R = 9×15 = 135 W.`},{id:`c2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Self-heating in the bus capacitor from its ESR (W)?`,answer:1,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P = I²·ESR = 4×0.25 = 1 W.`},{id:`c3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy stored in the output choke at a load current of 6 A (J)?`,answer:.018,tol:.03,unit:`J`,explain:`½×0.001×36 = 0.018 J.`},{id:`c4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`If the load current rises to 10 A, what happens to the output choke?`,options:[`It exceeds its saturation rating and its effective inductance collapses`,`Nothing, it has unlimited headroom`,`Its resistance drops to zero`,`Its inductance increases to compensate`],answer:0,explain:`10 A exceeds the 9 A saturation rating.`}],explain:`Each rating (power, ESR heating, saturation current) is an independent check against the actual operating current — matching one specification (value) while ignoring another (rating) is the recurring mistake this module warns against.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`f4-passive-ratings`}},{id:`F4.M1.P2`,title:`Diagnose a dried-out smoothing capacitor`,description:`Confirm, in the Repair Workshop, that you can apply the ESR-aging reasoning from this module to a real circuit-grounded fault.`,link:e.repair(`Dried-out smoothing capacitor`,`rsc-04`),evidence:{kind:`troubleshoot`,project:`rsc-04`}}]},{id:`F4.M2`,title:`Semiconductor devices`,summary:`Diodes, MOSFETs, IGBTs and the gate drivers that control them: how each works, how they switch and lose energy, how to test them, and how to recognise and safely replace a failed device.`,lessons:[{id:`F4.M2.L1`,title:`Introduction to semiconductors`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain how doping creates p-type and n-type silicon.`,`Describe a PN junction and why it conducts in one direction and blocks the other.`,`Place diodes, MOSFETs and IGBTs as different practical devices all built from one or more PN junctions.`],introduction:`Every [[diode|diode]], [[mosfet|MOSFET]] and [[igbt|IGBT]] you will meet from here on is built from the same raw idea: silicon whose conductivity has been deliberately engineered by [[doping|doping]]. This short lesson gives you that one idea once, properly, so every later lesson in this module can build on it rather than re-explaining it.`,lecture:`### Pure silicon is nearly an insulator

Pure (intrinsic) silicon has very few free charge carriers at room temperature — it barely conducts at all. Deliberately adding a tiny, controlled amount of another element (doping) changes this dramatically. Adding an element with one more outer electron than silicon (such as phosphorus) creates **n-type** silicon, with an excess of free electrons. Adding an element with one fewer outer electron (such as boron) creates **p-type** silicon, with an excess of "holes" — the absence of an electron, which behaves like a mobile positive charge carrier.

### The PN junction

Where a region of p-type silicon meets a region of n-type silicon, a [[pn-junction|PN junction]] forms. Near the junction, electrons and holes diffuse across and recombine, leaving a thin region depleted of free carriers and a small built-in voltage barrier. Apply an external voltage the right way (p-side positive relative to n-side) and the barrier shrinks, letting current flow easily — **forward bias**. Apply it the other way and the barrier widens, blocking current almost completely — **reverse bias**. This one asymmetry — easy one way, blocked the other — is the entire basis of a diode (F4.M2.L2), and a building block inside every MOSFET and IGBT.

### One idea, many devices

A diode is essentially a single PN junction with two external leads. A MOSFET and an IGBT are far more sophisticated structures (F4.M2.L4, L5) with multiple regions and a controlling gate, but they are still built from doped silicon and PN-junction behaviour at their core — understanding doping and the PN junction here means every later lesson in this module is explaining a variation on one theme, not an unrelated new mechanism each time.`,technical:`**Majority and minority carriers.** In n-type silicon, electrons are the majority carrier and holes the minority carrier (and the reverse in p-type) — this distinction matters for understanding switching speed differences between device families (F4.M2.L5's discussion of IGBT tail current, caused by minority-carrier recombination, follows directly from this).

**Why silicon, not some other element.** Silicon's natural oxide (silicon dioxide) is an excellent, stable insulator that can be grown directly on the silicon surface — this is what makes a MOSFET's insulated gate (F4.M2.L4) practical to manufacture reliably at low cost and in enormous volume.`,diagrams:[{id:`design-flow`,caption:`From doped silicon to a PN junction to the practical device families (diode, MOSFET, IGBT) built from that one underlying junction behaviour.`}],formulas:[{name:`Forward bias condition`,expr:`V_p-side > V_n-side (by more than the built-in barrier voltage)`,where:[[`V_p-side`,`voltage applied to the p-type region`,`V`],[`V_n-side`,`voltage applied to the n-type region`,`V`]],note:`Silicon's built-in barrier is roughly 0.6–0.7 V — this is where the familiar diode forward voltage (F4.M2.L2) comes from.`}],examples:[{title:`Identifying bias direction`,problem:`A PN junction has +5 V applied to its p-type side and 0 V to its n-type side. Is this forward or reverse bias?`,steps:[`p-side (5 V) is higher than n-side (0 V)`,`This matches the forward-bias condition`],answer:`Forward bias — the junction conducts (once the barrier voltage is exceeded).`},{title:`Why reverse bias blocks current`,problem:`Explain, in terms of the depletion region, why reverse bias nearly stops current flow across a PN junction.`,steps:[`Reverse bias pulls majority carriers away from the junction on both sides`,`This widens the depletion region (the carrier-free zone) and increases the barrier`,`With almost no free carriers left near the junction to carry current, conduction is blocked (aside from a tiny leakage current)`],answer:`The widened, carrier-depleted region presents a large barrier to current flow, which is why reverse bias blocks conduction almost completely.`}],applications:`Every later lesson in this module — diode rectification, MOSFET and IGBT switching, gate drivers, and the fault patterns you will diagnose in the Repair Workshop — is a direct consequence of this one PN-junction behaviour.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Thinking doping "contaminates" silicon and makes it worse.`,correction:`Doping is a deliberate, precisely controlled engineering step that creates the useful conductivity properties semiconductor devices depend on — it is the entire point, not a defect.`},{mistake:`Assuming a PN junction blocks current completely in reverse.`,correction:`A small reverse leakage current always flows; it only becomes a visible fault symptom when it rises far above its healthy, tiny value (F4.M2.L9).`},{mistake:`Treating MOSFETs and IGBTs as unrelated to diodes.`,correction:`All three are built from PN-junction behaviour; MOSFETs and IGBTs add a controlling gate and more complex structure on top of the same underlying physics.`}],demo:{title:`See the diode entry first`,text:`The diode is the simplest practical device built from a single PN junction — open it before the more complex MOSFET and IGBT entries.`,link:e.lib(`Diode`,`diode`)},activities:[e.lib(`Diode`,`diode`),e.lib(`MOSFET`,`mosfet`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Doping silicon with an element that has one extra outer electron creates...`,options:[`n-type silicon`,`p-type silicon`,`pure silicon`,`an insulator`],answer:0,explain:`Excess free electrons define n-type.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A PN junction conducts easily when...`,options:[`forward biased: p-side positive relative to n-side, beyond the barrier voltage`,`reverse biased`,`no voltage at all is applied`,`cooled below freezing`],answer:0,explain:`Forward bias shrinks the depletion barrier.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`MOSFETs and IGBTs are related to diodes because...`,options:[`all are built from PN-junction behaviour in doped silicon`,`they use completely unrelated physics`,`only diodes use silicon`,`MOSFETs contain no silicon`],answer:0,explain:`All three share the same underlying junction physics.`}],summary:[`Doping deliberately creates n-type (excess electrons) or p-type (excess holes) silicon from otherwise poorly-conducting pure silicon.`,`A PN junction forms where p-type meets n-type, with a built-in barrier voltage (~0.6–0.7 V for silicon).`,`Forward bias shrinks the barrier and allows easy conduction; reverse bias widens it and blocks conduction (aside from tiny leakage).`,`Diodes, MOSFETs and IGBTs are all built from this one underlying PN-junction behaviour, at increasing structural complexity.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L1.hw`,title:`PN junction basics`,brief:`Identify bias direction and its consequence.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A PN junction has 0 V on its p-side and +5 V on its n-side. This is...`,options:[`reverse bias`,`forward bias`,`neutral`,`undefined`],answer:0,explain:`n-side higher than p-side is reverse bias.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain, without using the words "diode" or "MOSFET", what a PN junction physically is and why it conducts in only one direction.`,model:`A PN junction is the boundary between two regions of silicon, one deliberately doped to have an excess of free electrons and the other to have an excess of "holes" (missing electrons that behave like mobile positive charges). Near this boundary, a thin region forms where these carriers have diffused across and recombined, leaving few free carriers and a small built-in voltage barrier. Applying voltage one way shrinks this barrier and lets current flow easily; applying it the other way widens the barrier, driving away the remaining carriers and almost completely blocking current — this asymmetry is what gives the junction its one-directional conducting behaviour.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M2.L2`,title:`Diodes: forward and reverse behaviour, ratings`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`State the typical forward voltage of a silicon diode and explain why it stays roughly constant across a range of currents.`,`Define reverse breakdown voltage and explain what happens if it is exceeded.`,`Identify the key ratings (V_f, I_f, V_rrm) needed to select or check a diode.`,`Explain the role of a diode's [[body-diode|anti-parallel]] use alongside a switching device.`],introduction:`The diode is the simplest practical device built from the PN junction of F4.M2.L1 — two leads, [[forward-voltage|conducts forward, blocks reverse]]. This lesson covers how to read a diode's key ratings and recognise its role both as a standalone rectifying part and as the "flyback" partner built into every MOSFET and IGBT.`,lecture:`### Forward conduction

Once forward voltage exceeds the junction's barrier (around 0.6–0.7 V for ordinary silicon diodes, lower for Schottky types, around 1.8–3+ V for LEDs depending on colour), the diode conducts, and its voltage drop stays roughly constant across a wide range of currents — very different from a resistor, whose drop rises proportionally with current. This near-constant [[forward-voltage|forward voltage (V_f)]] is why a diode's own power dissipation is approximately P ≈ V_f × I, not I²R.

### Reverse blocking and breakdown

In reverse bias, only a tiny leakage current flows — until the reverse voltage exceeds the diode's rated **reverse breakdown voltage (V_rrm, or peak reverse voltage)**, at which point the junction breaks down and conducts heavily in reverse, almost always destructively for an ordinary rectifier diode (a few specialised diode types, such as zener and avalanche diodes, are DESIGNED to break down controllably — not covered further here).

### Key ratings to check

- **V_f (forward voltage)**: the drop when conducting, at a stated current — sets conduction loss.
- **I_f (forward current, average and/or RMS)**: maximum continuous current the diode can carry.
- **V_rrm (peak repetitive reverse voltage)**: the reverse voltage it must never exceed in normal operation.
- **Reverse recovery time (t_rr)**: how quickly the diode stops conducting once reverse-biased after conducting forward — matters for fast-switching rectification (F4.M2.L3) where a slow diode wastes energy and generates extra heat during the transition.

### The body diode: a diode inside every power switch

Every power MOSFET (and most IGBT modules) includes an [[body-diode|anti-parallel "body" diode]] from source to drain (or emitter to collector), either an intrinsic structural feature (MOSFET) or added deliberately (many IGBT modules). This diode carries [[freewheeling|freewheeling]] current whenever an inductive load's current needs somewhere to go while the main switch is off — a genuinely essential part of every bridge leg (F3.M5, P2), not a side detail.`,technical:`**Schottky diodes.** A metal-semiconductor junction instead of a PN junction, giving a lower forward voltage (around 0.2–0.4 V) and very fast (near-instant) reverse recovery, at the cost of a lower reverse voltage rating and a somewhat higher reverse leakage current than an ordinary silicon diode — commonly used where switching speed or forward-voltage loss matters more than voltage headroom.

**Temperature effect on V_f.** A silicon diode's forward voltage falls slightly as it heats (a small negative temperature coefficient), the opposite of a resistor's usual behaviour — relevant when diodes are deliberately paralleled, since without care the hottest diode can end up carrying a disproportionate share of the current.`,diagrams:[{id:`diode-led`,caption:`A diode's forward-conduction and reverse-blocking regions, showing the roughly-constant forward voltage and the sharp reverse breakdown point.`}],formulas:[{name:`Diode conduction loss (approx.)`,expr:`P_d ≈ V_f · I_avg`,where:[[`P_d`,`approximate conduction power loss`,`W`],[`V_f`,`forward voltage at that current`,`V`],[`I_avg`,`average forward current`,`A`]]}],examples:[{title:`Diode conduction loss`,problem:`A rectifier diode has V_f = 0.7 V and carries an average 8 A. Approximate conduction loss?`,steps:[`P ≈ V_f × I_avg`,`= 0.7 × 8`],answer:`≈ 5.6 W.`},{title:`Checking reverse voltage headroom`,problem:`A diode rated V_rrm = 100 V is used in a circuit where the peak reverse voltage it will see is 95 V. Comment on this choice.`,steps:[`95 V against a 100 V rating leaves only 5 % margin`,`Normal supply variation, switching transients and component tolerance commonly need more headroom than this`],answer:`This is a poor, high-risk choice with almost no margin — a higher-rated diode (commonly chosen with at least 1.5–2× margin over the expected peak reverse voltage) would be the safer, standard engineering practice.`}],applications:`The bridge rectifier you will build in the Circuit Connection Laboratory, and every MOSFET/IGBT body diode in every bridge leg you study from here on, both rest directly on this lesson's forward/reverse behaviour.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating a diode's forward drop like a resistor's (proportional to current).`,correction:`Forward voltage stays roughly constant across a wide current range; it is not well modelled as a fixed resistance.`},{mistake:`Checking only V_f when selecting a diode, and ignoring V_rrm.`,correction:`Reverse voltage rating is just as critical a check — exceeding it destroys an ordinary rectifier diode.`},{mistake:`Forgetting the body diode exists when analysing a bridge leg with an inductive load.`,correction:`The body diode carries freewheeling current whenever the main switch turns off with inductive current still flowing — ignoring it gives an incomplete picture of the leg's behaviour.`}],demo:{title:`See diode ratings in the library`,text:`Compare forward voltage and reverse voltage ratings for the diode entry.`,link:e.lib(`Diode`,`diode`)},activities:[e.lib(`Diode`,`diode`),e.ckt(`LED circuit`,`ckt-02`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Approximate conduction loss (W) for V_f = 0.6 V at 10 A average?`,answer:6,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P ≈ 0.6×10 = 6 W.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Exceeding a diode's V_rrm rating causes...`,options:[`reverse breakdown, almost always destructive for an ordinary rectifier diode`,`a slightly higher forward voltage`,`no effect at all`,`an increase in reverse recovery time only`],answer:0,explain:`V_rrm is a hard reverse-voltage limit.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A MOSFET's body diode is used mainly to...`,options:[`carry freewheeling current from an inductive load while the switch is off`,`block all reverse current permanently`,`increase switching speed`,`replace the gate driver`],answer:0,explain:`Essential for inductive loads in a bridge leg.`}],summary:[`A diode conducts easily above its forward voltage (roughly constant across current) and blocks almost completely in reverse, up to its V_rrm rating.`,`Key ratings to check: V_f, I_f, V_rrm and reverse recovery time.`,`Every power MOSFET (and most IGBT modules) includes a body/anti-parallel diode that carries freewheeling current for inductive loads.`,`Schottky diodes trade a lower voltage rating for lower forward voltage and much faster reverse recovery.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L2.hw`,title:`Diode ratings practice`,brief:`Apply conduction loss and reverse-voltage margin checks.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Approximate conduction loss (W) for V_f = 0.65 V at 12 A average?`,answer:7.8,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`0.65×12 = 7.8 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`A design reuses a diode rated V_rrm = 50 V in a new circuit where the peak reverse voltage is only 20 V, but the current is now much higher than the diode's I_f rating. Explain which rating is actually at risk here, and why "the voltage rating is fine" is not sufficient reassurance.`,model:`The voltage margin (50 V rating against a 20 V peak) is indeed generous, but that says nothing about the current rating, which is a completely separate specification. If the new circuit's current exceeds the diode's I_f rating, the diode will overheat and fail from excessive conduction loss and thermal stress, regardless of how much reverse-voltage headroom it has. Every rating (V_f/conduction current, V_rrm, reverse recovery time) must be checked independently against the new application — comfortable margin on one rating never substitutes for a check on another.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M2.L3`,title:`Rectification with diodes`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Describe half-wave and full-wave (bridge) rectification and compare their output ripple frequency.`,`Explain how many diodes conduct at any instant in a bridge rectifier.`,`Relate ripple frequency and smoothing capacitor size using the ripple voltage formula.`],introduction:`[[rectifier|Rectification]] — turning AC into DC using diodes — is the reverse operation of the inversion this whole platform is built around, and you will meet it wherever an inverter's control electronics or a battery charger needs DC from an AC input. This lesson covers how a diode bridge does it and what determines the resulting ripple.`,lecture:`### Half-wave rectification

A single diode in series with an AC source and a load passes only the half-cycles where it is forward biased, blocking the other half entirely. The result is a train of one-sided pulses at the SAME frequency as the input, with the load current (and hence voltage, into a resistive load) at zero for half of every cycle — simple, but wasteful and rough.

### Full-wave (bridge) rectification

Four diodes arranged as a bridge conduct in diagonal PAIRS: one pair for the positive input half-cycle, the other pair for the negative half-cycle, and in both cases current is steered through the load in the SAME direction. At any instant, exactly two diodes conduct (one pair), while the other two block. The result is a train of pulses at TWICE the input frequency (both half-cycles now contribute a pulse), which is both a higher ripple frequency (easier to smooth, F3.M5) and a more complete use of the input waveform than half-wave rectification.

### Smoothing the rectified output

A capacitor across the rectified output (F3.M5, F4.M1.L2) charges toward the peak each pulse and then supplies the load current between pulses, with its voltage sagging by an amount set by the load current, the capacitor value and the time between pulses — the higher ripple FREQUENCY from full-wave rectification directly means LESS sag (less ripple voltage) for the same capacitor value, compared with half-wave. This is exactly the same smoothing-capacitor reasoning met for the output filter of F4.M1.L2, now applied to the raw pulsed output a rectifier bridge produces before any further filtering.`,technical:`**Diode drop in a bridge.** Current flows through TWO diodes in series at any instant in a bridge rectifier (one in series with the source going in, one coming back), so the total forward-voltage loss is roughly double a single diode's V_f — a detail that matters more at low output voltages, where two diode drops can be a significant fraction of the total.

**Three-phase rectification** (mentioned for completeness, beyond this course's main scope) uses six diodes and produces a much smoother raw output with far smaller ripple even before any smoothing capacitor, because the six pulses per cycle overlap far more than a single-phase bridge's two.`,diagrams:[{id:`circuit-loop`,caption:`Current paths through a four-diode bridge rectifier for each half-cycle of the AC input, showing which diagonal pair conducts.`}],formulas:[{name:`Ripple voltage (approx.)`,expr:`∆V ≈ I / (f_ripple · C)`,where:[[`∆V`,`peak-to-peak ripple voltage`,`V`],[`I`,`load current`,`A`],[`f_ripple`,`ripple frequency (2× mains for full-wave)`,`Hz`],[`C`,`smoothing capacitance`,`F`]]}],examples:[{title:`Ripple frequency from full-wave rectification`,problem:`A full-wave bridge rectifies a 50 Hz supply. What is the ripple frequency?`,steps:[`Full-wave gives two pulses per input cycle`,`f_ripple = 2 × 50`],answer:`100 Hz.`},{title:`Comparing ripple for half-wave vs full-wave`,problem:`A 2 A load is smoothed by a 2000 µF capacitor after (a) half-wave rectification (50 Hz ripple) and (b) full-wave rectification (100 Hz ripple). Compare the ripple voltage.`,steps:[`(a) ∆V ≈ 2/(50 × 0.002) = 2/0.1 = 20 V`,`(b) ∆V ≈ 2/(100 × 0.002) = 2/0.2 = 10 V`],answer:`Full-wave rectification halves the ripple voltage (20 V → 10 V) for the identical capacitor and load current, purely from doubling the ripple frequency.`,check:`Ripple is inversely proportional to frequency in the formula, so doubling frequency should exactly halve ripple — consistent with the result.`}],applications:`The Circuit Connection Laboratory's bridge rectifier exercise and DC filtering exercise are this lesson in practice — build the bridge, then watch the smoothing capacitor turn pulsed DC into a usable, low-ripple supply.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming only one diode conducts at a time in a bridge rectifier.`,correction:`Two diodes (one diagonal pair) conduct simultaneously at any instant; the other two block.`},{mistake:`Believing ripple frequency equals the input AC frequency for full-wave rectification.`,correction:`Full-wave rectification doubles the ripple frequency relative to the input, because both half-cycles now contribute a pulse.`},{mistake:`Forgetting the double diode drop in a bridge when working at low output voltages.`,correction:`Two series diode drops (not one) are lost in a bridge at any instant — significant when the target output voltage itself is low.`}],demo:{title:`Build the bridge rectifier`,text:`Build and test the four-diode bridge rectifier in the Circuit Connection Laboratory.`,link:e.ckt(`Bridge rectifier`,`ckt-04`)},activities:[e.ckt(`Bridge rectifier`,`ckt-04`),e.ckt(`DC filtering`,`ckt-05`),e.sim(`Bridge rectification experiment`,`sim-03`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple frequency (Hz) from full-wave rectification of a 60 Hz supply?`,answer:120,tol:.01,unit:`Hz`,explain:`2×60=120 Hz.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In a bridge rectifier, how many diodes conduct at any one instant?`,options:[`two`,`one`,`four`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`One diagonal pair conducts; the other pair blocks.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Compared with half-wave rectification at the same frequency and smoothing capacitor, full-wave rectification gives...`,options:[`lower ripple voltage, from its higher ripple frequency`,`higher ripple voltage`,`identical ripple`,`no DC output at all`],answer:0,explain:`Ripple is inversely proportional to ripple frequency.`}],summary:[`Half-wave rectification passes only alternate half-cycles; full-wave (bridge) rectification uses both.`,`A bridge rectifier conducts through one diagonal diode pair at a time, at twice the input frequency of ripple pulses.`,`Higher ripple frequency directly means lower ripple voltage for the same smoothing capacitor and load current.`,`Two diode drops (not one) are lost in series at any instant in a bridge rectifier.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L3.hw`,title:`Rectification and ripple practice`,brief:`Apply ripple frequency and ripple voltage calculations.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple frequency (Hz) from full-wave rectification of a 50 Hz supply?`,answer:100,tol:.01,unit:`Hz`,explain:`2×50=100 Hz.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple voltage (V) for I=3 A, f=100 Hz, C=3000 µF?`,answer:10,tol:.03,unit:`V`,explain:`∆V=3/(100×0.003)=3/0.3=10 V.`}],written:[{prompt:`A battery charger's output ripple is found to be twice as high as the design calculation predicted. A technician discovers one diode in the bridge has failed open-circuit. Explain, using this lesson's ideas, why this single open diode would roughly double the ripple.`,model:`With one diode open, the bridge can no longer rectify both half-cycles: effectively, the circuit has degraded from full-wave to half-wave rectification, since current can only complete a path through the bridge during the half-cycles that do not require the failed diode. Half-wave rectification has half the ripple frequency of full-wave for the same input, and ripple voltage is inversely proportional to ripple frequency, so halving the effective ripple frequency roughly doubles the ripple voltage for the same load current and capacitor — exactly matching the observed symptom.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M2.L4`,title:`MOSFETs: structure, V_th, R_DS(on), body diode`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a MOSFET's gate voltage controls conduction between drain and source.`,`Define threshold voltage (V_th) and R_DS(on), and explain how each affects switching behaviour and loss.`,`Explain why R_DS(on) rises with temperature and what this means for paralleled MOSFETs.`,`Identify the body diode's terminals and typical role in a bridge leg.`],introduction:`The [[mosfet|MOSFET]] is the dominant switching device at the voltage and frequency ranges most battery inverters operate in. This lesson covers how its gate voltage controls conduction, the two ratings ([[threshold-voltage|V_th]] and [[rdson|R_DS(on)]]) that matter most for selecting and testing one, and its built-in [[body-diode|body diode]].`,lecture:`### Voltage-controlled switching

A MOSFET's gate is insulated from the rest of the device by a thin oxide layer (hence "metal-oxide-semiconductor") — essentially no DC current flows into the gate once it is charged, only a brief charging/discharging current during switching transitions. Raising the gate-source voltage above the [[threshold-voltage|threshold voltage (V_th)]] creates a conducting channel between drain and source; below V_th, the channel does not form and the device blocks (aside from tiny leakage). This voltage-controlled, near-zero-steady-current gate behaviour is why MOSFETs are relatively easy and efficient to drive compared with older current-controlled device families.

### R_DS(on): the on-state resistance

Once fully on (gate driven well above V_th, commonly 10–15 V for a standard device), a MOSFET behaves approximately like a small resistance, [[rdson|R_DS(on)]], between drain and source. Conduction loss is then simply P = I²·R_DS(on) — exactly the same I²R relationship as a resistor (F4.M1.L1), which is why R_DS(on) is the single most important rating for conduction loss at a given current.

### R_DS(on) rises with temperature

Unlike many materials, a MOSFET's R_DS(on) INCREASES as it heats — commonly by a factor of roughly 1.5–2× from room temperature to a typical maximum junction temperature. This has a genuinely important consequence when MOSFETs are deliberately paralleled to share current: if one device happens to run slightly hotter, its R_DS(on) rises, which in a naive picture might seem to push current AWAY from it toward the cooler devices — a form of natural (if imperfect) self-balancing that helps (but does not guarantee) even current sharing among paralleled devices.

### The body diode, again

As introduced in F4.M2.L2, every power MOSFET has an intrinsic body diode from source to drain, conducting whenever the drain is pulled negative relative to the source by more than a diode drop — carrying freewheeling current for an inductive load whenever the main channel is off. It is generally slower (higher reverse recovery) than a dedicated fast rectifier diode, which matters in some high-frequency designs but not in the switching frequencies most battery inverters use.`,technical:`**Gate charge (Q_g).** The total charge the gate driver must deliver to fully turn the MOSFET on (and remove to turn it off), in nanocoulombs — together with switching frequency, this sets the gate-drive power the driver itself must supply, and is one factor in choosing a gate resistor value (F4.M1.L1, F4.M2.L6).

**Avalanche rating.** Many modern power MOSFETs are specifically rated to survive a limited amount of reverse breakdown ("avalanche") energy without damage, giving some tolerance of voltage transients beyond V_DS(max) — a deliberate design margin, not an invitation to operate beyond rated voltage routinely.`,diagrams:[{id:`hbridge-states`,caption:`A MOSFET's gate-controlled channel between drain and source, and how its state (on/off) sets the current path through a bridge leg.`}],formulas:[{name:`MOSFET conduction loss`,expr:`P_cond = I_rms² · R_DS(on)`,where:[[`P_cond`,`conduction power loss`,`W`],[`I_rms`,`RMS current through the device`,`A`],[`R_DS(on)`,`on-state drain-source resistance at operating temperature`,`Ω`]]}],examples:[{title:`MOSFET conduction loss`,problem:`A MOSFET with R_DS(on) = 15 mΩ carries 20 A RMS. Conduction loss?`,steps:[`P = I² × R_DS(on)`,`= 400 × 0.015`],answer:`= 6 W.`},{title:`Effect of R_DS(on) rising with temperature`,problem:`The same MOSFET's R_DS(on) rises to 25 mΩ at its hot operating temperature. Recompute the conduction loss at the same 20 A.`,steps:[`P = I² × R_DS(on), using the new (hotter) R_DS(on)`,`= 400 × 0.025`],answer:`= 10 W — a 67 % increase in loss purely from the temperature-driven R_DS(on) rise, which further raises temperature (a feedback loop thermal design (F4.M5.L3) must account for).`,check:`0.025/0.015 ≈ 1.67, matching the 10/6 ≈ 1.67 ratio in the loss — consistent.`}],applications:`Every MOSFET switching exercise in the Circuit Connection Laboratory and simulator, and the Repair Workshop's MOSFET-failed-open and MOSFET-failed-short scenarios, rest directly on this lesson's structure and ratings.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a MOSFET needs significant continuous gate current to stay on.`,correction:`The gate is insulated; only a brief charge/discharge current flows during switching transitions, not a sustained on-state current.`},{mistake:`Treating R_DS(on) as a fixed value regardless of temperature.`,correction:`R_DS(on) rises meaningfully with temperature — thermal design must use the HOT value, not the cool datasheet headline figure, for a worst-case loss estimate.`},{mistake:`Forgetting the body diode when analysing why a MOSFET conducts even with its gate held low.`,correction:`The body diode can still conduct (in one direction only) regardless of gate state, if the external voltage across drain-source forward-biases it — this is a real, expected path, not a fault by itself.`}],demo:{title:`See the MOSFET entry`,text:`Compare V_th and R_DS(on) ratings in the Component Library.`,link:e.lib(`MOSFET`,`mosfet`)},activities:[e.lib(`MOSFET`,`mosfet`),e.ckt(`MOSFET switching`,`ckt-06`),e.sim(`MOSFET switching experiment`,`sim-05`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Conduction loss (W) for R_DS(on)=20 mΩ at 15 A RMS?`,answer:4.5,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`15²×0.02=225×0.02=4.5 W.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Raising gate-source voltage above V_th...`,options:[`forms a conducting channel between drain and source`,`has no effect on conduction`,`always destroys the MOSFET`,`only affects the body diode`],answer:0,explain:`This is the basic switching action.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`As a MOSFET heats, its R_DS(on) typically...`,options:[`rises`,`falls`,`stays exactly constant`,`becomes negative`],answer:0,explain:`A positive temperature coefficient of R_DS(on) is typical.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The body diode conducts when...`,options:[`the drain is pulled negative relative to the source by more than a diode drop`,`the gate is driven high`,`the device is cooled`,`never, under any condition`],answer:0,explain:`It is a diode from source to drain, independent of gate state.`}],summary:[`A MOSFET's gate voltage (above or below V_th) controls whether a conducting channel forms between drain and source.`,`R_DS(on) sets conduction loss (P = I²·R_DS(on)) once fully on, and rises meaningfully with temperature.`,`The rising-R_DS(on)-with-temperature behaviour gives some natural (imperfect) current self-balancing when MOSFETs are paralleled.`,`The intrinsic body diode conducts in one direction regardless of gate state, carrying freewheeling current for inductive loads.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L4.hw`,title:`MOSFET conduction loss practice`,brief:`Apply the conduction-loss formula at different temperatures.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Conduction loss (W) for R_DS(on)=12 mΩ at 25 A RMS?`,answer:7.5,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`625×0.012=7.5 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`Two MOSFETs of the exact same part number are paralleled to share 40 A total. One happens to sit slightly closer to a cooling fan and runs a few degrees cooler than the other. Explain, using R_DS(on)'s temperature behaviour, which device is likely to carry more current, and why this is a self-limiting (not runaway) effect.`,model:`The cooler device has a slightly lower R_DS(on) (since R_DS(on) rises with temperature) and so initially carries a slightly larger share of the total current for the same voltage drop across both devices. However, carrying more current makes that device dissipate more power and heat up, which in turn raises ITS R_DS(on), pushing some current back toward the other device. This negative-feedback relationship between temperature and R_DS(on) is why parallelled MOSFETs tend toward a reasonably balanced (though rarely perfectly equal) current split, rather than one device progressively taking over all the current in an unstable runaway.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M2.L5`,title:`IGBTs: V_CE(sat), tail current`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain why IGBTs are preferred over MOSFETs at higher voltages.`,`Define V_CE(sat) and compare its loss behaviour with a MOSFET's R_DS(on).`,`Explain tail current and why it makes IGBTs slower to turn off than MOSFETs.`],introduction:`The [[igbt|IGBT]] combines a MOSFET-like insulated gate with a bipolar-transistor-like conduction path, giving it an advantage at higher voltages that a comparable MOSFET cannot easily match — at the cost of a switching-speed penalty this lesson explains.`,lecture:`### Why IGBTs dominate at higher voltage

A MOSFET's R_DS(on) rises roughly with the square of its voltage rating for a given die size — doubling the voltage rating can mean roughly four times the on-resistance (and conduction loss) for the same physical device size. An IGBT's conduction mechanism does not suffer this same voltage-squared penalty, so at higher voltages (roughly above a few hundred volts, depending on the specific technology generation) an IGBT typically gives lower conduction loss for a given die size and cost than a MOSFET rated for the same voltage — which is why IGBTs dominate the higher-power, higher-voltage end of inverter and motor-drive design, including the MPPT charger stage this platform's component tests already reference.

### V_CE(sat): the IGBT's conduction-loss rating

Rather than behaving like a resistance, a fully-on IGBT has a nearly CONSTANT collector-emitter voltage drop, [[vcesat|V_CE(sat)]] (typically 1.2–2.5 V), largely independent of current over a wide range — so IGBT conduction loss is approximately P ≈ V_CE(sat) × I, much like a diode's conduction loss (F4.M2.L2), rather than the I² relationship a MOSFET's R_DS(on) gives.

### Tail current: the switching-speed trade-off

When an IGBT turns off, it does not stop conducting instantly: a "tail current" persists briefly afterward, caused by minority carriers (F4.M2.L1) that were injected during conduction and must recombine before the device fully blocks. This tail current, flowing while voltage across the device is already rising, causes a genuine turn-off energy loss that MOSFETs (which turn off essentially instantly by comparison) do not suffer — the direct reason IGBTs are generally used at LOWER switching frequencies than MOSFETs for a comparable total loss budget.`,technical:`**IGBT generations.** Successive IGBT generations (planar, trench, field-stop and beyond) have progressively reduced tail-current losses and V_CE(sat), narrowing (without eliminating) the switching-speed gap with MOSFETs — datasheets for modern IGBTs quote tail-current-related turn-off energy (E_off) directly for this reason.

**Co-packaged diode.** Nearly every IGBT module includes a fast anti-parallel diode (distinct from a MOSFET's intrinsic body diode, since an IGBT has no equivalent built-in structure) specifically selected for fast reverse recovery to limit the extra loss this combination can otherwise cause during switching transitions.`,diagrams:[{id:`quasi-square`,caption:`An IGBT's collector current during turn-off, showing the sharp initial fall followed by the slower-decaying tail current.`}],formulas:[{name:`IGBT conduction loss (approx.)`,expr:`P_cond ≈ V_CE(sat) · I_avg`,where:[[`V_CE(sat)`,`saturation collector-emitter voltage`,`V`],[`I_avg`,`average collector current`,`A`]]}],examples:[{title:`IGBT conduction loss`,problem:`An IGBT has V_CE(sat) = 1.8 V and carries an average 15 A. Conduction loss?`,steps:[`P ≈ V_CE(sat) × I_avg`,`= 1.8 × 15`],answer:`= 27 W.`},{title:`Choosing between MOSFET and IGBT`,problem:`A 400 V, 10 kHz switching stage is being designed. Which device family is the more natural starting choice, and why?`,steps:[`400 V is in the range where IGBT conduction loss typically beats a MOSFET of equivalent die size/cost`,`10 kHz is a moderate switching frequency where tail-current turn-off loss, while present, is usually still manageable for an IGBT`],answer:`IGBT — the voltage level favours its conduction-loss advantage, and the switching frequency is not so high that tail-current loss would dominate the design, unlike it might at 100+ kHz.`}],applications:`The MPPT charger switch you tested in the Component Library's IGBT procedure, and any higher-voltage bridge stage in a hybrid or high-frequency inverter, are exactly where this lesson's trade-off plays out in practice.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming IGBTs are simply "better MOSFETs" in every respect.`,correction:`IGBTs trade switching speed (tail current) for lower conduction loss at higher voltage — neither device dominates the other universally; the right choice depends on voltage and frequency.`},{mistake:`Modelling IGBT conduction loss with an I² relationship.`,correction:`Use P ≈ V_CE(sat) × I, not I²R — an IGBT behaves more like a diode's roughly-constant voltage drop than a resistor.`},{mistake:`Ignoring tail current when estimating total IGBT switching loss.`,correction:`Tail current is a genuine, datasheet-quantified (E_off) loss mechanism at turn-off, not a negligible detail.`}],demo:{title:`See the IGBT entry`,text:`Compare V_CE(sat) against a MOSFET's R_DS(on) for the same current.`,link:e.lib(`IGBT`,`igbt`)},activities:[e.lib(`IGBT`,`igbt`),e.proc(`IGBT check procedure`,`igbt`,`igbt-check`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`IGBT conduction loss (W) for V_CE(sat)=1.5 V at 20 A average?`,answer:30,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`1.5×20=30 W.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`IGBTs are generally preferred over MOSFETs at...`,options:[`higher voltages, where their conduction-loss advantage is greatest`,`lower voltages only`,`all voltages equally`,`never, MOSFETs are always better`],answer:0,explain:`MOSFET R_DS(on) scales poorly with voltage rating.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Tail current occurs...`,options:[`briefly after turn-off, from minority-carrier recombination`,`only during turn-on`,`continuously while fully on`,`only in MOSFETs`],answer:0,explain:`A turn-off phenomenon specific to bipolar-type conduction.`}],summary:[`IGBTs combine a MOSFET-like gate with bipolar-style conduction, giving lower conduction loss than a comparable MOSFET at higher voltages.`,`V_CE(sat), not R_DS(on), sets IGBT conduction loss: P ≈ V_CE(sat) × I, roughly independent of current level.`,`Tail current after turn-off, from minority-carrier recombination, is a genuine IGBT switching-loss mechanism that MOSFETs do not share.`,`Neither device family dominates universally; the right choice depends on the voltage and switching frequency of the design.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L5.hw`,title:`IGBT conduction loss practice`,brief:`Apply the V_CE(sat) loss formula and compare device choices.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`IGBT conduction loss (W) for V_CE(sat)=2 V at 18 A average?`,answer:36,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`2×18=36 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`A designer wants to raise a 10 kHz IGBT-based bridge's switching frequency to 80 kHz to shrink the output filter's size, without changing the device. Explain, using tail current, why this is likely to cause a disproportionate rise in total losses.`,model:`Tail-current turn-off loss occurs once per switching cycle, so the total switching-loss POWER it contributes scales roughly in proportion to switching frequency. Raising the frequency eightfold (10 kHz to 80 kHz) would raise this component of loss roughly eightfold too, on top of whatever conduction loss remains — likely pushing total losses well beyond what the device and its cooling were designed for. This is exactly why IGBTs are generally kept at lower switching frequencies than MOSFETs for a comparable loss budget; a frequency increase this large would normally call for a different device family (or a different circuit topology) rather than simply reusing the same IGBTs faster.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M2.L6`,title:`Gate drivers: bootstrap, isolation, dead time`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain why a gate driver, not the control signal directly, is needed to switch a power MOSFET or IGBT.`,`Explain how a bootstrap supply powers a floating high-side driver.`,`State what dead time is and why every bridge leg's driver must enforce it.`],introduction:`A microcontroller or PWM controller (F4.M5.L1) produces small, logic-level signals — nowhere near enough voltage or current to switch a power MOSFET or IGBT quickly. The [[gate-driver|gate driver]] sits between the two, and this lesson covers its three defining jobs: enough drive, a floating high-side supply, and enforced [[dead-time|dead time]].`,lecture:`### Why a dedicated driver is needed

Switching a MOSFET or IGBT quickly means charging and discharging its gate capacitance fast, which needs a genuine current pulse (often amps, briefly) at the right voltage (commonly 10–15 V) — far beyond what a microcontroller pin or PWM controller output can supply directly. A gate driver is essentially a fast, high-current amplifier dedicated to this one job, placed as close to the gate as practical to minimise the inductance in the drive path.

### The floating high-side problem, and the bootstrap solution

In a [[half-bridge|half-bridge]] leg, the LOW-side switch's source sits at a fixed reference (commonly 0 V / the negative rail), so its driver can be powered from a simple fixed supply. The HIGH-side switch's source, however, sits at the leg's SWITCHED MIDPOINT voltage, which swings between the two rail voltages as the leg switches — the high-side driver's own supply must "float" and follow this swinging reference. A [[bootstrap|bootstrap supply]] solves this cheaply: a small capacitor and diode that charge while the low-side switch is on (when the midpoint is near the low rail, allowing the bootstrap capacitor to charge relative to it), then supply the high-side driver once it floats up with the midpoint — simple and cheap, but it needs regular low-side on-time to stay charged, which is a real design constraint at very high or very low duty cycles.

### Dead time: preventing shoot-through

If both switches of one leg were ever on simultaneously, even briefly, they would form a direct short circuit across the DC bus — [[shoot-through|shoot-through]] — destroying both switches in microseconds. Every competent gate-driver design enforces [[dead-time|dead time]]: a short, deliberate interval (commonly 0.5–3 µs) where NEITHER switch is driven on, ensuring one has fully turned off before the other is allowed to turn on, even accounting for each device's own turn-off delay.

### Isolated gate drivers

Where the high-side reference swings over a very large voltage range, or where safety requires a complete galvanic barrier between control and power stages, an isolated gate driver (using an isolation transformer or optocoupler internally) is used instead of a bootstrap supply — more complex and costly, but able to float over arbitrarily large voltage swings and provide true electrical isolation.`,technical:`**Bootstrap capacitor sizing.** Too small a bootstrap capacitor droops excessively during a long high-side on-time (risking insufficient gate voltage); too large slows its own recharging during the required low-side on-time — a genuine sizing trade-off, not an arbitrary choice.

**Dead time versus switching loss.** Longer dead time gives a larger shoot-through safety margin but also means a longer interval where the leg's output is determined by whichever device's body/freewheeling diode happens to be conducting (rather than the intended switch), introducing a small timing-dependent distortion at the output — so dead time is set with margin, not maximised arbitrarily.`,diagrams:[{id:`deadtime`,caption:`The enforced dead-time interval between one switch turning off and the other turning on in a bridge leg, preventing shoot-through.`}],formulas:[{name:`Minimum dead time (practical rule)`,expr:`t_dead > t_off(slowest switch) + margin`,where:[[`t_dead`,`enforced dead time`,`s`],[`t_off`,`the slower switch's actual turn-off delay`,`s`]],note:`Dead time must comfortably exceed the slowest device's own turn-off delay, with margin for variation between devices and temperature.`}],examples:[{title:`Why shoot-through is so destructive`,problem:`Explain, in terms of path resistance, why even a brief overlap of both switches in a leg being on causes severe damage.`,steps:[`Both switches being on connects the full DC bus voltage across only the switches' own low on-state resistance/voltage, with no load limiting current`,`Current rises extremely rapidly (limited only by stray inductance and the switches' own tiny resistance), reaching destructive levels in microseconds`,`This is functionally identical to directly short-circuiting the bus with a piece of wire`],answer:`Shoot-through presents almost no impedance to the full bus voltage, so current rises to destructive levels almost instantly — this is why dead time is treated as a non-negotiable safety margin, not a tunable performance parameter.`},{title:`Why a bootstrap supply needs regular low-side on-time`,problem:`A leg is driven at a very high duty cycle, so the high-side switch is on almost continuously and the low-side switch is on only very briefly each cycle. Explain the risk to the bootstrap supply.`,steps:[`The bootstrap capacitor only recharges while the low-side switch is on`,`A very brief low-side on-time gives very little recharging opportunity each cycle`,`Meanwhile the high-side driver continuously draws a small current from the bootstrap capacitor while the high side is on`],answer:`The bootstrap capacitor can droop further each cycle than it recovers, eventually leaving the high-side gate under-driven — a known limitation of bootstrap supplies at extreme duty cycles, one reason isolated drivers are sometimes chosen instead for such applications.`}],applications:`Every H-bridge and half-bridge exercise in the Circuit Connection Laboratory assumes correctly enforced dead time; the Repair Workshop's gate-driver-signal-lost scenario is the direct fault-mode counterpart of this lesson.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a microcontroller pin can switch a power MOSFET directly.`,correction:`A dedicated gate driver is needed to supply the fast, high-current gate charge/discharge a power switch requires.`},{mistake:`Treating dead time as a performance knob to minimise for best waveform quality.`,correction:`Dead time is primarily a shoot-through safety margin; it must comfortably exceed the slowest switch's turn-off delay, not be minimised for cosmetic waveform reasons.`},{mistake:`Forgetting that a bootstrap supply needs the low-side switch to turn on periodically to stay charged.`,correction:`At very high (or very low) duty cycles, bootstrap charging can become marginal — a genuine design limitation, not a minor detail.`}],demo:{title:`Check a gate-driver test procedure`,text:`Walk through the gate-driver test procedure, noting the safety steps around the floating high-side reference.`,link:e.proc(`Gate driver test (scope)`,`gate-driver`,`driver-scope`)},activities:[e.lib(`Gate driver`,`gate-driver`),e.proc(`Gate driver scope test`,`gate-driver`,`driver-scope`),e.repair(`Gate-driver signal lost`,`rsc-10`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A gate driver is needed mainly because...`,options:[`switching a power device quickly needs a fast, high-current pulse beyond a logic signal's capability`,`the gate needs a continuous large current to stay on`,`MOSFETs have no gate`,`IGBTs do not need gate drive`],answer:0,explain:`The gate itself draws little steady current, but switching transitions need a fast charge/discharge pulse.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A bootstrap supply charges while...`,options:[`the low-side switch is on`,`the high-side switch is on`,`both switches are off`,`the bus is disconnected`],answer:0,explain:`The midpoint sits near the low rail during low-side conduction, allowing the bootstrap capacitor to charge.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Dead time exists to prevent...`,options:[`shoot-through (both leg switches on at once)`,`excessive switching frequency`,`reverse polarity`,`gate driver overheating`],answer:0,explain:`A direct short across the bus if both switches overlap.`}],summary:[`A gate driver supplies the fast, high-current gate charge/discharge a power device needs, which a logic-level signal cannot provide directly.`,`A bootstrap supply (capacitor and diode) powers a floating high-side driver cheaply, charging during low-side on-time.`,`Dead time is an enforced interval with neither leg switch on, preventing destructive shoot-through — set with safety margin, not minimised for performance.`,`Isolated gate drivers replace bootstrap supplies where voltage swing or safety isolation requirements exceed what a bootstrap can provide.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L6.hw`,title:`Gate driver reasoning practice`,brief:`Reason about dead time and bootstrap limitations.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`If dead time is set shorter than the slowest switch's actual turn-off delay, the risk is...`,options:[`shoot-through`,`reduced switching frequency`,`lower conduction loss`,`improved waveform quality`],answer:0,explain:`The faster switch could turn on before the slower one has fully turned off.`}],written:[{prompt:`A bridge leg's output waveform shows a brief, unintended glitch at every switching transition. Diagnostics confirm dead time is correctly set and shoot-through is not occurring. Suggest, using this lesson's ideas, a plausible benign explanation for the glitch, rather than assuming a fault.`,model:`During the dead-time interval itself, with neither switch actively driven on, the leg's output is determined by whichever device's body or freewheeling diode happens to be conducting the load current at that moment — not by either switch's intended state. This is an expected, designed-in consequence of dead time (not a fault): the output briefly follows the conducting diode's voltage during every dead-time window, producing a small, consistent glitch at every transition. Confirming the glitch's size and timing match the configured dead time, rather than appearing randomly, would support this benign explanation over a developing fault.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M2.L7`,title:`Switching characteristics and losses`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Distinguish conduction loss from switching loss and explain how each scales with operating conditions.`,`Explain why switching loss rises with switching frequency.`,`Combine conduction and switching loss into a total device loss estimate.`],introduction:`F4.M2.L4 and L5 covered each device family's conduction loss separately. This lesson adds the other half of the picture — [[switching-loss|switching loss]] — and shows how the two combine into the total heat a switching device actually produces, which (F4.M5.L3) ultimately sets its cooling requirement.`,lecture:`### Two different loss mechanisms

- **[[conduction-loss|Conduction loss]]**: dissipated while the device is fully ON, carrying load current (I²·R_DS(on) for a MOSFET, V_CE(sat)·I for an IGBT, F4.M2.L4/L5). Independent of switching frequency.
- **[[switching-loss|Switching loss]]**: dissipated during each turn-ON and turn-OFF TRANSITION, while voltage and current briefly overlap (the device is neither fully off nor fully on) before settling to its new state, plus (for an IGBT) the tail-current energy at turn-off. This happens once per transition, so its total POWER contribution scales roughly in proportion to switching FREQUENCY — more transitions per second means more switching-loss energy dissipated per second.

### Why the trade-off matters for design choices

A higher switching frequency (F3.M5, P3) gives a smaller, cheaper output filter (because the switching "noise" sits further above the fundamental and is easier to filter) but directly raises switching loss. A design must balance filter size/cost against switching loss/cooling cost — neither extreme is free, and the right balance depends on the specific device, voltage and power level, which is exactly why switching frequency is a genuine design decision, not an arbitrary number.

### Total device loss

Total power dissipated by one switching device is, to a reasonable first approximation, the SUM of its conduction loss and its switching loss at the actual operating current, voltage and switching frequency. This total is what the thermal design (F4.M5.L3) must successfully remove as heat to keep the device below its maximum junction temperature.`,technical:`**Switching energy (E_on, E_off).** Datasheets commonly quote turn-on and turn-off energy (in µJ or mJ) at a stated test voltage and current; switching loss power is then approximately P_sw ≈ (E_on + E_off) × f_sw, scaled for the actual operating voltage and current if they differ from the datasheet test conditions (switching energy scales roughly with both voltage and current, though not always perfectly linearly).

**Soft-switching techniques** (mentioned for context, beyond this course's main scope) arrange the circuit so that voltage or current is near zero during a transition, substantially reducing switching loss — used in some higher-frequency converter topologies at the cost of added circuit complexity.`,diagrams:[{id:`thermal-network`,caption:`Conduction loss and switching loss both feeding into the same total heat a switching device must shed through its thermal path.`}],formulas:[{name:`Switching loss (approx.)`,expr:`P_sw ≈ (E_on + E_off) · f_sw`,where:[[`P_sw`,`switching loss power`,`W`],[`E_on`,`turn-on switching energy per event`,`J`],[`E_off`,`turn-off switching energy per event`,`J`],[`f_sw`,`switching frequency`,`Hz`]]},{name:`Total device loss`,expr:`P_total = P_cond + P_sw`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Switching loss scaling with frequency`,problem:`A device has combined switching energy (E_on+E_off) = 50 µJ. Compare switching loss at 10 kHz and 50 kHz.`,steps:[`At 10 kHz: P = 50e-6 × 10,000 = 0.5 W`,`At 50 kHz: P = 50e-6 × 50,000 = 2.5 W`],answer:`Switching loss rises fivefold (0.5 W → 2.5 W) for a fivefold frequency increase — directly proportional, as the formula predicts.`},{title:`Total device loss`,problem:`A MOSFET has conduction loss 6 W and switching loss 2.5 W at its operating point. Total device loss, and what must remove it?`,steps:[`P_total = P_cond + P_sw`,`= 6 + 2.5`],answer:`= 8.5 W, which the device's thermal path (case, heat sink, airflow, F4.M5.L3) must remove to keep junction temperature within its rated limit.`}],applications:`Every efficiency figure quoted for a Build Your Own Inverter project, and every heat-sink/fan sizing decision in F4.M5.L3, ultimately traces back to exactly this conduction-plus-switching loss sum for each switching device in the design.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming switching loss is negligible compared with conduction loss.`,correction:`At higher switching frequencies, switching loss can equal or exceed conduction loss — it must be checked, not assumed away.`},{mistake:`Believing conduction loss depends on switching frequency.`,correction:`Conduction loss depends on current and on-state resistance/voltage only, independent of how fast the device switches; only switching loss scales with frequency.`},{mistake:`Comparing switching-energy figures from different datasheet test conditions without scaling.`,correction:`E_on/E_off figures are quoted at specific test voltage and current; scale appropriately (or use manufacturer-provided scaling curves) before applying them at a different operating point.`}],demo:{title:`See real loss figures`,text:`Compare conduction and switching behaviour on the MOSFET switching circuit exercise.`,link:e.ckt(`MOSFET switching`,`ckt-06`)},activities:[e.ckt(`MOSFET switching`,`ckt-06`),e.sim(`PWM generation experiment`,`sim-06`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Switching loss (W) for (E_on+E_off)=30 µJ at 20 kHz?`,answer:.6,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`30e-6×20000=0.6 W.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Switching loss scales roughly in proportion to...`,options:[`switching frequency`,`conduction current only`,`ambient temperature only`,`it does not scale with anything`],answer:0,explain:`More transitions per second means more switching-loss energy dissipated per second.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Total device loss is best estimated as...`,options:[`conduction loss plus switching loss`,`conduction loss only`,`switching loss only`,`the larger of the two, ignoring the smaller`],answer:0,explain:`Both mechanisms contribute real heat and should be summed.`}],summary:[`Conduction loss (frequency-independent) and switching loss (frequency-dependent) are two separate mechanisms that together set total device loss.`,`Switching loss power scales roughly in proportion to switching frequency: P_sw ≈ (E_on+E_off)×f_sw.`,`Higher switching frequency trades smaller/cheaper filtering against higher switching loss — a genuine design balance.`,`Total device loss (conduction + switching) is what the thermal design must remove as heat.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L7.hw`,title:`Combined loss practice`,brief:`Compute conduction, switching and total loss.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Switching loss (W) for (E_on+E_off)=40 µJ at 25 kHz?`,answer:1,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`40e-6×25000=1 W.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Total loss (W) for conduction loss 5 W and switching loss 1.2 W?`,answer:6.2,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`5+1.2=6.2 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`A design team proposes doubling a converter's switching frequency to halve its output filter's physical size, without changing the switching devices. Explain what else must be re-checked before accepting this change, using this lesson's loss model.`,model:`Doubling switching frequency roughly doubles switching-loss power (since P_sw scales in proportion to frequency), while conduction loss stays unchanged. The new total device loss, and whether the existing heat sink and airflow can still keep the device below its maximum junction temperature at this higher total loss, must be re-checked (F4.M5.L3) — shrinking the filter is not free, and the thermal design that was adequate at the original frequency may no longer be adequate at the doubled frequency.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M2.L8`,title:`Component testing: diode, MOSFET and IGBT checks`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Apply the diode-test method to confirm a diode, a MOSFET body diode and an IGBT's co-packaged diode.`,`Apply the out-of-circuit MOSFET check (diode test plus gate-charge test) to confirm a suspect switch.`,`Explain why these devices are normally tested OUT of circuit, removed from the board.`],introduction:`This lesson turns F4.M2.L2–L5's theory into bench practice: the actual test procedures used in the Component Library and the Repair Workshop to confirm whether a diode, MOSFET or IGBT is healthy, out of circuit and before it is ever trusted in a repair.`,lecture:`### The diode-test method

Most digital multimeters have a dedicated "diode test" range: it forces a small, known current through the probes and displays the resulting voltage drop, which for a healthy silicon PN junction reads close to its expected forward voltage (roughly 0.5–0.7 V) in one probe direction and "OL" (open/no reading) in the other — confirming correct one-directional conduction. A short reads near 0 V in BOTH directions; an open reads OL in both.

### Testing a MOSFET out of circuit

The MOSFET check in the Component Library combines three steps, each isolating one failure mode: (1) diode-test the body diode (source to drain forward, drain to source reverse) to catch a shorted or badly leaking device; (2) diode-test gate-to-source and gate-to-drain in BOTH directions, expecting OL both ways, to confirm the gate oxide has not been punctured (a gate short is a common consequence of a voltage spike reaching the gate, often implicating the gate driver too); (3) briefly charge the gate (forward-biasing it above V_th with the meter's own diode-test voltage, or a small bench supply) and re-check drain-source conduction, confirming the channel actually turns on when commanded.

### Testing an IGBT out of circuit

Because an IGBT's gate is also insulated (like a MOSFET's), a similar two-part check applies: diode-test its co-packaged anti-parallel diode (collector-emitter reverse direction for the diode's forward path), then drive the gate on (commonly +15 V through a current-limiting resistor from a bench supply, since IGBTs are usually used at voltages where a meter's own diode-test current is not representative) and confirm the device conducts with a low V_CE, matching its expected V_CE(sat).

### Why out of circuit

As with passive components (F4.M1.L4), testing in circuit risks reading a parallel path through the rest of the board — doubly true for semiconductor devices in a bridge, where neighbouring devices and freewheeling paths can easily mask or mimic a fault if the suspect device is not isolated first.`,technical:`**Gate-charge test current.** A meter's diode-test range typically forces only a few hundred microamps to a few milliamps — enough to momentarily charge a MOSFET's small gate capacitance above V_th for a quick functional check, but far below what a real gate driver supplies; this test confirms the device CAN turn on, not its full switching speed or current-handling capability.

**ESD precautions.** MOSFET and IGBT gates, being capacitively coupled through a thin oxide layer, can be damaged by an electrostatic discharge through the gate pin — anti-static precautions (grounded wrist strap, anti-static mat) are a standard, non-optional part of handling these parts out of circuit, not an excessive precaution.`,diagrams:[{id:`test-bench`,caption:`The sequence of a MOSFET out-of-circuit check: body diode, gate insulation in both directions, then gate-charge functional test.`}],formulas:[{name:`Expected diode-test reading (healthy silicon)`,expr:`V_f ≈ 0.45–0.7 V (forward), OL (reverse)`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Interpreting a MOSFET body-diode reading`,problem:`A suspect MOSFET reads 0.00 V (not OL) with the meter's red lead on source and black on drain, in diode-test mode. What does this indicate?`,steps:[`A healthy body diode would read roughly 0.5 V in this forward direction, not 0.00 V`,`0.00 V in a direction that should show a diode drop indicates a direct short, not a healthy junction`],answer:`The MOSFET has a drain-source short (a "failed short" device) and should be replaced; 0.00 V where a diode drop is expected is the signature reading, matching the Repair Workshop's MOSFET-failed-short scenario.`},{title:`Interpreting a gate-insulation reading`,problem:`A suspect MOSFET reads 0.0x V (a small but non-zero reading, not OL) from gate to source. What does this suggest, and what else should be checked?`,steps:[`A healthy gate should read OL (no path) in this test — the gate oxide should present an open circuit`,`A small, non-zero reading suggests the gate oxide has been punctured, often from a voltage spike`],answer:`The gate has very likely been punctured (a gate short), and — because a spike reaching the gate this way is often caused by a gate-driver fault rather than a random event — the gate driver itself should also be checked before simply replacing the switch.`}],applications:`These exact readings, in this exact sequence, are what you take and interpret in the Repair Workshop's MOSFET-failed-open and MOSFET-failed-short scenarios — this lesson is the theory behind those live measurements.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Testing a MOSFET or IGBT while still soldered into the board.`,correction:`Remove it (or at minimum lift the relevant leads) first — parallel paths through the rest of the board can mask or mimic a fault.`},{mistake:`Skipping the gate-insulation check because the body-diode reading looked healthy.`,correction:`A healthy body diode does not rule out a punctured gate — both failure modes are independent and both must be checked.`},{mistake:`Forgetting ESD precautions when handling a MOSFET or IGBT out of circuit.`,correction:`The insulated gate is genuinely vulnerable to electrostatic discharge damage; anti-static handling is standard practice, not optional caution.`}],demo:{title:`Run the MOSFET check procedure`,text:`Work through the full three-step out-of-circuit MOSFET check.`,link:e.proc(`Out-of-circuit MOSFET check`,`mosfet`,`mosfet-diode`)},activities:[e.proc(`MOSFET diode/gate-charge check`,`mosfet`,`mosfet-diode`),e.proc(`IGBT check`,`igbt`,`igbt-check`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A healthy silicon body diode, forward direction, reads approximately...`,options:[`0.5–0.7 V`,`0.00 V`,`OL`,`5 V`],answer:0,explain:`A typical silicon forward-voltage reading.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A gate-to-source reading of OL (both directions) on a MOSFET indicates...`,options:[`the gate oxide is intact (healthy)`,`the gate is punctured`,`the channel is conducting`,`the device is shorted`],answer:0,explain:`OL both ways means no path exists, as expected for an intact insulated gate.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A 0.00 V diode-test reading where a body-diode drop is expected indicates...`,options:[`a drain-source short`,`a healthy device`,`an open gate`,`normal gate-charge behaviour`],answer:0,explain:`A genuine diode drop should not read as a dead short.`}],summary:[`A meter's diode-test range reads a healthy silicon junction's forward voltage (~0.5–0.7 V) one way and OL the other.`,`A MOSFET's out-of-circuit check combines a body-diode test, a gate-insulation test (both directions) and a gate-charge functional test.`,`An IGBT check combines its co-packaged diode test with a driven-gate functional check (commonly from a bench supply at the device's real operating voltage).`,`Testing out of circuit, with ESD precautions for the insulated gate, avoids both masked parallel-path readings and accidental gate damage.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L8.hw`,title:`Semiconductor test interpretation`,brief:`Interpret diode-test readings for suspected faults.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A MOSFET reads OL in BOTH directions across drain-source in diode-test mode (no gate charge applied). This is...`,options:[`expected/healthy behaviour with the channel off`,`definitely a fault`,`impossible`,`evidence of a short`],answer:0,explain:`With no gate charge, the channel is off; OL both ways across drain-source before the body-diode direction is separately checked is a plausible healthy intermediate reading, not a fault by itself.`}],written:[{prompt:`A suspect MOSFET passes the body-diode test and the gate-insulation test, but fails to show the expected drain-source conduction after the gate-charge step. Explain what this pattern of results suggests, and why testing all three steps (rather than stopping after the first pass) matters.`,model:`Passing the body-diode and gate-insulation tests rules out a drain-source short and a punctured gate, but failing to conduct after the gate is charged indicates the channel itself is not forming correctly when commanded — the device cannot be turned on, even though it is not shorted or gate-punctured. This is a distinct failure mode (effectively an open or non-functional channel) from the other two, and it would have been missed entirely if testing had stopped after the first two steps passed — exactly why all three checks are needed to characterise a MOSFET fully, not just enough to find one problem and stop.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M2.L9`,title:`Fault identification: short, open, leakage`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Classify a semiconductor fault as short, open or excessive leakage from its symptoms and readings.`,`Explain typical root causes for each fault category in MOSFETs, IGBTs and diodes.`,`Explain why identifying the ROOT CAUSE, not just the failed part, matters before replacement.`],introduction:`Every semiconductor fault you will meet, in this course and in the Repair Workshop, reduces to one of three categories: short, open, or excessive leakage. This lesson gives you the vocabulary and reasoning to classify a fault correctly and — critically — to ask WHY it happened before simply replacing the part.`,lecture:`### The three fault categories

- **Short**: the device conducts in both directions, or in a direction/state it should not, offering near-zero resistance where a healthy device would block or show a diode drop. Common causes: voltage/current beyond rating, gate oxide puncture leading to channel failure, or manufacturing/age-related breakdown.
- **Open**: the device fails to conduct even when it should (a blown internal bond wire, a cracked die, a severed internal connection). The device may read OL everywhere, including paths that should show a diode drop.
- **Excessive leakage**: the device still broadly behaves correctly (blocks when it should, conducts when it should) but its OFF-state leakage current has risen far above healthy — often an early-stage, partially-degraded fault rather than a complete failure, and sometimes missed by a simple pass/fail diode test if the leakage is not large enough to show up as a clear reading change.

### Root cause matters, not just the symptom

A shorted MOSFET is a SYMPTOM; the question that actually prevents a repeat failure is WHY it shorted. Common real-world root causes include: a gate driver fault that let shoot-through occur (F4.M2.L6); exceeding the device's voltage rating from an unsuppressed transient; exceeding its current rating from an unrelated downstream fault (an output short, or a saturating inductor, F4.M1.L3); or simple end-of-life degradation under normal stress. Replacing the failed device without investigating which of these applies risks an immediate repeat failure — exactly the reasoning the Repair Workshop's rubric rewards (diagnosis AND repair AND verification, not diagnosis alone).

### Cascading failures

Semiconductor faults often cascade: a shorted MOSFET can blow the fuse protecting it (F4.M4.L1), and a lost gate-driver signal (F4.M2.L6) can leave a switch permanently off, which can then starve a downstream load or confuse a feedback loop. Correctly distinguishing the ORIGINAL fault from its downstream consequences is part of fault identification, not a separate skill.`,technical:`**Leakage current versus temperature.** Reverse leakage current in any semiconductor junction roughly doubles for every 8–10 °C rise in junction temperature (an approximate rule of thumb, not a precise law) — a device with borderline-elevated leakage at room temperature can leak dramatically more once hot, sometimes triggering thermal runaway in a weakened part.

**Intermittent faults.** A fault that only appears under load, vibration or temperature (a cracked solder joint, a marginal connector, F4.M4) can pass every static out-of-circuit test yet still be the real cause of a reported symptom — this is why the Repair Workshop's checkpoints also test REPRODUCING a fault under representative conditions, not just a single static reading.`,diagrams:[{id:`fault-method`,caption:`Classifying a semiconductor fault as short, open or excessive leakage, then tracing back to a plausible root cause before repair.`}],formulas:[{name:`Approximate leakage-current temperature rule`,expr:`I_leak(T+10°C) ≈ 2 × I_leak(T)`,where:[[`I_leak`,`reverse/off-state leakage current`,`A`]],note:`A rough rule of thumb for reasoning about thermal sensitivity of leakage faults, not a precise datasheet figure.`}],examples:[{title:`Classifying a fault from a reading`,problem:`A diode reads 0.00 V in both forward and reverse diode-test directions. Classify this fault.`,steps:[`Healthy: ~0.6 V forward, OL reverse`,`Reading 0.00 V in BOTH directions means it conducts both ways with no barrier at all`],answer:`Short — the junction offers no blocking behaviour in either direction.`},{title:`Reasoning about root cause before replacement`,problem:`A replacement MOSFET, fitted after the original failed short, itself fails short again within minutes of being powered up. Suggest a reasoning approach rather than a specific cause.`,steps:[`A second, near-immediate failure of a like-for-like replacement strongly suggests an ongoing external cause, not a coincidental second random failure`,`Likely candidates to investigate: the gate driver (is it genuinely enforcing dead time and driving correctly?), the protection sized for this leg (is a fuse/breaker actually interrupting an overcurrent fast enough?), or an unrelated fault elsewhere forcing excessive current or voltage onto this device`],answer:`The repair should not stop at fitting a second replacement; the gate-driver signal, dead-time enforcement, and upstream protection sizing all need investigating as the likely ROOT cause before the switch is trusted to survive.`}],applications:`This exact reasoning — short/open/leakage classification, then root-cause investigation — is scored explicitly by the Repair Workshop's "diagnose" AND "eliminate" rubric categories; neither is satisfied by naming the symptom alone.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating "the MOSFET failed" as a complete diagnosis.`,correction:`Classify HOW it failed (short/open/leakage) and investigate WHY, before considering the diagnosis complete.`},{mistake:`Assuming a repeat failure of a replacement part means a bad batch of parts.`,correction:`A near-immediate repeat failure much more often indicates an unaddressed external root cause than coincidental part defects — investigate the circuit around the device first.`},{mistake:`Dismissing a borderline leakage reading as "probably fine" without comparing to a healthy reference.`,correction:`Elevated leakage is a real, if early-stage, fault signature — compare against a known-healthy reading rather than a gut-feel threshold.`}],demo:{title:`Revisit a faulted scenario`,text:`Work through the Repair Workshop's MOSFET-failed-short scenario, classifying the fault before confirming root cause.`,link:e.repair(`MOSFET failed short`,`rsc-09`)},activities:[e.repair(`MOSFET failed open`,`rsc-08`),e.repair(`MOSFET failed short`,`rsc-09`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A device that conducts in both directions with near-zero drop, where it should block one way, has failed...`,options:[`short`,`open`,`with elevated leakage only`,`within normal tolerance`],answer:0,explain:`No blocking behaviour in either direction is the short signature.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why investigate root cause before simply replacing a shorted switch?`,options:[`An unaddressed external cause (e.g. a gate-driver fault) can destroy the replacement too`,`It is required by the meter`,`Root cause never matters once the part is replaced`,`Replacement parts are always defective`],answer:0,explain:`Replacing without fixing the underlying cause risks an immediate repeat failure.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Reverse leakage current in a semiconductor junction typically...`,options:[`rises roughly exponentially with junction temperature`,`is unaffected by temperature`,`falls as temperature rises`,`only matters for resistors`],answer:0,explain:`A common rule of thumb is roughly doubling every 8–10 °C.`}],summary:[`Every semiconductor fault classifies as short, open, or excessive leakage, each with a distinct reading signature.`,`Classifying HOW a device failed is necessary but not sufficient — WHY it failed (root cause) must also be investigated.`,`A near-immediate repeat failure of a replacement part strongly suggests an unaddressed external root cause, not coincidence.`,`Leakage current rises sharply with temperature, so a borderline leakage fault can worsen dramatically once the device is hot.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L9.hw`,title:`Fault classification practice`,brief:`Classify faults from readings and reason about root cause.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A diode reads OL in both forward and reverse diode-test directions. This is classified as a...`,options:[`open fault`,`short fault`,`healthy device`,`leakage fault`],answer:0,explain:`No conduction at all in either direction, where forward conduction is expected, is the open-fault signature.`}],written:[{prompt:`A technician replaces a MOSFET that read as short, confirms the new part passes all three out-of-circuit checks, refits it, and the circuit works correctly for several weeks before the same fault recurs. Explain why this delayed recurrence is still consistent with an unaddressed root cause, rather than ruling one out.`,model:`A root cause does not have to produce an immediate failure every time — a marginal gate-driver timing issue, a borderline-undersized protective device, or an intermittent overcurrent condition elsewhere in the circuit can stress a replacement part gradually over weeks before it finally fails, rather than destroying it instantly. The delay does not rule out an unaddressed root cause; it is consistent with one that stresses the device more slowly than the original failure did. The same root-cause investigation (gate driver, protection sizing, other components feeding excessive current or voltage to this device) is still warranted, not ruled out by the gap in time.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M2.L10`,title:`Component replacement and compatibility`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`List the ratings that must be checked (not just matched on one headline figure) before fitting a replacement semiconductor.`,`Explain the risk of a "close enough" substitute part.`,`Explain why a replacement MOSFET or IGBT should be checked before (not only after) fitting.`],introduction:`This lesson closes the module by turning F4.M2's component knowledge into a disciplined REPLACEMENT checklist — the single most consequential practical skill this module builds toward, since a careless replacement can turn a successful diagnosis into a second failure.`,lecture:`### Matching more than one rating

A direct like-for-like replacement (the same manufacturer part number) is always the safest choice when available. Where it is not, every relevant rating from this module must be independently checked against the original: voltage rating (V_DS/V_CE(max), with margin), current rating (continuous and, where relevant, pulsed), R_DS(on) or V_CE(sat) (affects loss and thermal margin, not just "does it fit"), gate characteristics (V_th, gate charge — a mismatched gate driver/switch pairing can leave the new device under-driven or over-stressed), package and pinout, and — for diodes — V_rrm and reverse recovery time. Matching only the headline voltage or current rating while ignoring the rest is "close enough" thinking that frequently causes a second failure.

### Why "close enough" substitutes are a real risk

A substitute with a higher R_DS(on) than the original, fitted because its voltage and current ratings matched, will run hotter at the same load — potentially fine under light load, but capable of overheating and failing under the conditions that matter most (peak load, hot ambient), exactly the conditions the original part was sized for. Similarly, a substitute with different gate characteristics (a higher gate threshold, say) can leave the device under-driven by an existing gate driver sized for the original part's gate charge, causing excessive switching loss or incomplete turn-on that the original circuit's protection may not have been designed to catch.

### Checking a replacement before fitting, not only after

Applying the out-of-circuit checks from F4.M2.L8 to a NEW replacement part before fitting it — not only to the suspect failed part — catches a defective-from-new replacement before it is soldered in, and confirms the part pulled from stock is genuinely the type expected (mislabelled or miscounted stock is a real, if avoidable, source of repeat failures).`,technical:`**Second-source parts.** Many popular power devices are manufactured by more than one company under compatible (but rarely byte-for-byte identical) specifications — a second-source part can be a perfectly valid substitute, but its datasheet must still be checked individually rather than assumed identical to the original manufacturer's part.

**Upgrading, not just matching.** Occasionally a design deliberately fits a BETTER-rated replacement (lower R_DS(on), higher voltage margin) than the original, as a considered improvement — this is a valid engineering decision, but it should be a deliberate choice with its consequences considered (cost, gate-drive compatibility), not an accident of "whatever was in stock that seemed close".`,diagrams:[{id:`design-chain`,caption:`The chain of ratings — voltage, current, on-state loss, gate characteristics, package — that must each be checked before a replacement semiconductor is accepted as compatible.`}],formulas:[{name:`Minimum safe voltage margin (rule of thumb)`,expr:`V_rating ≥ 1.5 × V_peak(expected)`,where:[[`V_rating`,`device's rated blocking voltage`,`V`],[`V_peak`,`the highest voltage the device will actually see, including transients`,`V`]],note:`A commonly used rule of thumb for comfortable margin; actual required margin depends on the specific application and any additional transient suppression.`}],examples:[{title:`Checking voltage margin for a replacement`,problem:`A MOSFET's drain will see a peak of 60 V including switching transients. Is a 75 V-rated replacement an adequate margin, by the 1.5× rule of thumb?`,steps:[`Required: 1.5 × 60 = 90 V`,`75 V < 90 V`],answer:`No — 75 V falls short of the 90 V suggested by the 1.5× margin rule; a 100 V-rated (or higher) device would be the safer, standard choice here.`},{title:`Why a lower R_DS(on) substitute is not automatically "better"`,problem:`A substitute MOSFET has a much lower R_DS(on) than the original but a smaller gate charge capability relative to its size, meaning it needs faster, more demanding gate drive to switch cleanly at the same frequency. Explain the risk.`,steps:[`Lower R_DS(on) genuinely reduces conduction loss, which looks like a pure improvement`,`But if the existing gate driver cannot drive the new device's gate characteristics as cleanly as it drove the original, switching loss or switching behaviour (ringing, incomplete turn-on) could worsen instead`],answer:`A single improved rating does not guarantee overall improvement — gate-drive compatibility must be checked alongside R_DS(on), or the substitution can trade a conduction-loss improvement for a switching-loss or reliability regression.`}],applications:`This replacement discipline is exactly what the Repair Workshop's "repair" rubric category rewards: not just naming the right part family, but selecting and fitting a genuinely compatible replacement.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Matching only voltage and current rating, and assuming that is sufficient.`,correction:`On-state loss rating (R_DS(on)/V_CE(sat)) and gate characteristics must also be checked — a "fits the slot" substitute can still cause a second failure.`},{mistake:`Assuming a lower R_DS(on) or higher-rated substitute is automatically a safe upgrade.`,correction:`Check gate-drive compatibility and package/pinout too; an improvement in one rating does not guarantee overall compatibility.`},{mistake:`Fitting a replacement part straight from stock without testing it first.`,correction:`Apply the same out-of-circuit checks (F4.M2.L8) to the NEW part before fitting it, to catch a defective-from-new or mislabelled replacement.`}],demo:{title:`Compare MOSFET vs IGBT selection`,text:`Use the comparison guide to see how rating trade-offs are laid out side by side when selecting a device.`,link:e.cmp(`MOSFET vs IGBT comparison`,`mosfet-vs-igbt`)},activities:[e.cmp(`MOSFET vs IGBT`,`mosfet-vs-igbt`),e.repair(`MOSFET failed open`,`rsc-08`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Before fitting a non-identical replacement semiconductor, which of these must be checked?`,options:[`Voltage rating, current rating, on-state loss rating and gate characteristics, together`,`Voltage rating only`,`Package size only`,`Nothing — any part that fits physically is acceptable`],answer:0,explain:`Every relevant rating must be checked independently.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum suggested voltage rating (V) by the 1.5× rule for a 40 V expected peak?`,answer:60,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`1.5×40=60 V.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A replacement with a lower R_DS(on) but different gate characteristics than the original...`,options:[`is not automatically an improvement — gate-drive compatibility must also be checked`,`is always a safe and complete upgrade`,`cannot be used under any circumstance`,`has no effect on switching behaviour`],answer:0,explain:`One improved rating does not guarantee overall compatibility.`}],summary:[`A direct like-for-like replacement is the safest choice; otherwise every relevant rating (voltage, current, on-state loss, gate characteristics, package) must be checked independently.`,`A "close enough" substitute matched on only one headline rating is a frequent, avoidable cause of repeat failures.`,`A single improved rating on a substitute does not guarantee overall compatibility — other characteristics (especially gate drive) can regress.`,`Testing a new replacement part before fitting catches a defective-from-new or mislabelled part before it causes a second failure.`],homework:{id:`F4.M2.L10.hw`,title:`Replacement compatibility practice`,brief:`Check replacement ratings against the original.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum suggested voltage rating (V) by the 1.5× rule for a 100 V expected peak?`,answer:150,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`1.5×100=150 V.`}],written:[{prompt:`A spares drawer holds a MOSFET with the correct voltage and current rating for a repair, but its R_DS(on) is roughly three times higher than the original part's. Explain whether this is a safe substitute, and what would need to be re-checked before using it.`,model:`Matching voltage and current rating is necessary but not sufficient. A threefold higher R_DS(on) means roughly three times the conduction loss (P=I²R_DS(on)) at the same current — this substitute will run noticeably hotter than the original at the same load, and the existing heat sink/airflow (sized for the original device's lower loss) may not keep it within its safe operating temperature, especially at peak load. Before using it, the resulting conduction loss at the circuit's actual worst-case current, and whether the existing thermal design can still handle it, must both be checked — the "correct voltage and current rating" alone does not confirm this is a safe substitute.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A PN junction conducts easily when forward biased because...`,options:[`the depletion barrier shrinks, allowing carriers to cross`,`the silicon melts`,`current reverses direction spontaneously`,`doping is removed`],answer:0,explain:`Forward bias shrinks the built-in barrier.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Approximate diode conduction loss (W) for V_f=0.65 V at 10 A average?`,answer:6.5,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`0.65×10=6.5 W.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`How many diodes conduct at any instant in a bridge rectifier?`,options:[`two`,`one`,`four`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`One diagonal pair conducts.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`MOSFET conduction loss (W) for R_DS(on)=10 mΩ at 30 A RMS?`,answer:9,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`30²×0.01=900×0.01=9 W.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`IGBT conduction loss is best modelled as...`,options:[`P ≈ V_CE(sat) × I`,`P = I² × R`,`P = V²/R`,`IGBTs have zero conduction loss`],answer:0,explain:`A nearly constant saturation voltage, unlike a resistance.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Dead time exists mainly to prevent...`,options:[`shoot-through across a bridge leg`,`excessive gate charge`,`reverse battery polarity`,`capacitor ESR rise`],answer:0,explain:`Both leg switches on at once would short the DC bus.`},{id:`q7`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Switching loss (W) for (E_on+E_off)=25 µJ at 30 kHz?`,answer:.75,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`25e-6×30000=0.75 W.`},{id:`q8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A device reading 0.00 V in both diode-test directions, where one should show a diode drop, has failed...`,options:[`short`,`open`,`with normal leakage`,`within tolerance`],answer:0,explain:`No blocking behaviour in either direction.`},{id:`q9`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Before fitting a non-identical replacement MOSFET, which rating is it safe to skip checking?`,options:[`None — voltage, current, on-state loss and gate characteristics should all be checked`,`Current rating`,`Voltage rating`,`Gate characteristics`],answer:0,explain:`Every relevant rating should be checked; none is safe to skip.`}]},assignment:{id:`F4.M2.assignment`,title:`Semiconductor device selection and fault reasoning`,brief:`Apply loss calculations and fault classification to short scenarios.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Total device loss (W): conduction 8 W, switching 1.5 W?`,answer:9.5,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`8+1.5=9.5 W.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`IGBT conduction loss (W) for V_CE(sat)=1.6 V at 22 A average?`,answer:35.2,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`1.6×22=35.2 W.`},{id:`a3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A diode reads OL in both directions. This is classified as...`,options:[`open`,`short`,`healthy`,`elevated leakage`],answer:0,explain:`No conduction at all where forward conduction is expected.`}],written:[{prompt:`A repair technician fits a replacement MOSFET matched exactly on voltage and current rating, but with a noticeably different gate threshold voltage (V_th) than the original, and the existing gate driver's output voltage was only just adequate for the original part. Explain the risk this specific mismatch creates, distinct from the voltage/current ratings being correct.`,model:`A higher V_th on the replacement, combined with a gate driver whose output was only marginally adequate for the original part's lower threshold, risks the new device not being driven fully on — leaving it operating in a partially-conducting state with a much higher effective on-resistance than its rated R_DS(on), or switching more slowly than intended. This would show up as excessive heating or poor switching behaviour even though voltage and current ratings matched exactly, because gate threshold and gate-drive adequacy are an independent compatibility check, not implied by matching the device's power ratings.`}]},practicals:[{id:`F4.M2.P1`,title:`Semiconductor fault classification and loss calculations`,description:`Classify faults from readings and compute device losses. All answers must be correct.`,task:{id:`f4-semiconductor-faults`,kind:`calc`,title:`Classifying faults and computing losses`,brief:`A diode reads 0.60 V forward / OL reverse (healthy reference). A suspect MOSFET reads 0.00 V in both diode-test directions across drain-source. A second MOSFET has R_DS(on)=18 mΩ and carries 24 A RMS, plus switching energy (E_on+E_off)=35 µJ at 15 kHz.`,questions:[{id:`c1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`How is the suspect MOSFET's fault classified?`,options:[`Short`,`Open`,`Elevated leakage`,`Healthy`],answer:0,explain:`0.00 V both ways where a diode drop is expected is a short.`},{id:`c2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Conduction loss (W) of the second MOSFET?`,answer:10.368,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`24²×0.018=576×0.018=10.368 W.`},{id:`c3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Switching loss (W) of the second MOSFET?`,answer:.525,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`35e-6×15000=0.525 W.`},{id:`c4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Total device loss (W)?`,answer:10.893,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`10.368+0.525=10.893 W.`}],explain:`A full semiconductor fault assessment classifies the fault pattern correctly AND computes real operating losses — neither alone is a complete answer.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`f4-semiconductor-faults`}},{id:`F4.M2.P2`,title:`Diagnose a MOSFET fault in the Repair Workshop`,description:`Confirm, on a real circuit-grounded fault, that you can apply the body-diode and gate-charge reasoning from this module.`,link:e.repair(`MOSFET failed short`,`rsc-09`),evidence:{kind:`troubleshoot`,project:`rsc-09`}}]},{id:`F4.M3`,title:`Magnetic components`,summary:`Magnetic circuits and saturation, transformers at low and high frequency, output and filter inductors, and testing windings.`,lessons:[{id:`F4.M3.L1`,title:`Magnetic circuits: flux, B-H, saturation`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a magnetic core concentrates flux produced by a winding's current.`,`Read a B-H curve's general shape and locate the saturation knee on it.`,`Explain why core material choice is a genuine engineering trade-off, not an arbitrary preference.`],introduction:`F4.M1.L3 introduced [[saturation|saturation]] for a single inductor. This lesson gives the underlying magnetic-circuit picture properly, as the shared foundation for both inductors and the transformer this module covers next.`,lecture:`### Flux and the magnetic circuit

Current flowing through a [[winding|winding]] produces a magnetic field; a core of magnetic material (iron, ferrite, F4.M1.L3) concentrates this field into a well-defined path, called [[magnetic-flux|magnetic flux (Φ)]], much as a wire concentrates electric current into a well-defined path. The core's **permeability** describes how strongly it concentrates flux for a given magnetising force — a high-permeability core gives far more inductance (or far more flux for the same current) than an air core of the same geometry.

### The B-H curve

A core material's behaviour is summarised by its B-H curve: B (flux density, how much flux per unit cross-sectional area) plotted against H (magnetising force, proportional to current and turns). At low H, B rises steeply and roughly linearly with H — this is the normal, useful operating region, where inductance stays essentially constant. Above some H, the curve bends over sharply (the "saturation knee") and B rises only slowly however much further H increases — the core has run out of additional flux-carrying capacity, and effective inductance collapses (F4.M1.L3's saturation).

### Why core material is a real design trade-off

Different core materials trade permeability, saturation flux density, loss at a given frequency, cost and temperature stability against each other — there is no single "best" material for every application. A material with very high permeability (more inductance per turn) may saturate at a lower flux density, or have excessive loss at the switching frequency the design actually needs; a material with excellent high-frequency loss behaviour may need more turns (more copper, more cost, more copper loss) to reach the same inductance. Selecting a core is a balance across all of these, driven by the application's voltage, current and frequency.`,technical:`**Core losses: hysteresis and eddy current.** Beyond the DC saturation picture, a core operating on AC also dissipates energy on every cycle through hysteresis loss (the B-H curve does not retrace itself exactly, enclosing a small loop whose area is energy lost per cycle) and eddy-current loss (circulating currents induced within the core material itself by the changing flux, reduced by laminating the core into thin, insulated sheets or by using a naturally resistive material like ferrite).

**Air gaps.** A deliberate small gap in an otherwise closed magnetic core lowers overall permeability but raises the current at which saturation occurs, letting a designer trade some inductance-per-turn for more current-handling headroom — a standard technique in inductor design, not a manufacturing defect.`,diagrams:[{id:`rl-curve`,caption:`A B-H curve: the roughly linear low-field region, the saturation knee, and the flattened region beyond it where additional magnetising force produces little extra flux.`}],formulas:[{name:`Flux density (concept)`,expr:`B = Φ / A`,where:[[`B`,`flux density`,`T (tesla)`],[`Φ`,`magnetic flux`,`Wb (weber)`],[`A`,`core cross-sectional area`,`m²`]],note:`Saturation is reached at a material-specific maximum B — beyond it, further current gives little additional flux.`}],examples:[{title:`Reasoning about the saturation knee`,problem:`A core's datasheet gives a saturation flux density of 0.35 T for a ferrite material and 1.5 T for an iron-powder material, for cores of similar size. Explain one practical consequence of this difference.`,steps:[`A higher saturation flux density means the iron-powder core can carry substantially more flux (and hence handle more current) before saturating, for a similar core size`,`The ferrite core, with its lower saturation point, would need to be physically larger (or accept a lower current rating) to avoid saturating at the same current`],answer:`For a given current rating, the iron-powder core can be smaller (or carry more current at the same size) before saturating than the lower-saturation ferrite — exactly why material choice depends on the application's needs, not a single universally-best material.`},{title:`Why an air gap raises the saturation current`,problem:`Explain, conceptually, why adding a small air gap to an otherwise closed core raises the current at which it saturates, at the cost of some inductance per turn.`,steps:[`The air gap has far lower permeability than the core material, so it dominates the magnetic circuit's overall "resistance" to flux (its magnetic reluctance)`,`This lowers the overall inductance per turn for the same winding`,`But it also means a much larger magnetising force (current) is needed before the CORE material itself reaches its own saturation flux density, since most of the magnetic "effort" is now spent across the gap`],answer:`The gap trades away some inductance per turn in exchange for a substantially higher current-handling headroom before saturation — a deliberate, standard inductor-design technique.`}],applications:`This magnetic-circuit picture underlies every transformer and inductor you will meet in F4.M3.L2–L3, and the saturation reasoning you apply when diagnosing an overloaded output choke in the Repair Workshop.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming all core materials behave identically, differing only in price.`,correction:`Permeability, saturation flux density, and high-frequency loss genuinely differ by material, and the right choice depends on the application.`},{mistake:`Treating an air gap in a core as a manufacturing fault.`,correction:`A deliberate air gap is a standard design technique that trades inductance per turn for higher saturation-current headroom.`},{mistake:`Believing saturation means "the current stops flowing".`,correction:`Saturation means the CORE stops contributing additional flux for more current — current often then rises sharply, the opposite of stopping.`}],demo:{title:`See the transformer entry next`,text:`This magnetic-circuit picture is the foundation for the transformer lesson that follows.`,link:e.lib(`Transformer`,`transformer`)},activities:[e.lib(`Transformer`,`transformer`),e.lib(`Inductor`,`inductor`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A core's permeability describes...`,options:[`how strongly it concentrates flux for a given magnetising force`,`its physical weight`,`its colour`,`its cost only`],answer:0,explain:`Higher permeability concentrates more flux for the same current/turns.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The "saturation knee" on a B-H curve marks...`,options:[`where further magnetising force produces little additional flux`,`where the core material melts`,`the origin (zero point) of the graph`,`the point of maximum permeability`],answer:0,explain:`The curve flattens once the core runs out of flux-carrying capacity.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A deliberate air gap in a core...`,options:[`lowers inductance per turn but raises the saturation current`,`has no effect on magnetic behaviour`,`always increases inductance`,`is always a manufacturing defect`],answer:0,explain:`A standard trade-off technique in inductor design.`}],summary:[`A magnetic core concentrates the flux produced by a current-carrying winding, described by its permeability.`,`A B-H curve's roughly linear low-field region gives way to a saturation knee, beyond which additional current produces little extra flux.`,`Core material choice trades permeability, saturation flux density, high-frequency loss and cost against each other — no single material wins every application.`,`A deliberate air gap trades some inductance per turn for a higher saturation-current rating.`],homework:{id:`F4.M3.L1.hw`,title:`Magnetic circuit reasoning`,brief:`Reason about core material trade-offs.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A core material with very high permeability but a low saturation flux density is best suited to an application with...`,options:[`low current and a need for high inductance in a small size`,`very high current and minimal concern for size`,`no current at all`,`only AC applications above 1 MHz`],answer:0,explain:`High permeability gives compact high inductance, but low saturation limits current headroom.`}],written:[{prompt:`An output choke saturates under a sustained overload, and a colleague proposes simply winding more turns on the same core to "increase its inductance and fix the problem". Explain why this specific fix is likely to make the saturation problem worse, not better, referring to the B-H curve.`,model:`Adding turns increases inductance at LOW current (more flux per unit current, since inductance scales with turns squared for a given core), but it does not raise the core's saturation flux density, which is a property of the core material itself, not the winding. For the SAME applied voltage, a higher-inductance winding reaches the same saturation flux density at a LOWER current than before (since more turns means more flux is produced per ampere), so the core would likely saturate at an even lower current than the original design — the opposite of the intended fix. Addressing saturation under overload generally needs a core with a higher saturation flux density or current rating (or a gapped core, or simply addressing the overload itself), not more turns on the same core.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M3.L2`,title:`Transformers in inverters: LF vs HF`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain the role of a transformer in a low-frequency (LF) inverter design versus a high-frequency (HF) link design.`,`Compare the size, weight and efficiency trade-offs between LF and HF transformer designs.`,`State why galvanic isolation matters for an inverter's transformer, beyond voltage transformation alone.`],introduction:`You met the [[transformer|transformer]]'s basic principle in F3.M4: mutual induction between windings requires a changing flux, which is why only AC (or switched DC) transforms. This lesson applies that principle to the two very different ways real inverters actually use a transformer: at line frequency (50/60 Hz) or inside a high-frequency switching link.`,lecture:`### Low-frequency (LF) transformer designs

A traditional "LF" inverter design switches the battery DC directly at the output frequency (50/60 Hz, or a stepped approximation of it) through a line-frequency transformer that steps the switched low voltage up to mains-level AC. This transformer is large and heavy (transformer size scales inversely with operating frequency for a given power, since lower frequency needs more [[magnetic-flux|flux]] swing per cycle and hence a larger core to avoid saturating), but the design is simple, extremely robust, and tolerates large transient overloads well because of its sheer physical mass and thermal capacity.

### High-frequency (HF) link designs

An "HF" inverter design instead switches the DC at a much higher frequency (commonly tens of kHz) through a small, light HF transformer, then rectifies and further processes the result to produce the final AC output. Because transformer size falls sharply with rising frequency for the same power rating, an HF transformer achieves the SAME power transformation in a small fraction of the size and weight of an equivalent LF transformer — the main reason "HF" or "transformerless-sounding" compact inverters are so much lighter than traditional LF designs of the same power rating. The trade-off is more complex power-electronics control (fast switching devices, F4.M2, and more intricate control, F4.M5) and typically a somewhat lower surge-overload tolerance than a large LF transformer's simple thermal mass provides.

### Isolation is not just about voltage ratio

Beyond stepping voltage up or down, a transformer provides [[galvanic-isolation|galvanic isolation]]: no direct conductive path exists between the battery-side and the output-side windings, energy crossing only magnetically. This isolation is a genuine safety feature in its own right (it can prevent a fault on one side from directly energising the other side with an unexpected reference), independent of whatever voltage ratio the transformer happens to provide — some inverter topologies are explicitly described as "transformerless" precisely because they trade away this isolation for a size/efficiency benefit, which has real implications for how the installation must otherwise be protected and earthed (S3).`,technical:`**Core material follows frequency.** LF transformers typically use laminated steel (acceptable loss at low frequency, adequate saturation flux density, lower cost at larger sizes); HF transformers almost always use [[ferrite|ferrite]] cores, since laminated steel's eddy-current losses would be prohibitive at tens of kHz, while ferrite's low high-frequency loss and adequate flux handling suit the smaller HF design well.

**Leakage inductance.** No real transformer couples 100 % of one winding's flux to the other; the small uncoupled fraction behaves as an effective series inductance (leakage inductance) on each winding, which matters more at higher switching frequencies and must be accounted for in HF converter design (often deliberately used, in some topologies, rather than simply minimised).`,diagrams:[{id:`hf-link`,caption:`Comparing a line-frequency transformer stage against a high-frequency switched link, for the same power transformation.`}],formulas:[{name:`Transformer size scaling (concept)`,expr:`Core size (for a given power) decreases as switching frequency increases`,where:[],note:`A qualitative design relationship, not a precise formula: higher frequency needs less flux swing per cycle for the same power, allowing a smaller core before saturating.`}],examples:[{title:`Why an HF transformer can be so much smaller`,problem:`Explain, using flux swing per cycle, why a transformer operating at 50 kHz can be dramatically smaller than one of the same power rating operating at 50 Hz.`,steps:[`Transformer EMF per turn is proportional to frequency times flux swing per cycle (from Faraday's law)`,`For the SAME voltage and turns, a 1000× higher frequency (50 kHz vs 50 Hz) allows roughly 1000× LESS flux swing per cycle for the same induced voltage`,`Less required flux swing means a far smaller core can be used before reaching its saturation flux density, for the same power rating`],answer:`The dramatically higher switching frequency lets the core do the same job with a far smaller required flux swing, which is why HF transformers are so much smaller and lighter than LF transformers of equal power rating.`},{title:`Choosing between LF and HF designs`,problem:`A rugged off-grid inverter must tolerate large, repeated surge loads (motor starting) without damage, and weight is not a major constraint. Which design philosophy (LF or HF) better suits this requirement, and why?`,steps:[`LF transformers' large thermal and physical mass gives them substantial inherent tolerance of brief large overloads`,`HF designs rely more on fast electronic protection and generally have less inherent surge-overload margin from mass alone`],answer:`An LF (transformer-based) design is often favoured for this kind of rugged, surge-tolerant application, accepting the weight penalty in exchange for robustness — exactly why many heavy-duty off-grid inverters remain LF designs despite HF alternatives being lighter.`}],applications:`Both the LF (LF-3K) and HF-influenced (SQ-1K) model inverters in the 3D Dissection Laboratory embody this exact design choice — P4's architecture tour lets you see the physical consequence of it directly.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`"HF designs are strictly better because they are smaller and lighter."`,correction:`HF designs trade control complexity and typically lower inherent surge tolerance for size and weight — the right choice depends on the application, not a universal ranking.`},{mistake:`Assuming all transformers provide galvanic isolation equally, regardless of topology.`,correction:`Some "transformerless" inverter topologies deliberately omit this isolation for other benefits, with real implications for how the installation is otherwise protected and earthed.`},{mistake:`Assuming an LF transformer's core material would work equally well at HF switching frequencies.`,correction:`Laminated steel's eddy-current losses become prohibitive at tens of kHz; ferrite is the practical HF choice.`}],demo:{title:`Tour the 3D laboratory's magnetics`,text:`Compare the physical transformer in each model inverter.`,link:e.lab3d(`Transformer and inductor`,`lf3k`,`t1`)},activities:[e.lib(`Transformer`,`transformer`),e.lab3d(`Magnetics tour`,`lf3k`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`HF transformers are typically much smaller than LF transformers of the same power rating mainly because...`,options:[`higher frequency needs less flux swing per cycle for the same induced voltage, allowing a smaller core`,`HF transformers use no core material at all`,`HF transformers carry less power`,`LF transformers are made from cheaper materials`],answer:0,explain:`Faraday's law links EMF to frequency × flux swing.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`LF transformer designs are often preferred where...`,options:[`large surge-overload tolerance from physical/thermal mass matters more than weight`,`minimum weight is the only requirement`,`galvanic isolation is unwanted`,`switching frequency must be in the tens of kHz`],answer:0,explain:`LF transformers' mass gives inherent surge tolerance.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Galvanic isolation from a transformer provides...`,options:[`no direct conductive path between the two sides, a genuine safety benefit independent of voltage ratio`,`a higher voltage ratio only`,`lower cost only`,`faster switching`],answer:0,explain:`Isolation is a separate benefit from voltage transformation.`}],summary:[`LF transformer designs switch DC directly at 50/60 Hz through a large, heavy, line-frequency transformer, with strong inherent surge tolerance.`,`HF link designs switch at tens of kHz through a small, light ferrite transformer, trading some surge-tolerance margin and control simplicity for size and weight.`,`Transformer size for a given power falls as switching frequency rises, because less flux swing per cycle is needed for the same induced voltage.`,`Galvanic isolation is a distinct safety benefit of a transformer, separate from its voltage ratio, and is deliberately absent in some "transformerless" topologies.`],homework:{id:`F4.M3.L2.hw`,title:`LF vs HF transformer reasoning`,brief:`Apply the trade-offs to a short scenario.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A portable, lightweight inverter for camping, with modest surge requirements, most likely uses a...`,options:[`high-frequency (HF) link design`,`large line-frequency (LF) transformer`,`no magnetics of any kind`,`only a battery, no transformer`],answer:0,explain:`Weight and size favour an HF design where surge tolerance is not the dominant requirement.`}],written:[{prompt:`A customer complains that their new lightweight HF-design inverter "feels less robust" than their old heavy LF-design unit, even though both are rated for the same continuous power. Explain, in engineering terms (not just "the new one is cheaper"), what real difference between the two designs could justify this impression.`,model:`The two designs can have genuinely the same continuous power rating while differing substantially in surge-overload tolerance: the old LF unit's large transformer has significant physical and thermal mass that lets it absorb a brief, large overload (such as motor starting current) without immediate damage, essentially using its bulk as a buffer. The new HF unit achieves the same continuous rating in a much smaller package specifically because it relies on fast active protection and control rather than thermal mass to survive excess current — it may protect itself correctly, but by shutting down or limiting output rather than simply "riding through" a surge the way the heavier unit's mass allows. The customer's impression reflects a real, explainable design trade-off, not simply a cheaper build.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M3.L3`,title:`Output and filter inductors`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain the role of an output inductor in an LC filter stage.`,`Relate inductor value, switching frequency and ripple current for a given application.`,`Distinguish an output/filter inductor's design priorities from a transformer's.`],introduction:`This lesson narrows F4.M3's magnetic-circuit picture to the specific component you tested conceptually in F4.M1.L3: the output or filter inductor, whose job is shaping current rather than transforming voltage or providing isolation.`,lecture:`### The output inductor's job

In an [[lc-filter|LC filter]] stage (F3.M5), the inductor sits in series with the switched output, resisting rapid changes in current and smoothing the fast-switching current pulses from the bridge into something closer to the intended output waveform, while the paired capacitor handles the voltage side of the same filtering job. Unlike a transformer, an output inductor has only one winding, provides no isolation, and its design priority is purely about current-handling and inductance value at the switching frequency in use — not voltage ratio.

### Ripple current and inductor value

A larger inductance value, for a given switching frequency and voltage, gives LESS ripple current (smoother current) but requires more turns (more copper, more size, more copper loss) and a correspondingly higher saturation-current design margin (F4.M3.L1). A smaller inductance gives a more compact, cheaper part but accepts more ripple current, which in turn means more RMS current for the same average power (worse conduction loss elsewhere) and a rougher raw waveform before capacitor smoothing. Selecting an output inductor's value is this exact balance, almost always informed by the SAME filter-design calculation (corner frequency, F3.M5) used for the paired capacitor.

### Current rating still matters most

As in F4.M1.L3, the output inductor's saturation-current and continuous-current ratings remain the dominant practical concern: an output inductor undersized for the load's real peak current will saturate under exactly the conditions (peak or surge load) where it is needed most, producing the steep, uncontrolled current rise that is this component's characteristic fault.`,technical:`**Ripple current as a fraction of average current.** A common design rule of thumb targets output-inductor ripple current at roughly 20–40 % of the average (DC or fundamental) current — enough to keep the filter compact and the inductor's own hysteresis/eddy losses reasonable, without letting ripple current dominate the RMS current (and hence the conduction losses) elsewhere in the circuit.

**Multiple inductors in a filter.** Some designs use two-stage LC filtering (an inductor-capacitor-inductor-capacitor arrangement) for steeper attenuation of switching-frequency content than a single LC stage achieves — a refinement on the same basic principle, not a different mechanism.`,diagrams:[{id:`lc-filter`,caption:`The output inductor's role in an LC filter stage, smoothing switched current into the intended output waveform.`}],formulas:[{name:`LC corner frequency`,expr:`f_c = 1 / (2π√(LC))`,where:[[`f_c`,`filter corner frequency`,`Hz`],[`L`,`output inductance`,`H`],[`C`,`filter capacitance`,`F`]],note:`Set well above the fundamental output frequency but well below the switching frequency, so the fundamental passes and switching-frequency content is attenuated (F3.M5).`}],examples:[{title:`Corner frequency of an output filter`,problem:`An output filter uses L = 2 mH and C = 10 µF. Find its corner frequency.`,steps:[`f_c = 1/(2π√(LC))`,`= 1/(2π√(0.002 × 10e-6))`,`= 1/(2π√(2e-8))`,`= 1/(2π × 1.414e-4)`],answer:`≈ 1126 Hz — comfortably above a 50/60 Hz fundamental and well below a typical multi-kHz switching frequency.`},{title:`Trade-off of a smaller inductance`,problem:`A designer halves the output inductor's value to reduce size and cost, without changing anything else. Explain the likely consequence for ripple current and for the capacitor's job.`,steps:[`Lower inductance resists current change less effectively at the same switching frequency and voltage, so ripple current rises`,`The corner frequency f_c = 1/(2π√(LC)) rises too (since L fell), which, if left unaddressed, reduces how well the filter attenuates switching-frequency content before it reaches the output`],answer:`Ripple current rises and the filter's attenuation of switching noise weakens — the capacitor would likely need to be increased to compensate and restore the original corner frequency, so the "simple" size/cost reduction is not free elsewhere in the design.`}],applications:`The DC filtering exercise in the Circuit Connection Laboratory, and the output-inductor-related readings in the simulator's filtering experiments, are this lesson applied directly.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating an output inductor as functionally the same as a transformer.`,correction:`An output inductor has one winding, no isolation and no voltage-ratio function; its job is purely current smoothing.`},{mistake:`Choosing output inductance purely to minimise size/cost, without checking the resulting ripple current and filter corner frequency.`,correction:`Inductance value, ripple current and corner frequency are all linked — changing one without checking the others can silently degrade filter performance.`},{mistake:`Assuming a higher continuous current rating alone guarantees adequate performance under peak/surge load.`,correction:`Saturation current rating, not just continuous current rating, is what matters at peak or surge load (F4.M3.L1).`}],demo:{title:`Build the DC filtering exercise`,text:`See an output inductor smoothing a switched waveform directly.`,link:e.ckt(`DC filtering`,`ckt-05`)},activities:[e.ckt(`DC filtering`,`ckt-05`),e.lib(`Inductor`,`inductor`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Corner frequency (Hz, to nearest whole number) for L=1 mH, C=20 µF?`,answer:1125,tol:.02,unit:`Hz`,explain:`f_c=1/(2π√(0.001×20e-6))≈1125 Hz.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A smaller output inductance, all else equal, gives...`,options:[`more ripple current`,`less ripple current`,`no change in ripple current`,`infinite ripple current`],answer:0,explain:`Less current-change resistance means more ripple for the same switching behaviour.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An output inductor, unlike a transformer, provides...`,options:[`no voltage-ratio transformation and no isolation`,`galvanic isolation`,`a step-up voltage ratio`,`a step-down voltage ratio only`],answer:0,explain:`It has a single winding and purely a current-smoothing role.`}],summary:[`An output/filter inductor smooths switched current in an LC filter stage, with one winding and no isolation or voltage-ratio role.`,`Inductance value trades ripple current against size, copper loss and saturation-current margin.`,`Corner frequency f_c = 1/(2π√(LC)) links the output inductor and its paired capacitor — changing one without the other shifts filter performance.`,`Saturation-current rating, not just continuous rating, is the dominant practical limit at peak or surge load.`],homework:{id:`F4.M3.L3.hw`,title:`Output filter reasoning`,brief:`Apply the corner-frequency formula and ripple reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Corner frequency (Hz, to nearest whole number) for L=3 mH, C=5 µF?`,answer:1299,tol:.02,unit:`Hz`,explain:`f_c=1/(2π√(0.003×5e-6))≈1299 Hz.`}],written:[{prompt:`An inverter's output is noticeably rougher (more residual switching-frequency content) after a repair that replaced a failed output inductor with one of a lower inductance value than the original, matched only on current rating. Explain why matching current rating alone was insufficient here.`,model:`Current rating governs whether the inductor can safely carry the operating and peak current without saturating or overheating, but it says nothing about the filter's corner frequency, which depends on BOTH the inductance value and the paired capacitor's value. A lower inductance value raises the corner frequency (f_c = 1/(2π√(LC))), weakening the filter's attenuation of switching-frequency content relative to the original design — exactly the rougher output observed. The replacement needed to match inductance value (to preserve the intended corner frequency) in addition to current rating, not current rating alone.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M3.L4`,title:`Testing windings`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Apply a winding-resistance test and an insulation test to a transformer or inductor.`,`Interpret an open, shorted-turns or insulation-breakdown reading correctly.`,`Explain why insulation testing uses a high test voltage, and the safety precautions this requires.`],introduction:`This lesson closes F4.M3 with the bench practice behind every winding-related reading you will take: how to confirm a transformer or inductor's winding is sound, using the Component Library's own test procedures.`,lecture:`### Winding resistance

A simple Ω-range (or 4-wire, for very low values, F4.M1.L4) measurement across a winding confirms basic continuity: a healthy winding reads a small, consistent resistance; an OPEN winding (a broken wire, a failed internal joint) reads OL — no path at all. This is the same reading you take for an inductor's DC resistance (F4.M1.L4), applied here to each of a transformer's windings in turn.

### Inductance, and detecting shorted turns

An LCR meter's inductance reading, compared against the healthy marked or reference value, can reveal a winding with a small number of SHORTED turns (adjacent turns whose insulation has failed and are touching) — the winding may still show reasonable continuity on a simple resistance check, since only a small fraction of turns are affected, but inductance drops noticeably because the shorted turns no longer contribute their intended share of the total magnetic effect — a fault a resistance check alone would likely miss.

### Insulation testing

Beyond the windings' own continuity, a transformer must maintain insulation BETWEEN its windings (for galvanic isolation, F4.M3.L2) and between each winding and the core/case (for safety earthing). An insulation (megohmmeter) test applies a high DC test voltage (commonly 500 V for low-voltage equipment) between these points and reads the resulting resistance — a healthy result is very high (commonly hundreds of megohms or more); a low reading indicates insulation breakdown, a genuine safety hazard (it can mean the windings are no longer properly isolated from each other or from earth) requiring the part to be taken out of service, not merely noted.

### Safety around insulation testing

Because the insulation tester itself deliberately produces several hundred volts, the test procedure requires the same isolation and discharge precautions as any other live-adjacent work, plus care to keep hands clear of the test leads during the test and to discharge the winding afterward (a winding under insulation test can retain a stored charge briefly, similar to a capacitor).`,technical:`**Turns ratio verification.** Applying a known, safe low AC voltage to a transformer's primary and measuring the resulting secondary voltage lets you verify the actual turns ratio matches the expected design value (F3.M4) — a mismatch (with otherwise healthy resistance and insulation readings) can indicate an incorrectly wound or mislabelled replacement part, a different failure mode from either an open winding or shorted turns.

**Insulation resistance and humidity.** A winding's insulation resistance reading can be temporarily depressed by surface moisture or contamination (without any real internal breakdown) — a genuinely low reading that recovers after cleaning and drying is a different situation from one that stays low, and the two should not be confused.`,diagrams:[{id:`test-bench`,caption:`The winding test sequence: resistance (continuity), inductance (shorted-turns check), then insulation (breakdown check) — each catching a different failure mode.`}],formulas:[{name:`Insulation resistance (concept)`,expr:`A healthy result is typically > 100 MΩ (varies by standard and equipment class)`,where:[],note:`A specific pass/fail limit should be taken from the applicable standard or manufacturer's specification, not assumed universally.`}],examples:[{title:`Distinguishing an open winding from shorted turns`,problem:`Winding A reads OL on resistance. Winding B reads a normal, healthy resistance but an inductance roughly 30 % below its marked value. Classify each fault.`,steps:[`Winding A: no continuity at all → open winding`,`Winding B: continuity present (healthy resistance) but reduced inductance → a small number of shorted turns, not a complete open`],answer:`Winding A has an open (broken) winding; Winding B has shorted turns — two distinct fault mechanisms that the resistance test alone would not have told apart.`},{title:`Interpreting a low insulation reading`,problem:`A transformer's primary-to-secondary insulation reads 0.4 MΩ, far below the expected >100 MΩ for a healthy part. What should be done?`,steps:[`0.4 MΩ indicates a significant insulation breakdown, not a borderline or measurement-error result`,`This compromises the galvanic isolation the transformer is relied on to provide`],answer:`The transformer should be taken out of service and replaced — a result this far below healthy is a genuine safety finding, not something to monitor and reassess later.`}],applications:`The transformer winding-resistance and insulation test procedure in the Component Library, and any Repair Workshop scenario involving a magnetic component, draw on exactly this three-part test sequence.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Checking winding resistance only, and skipping an inductance check.`,correction:`A small number of shorted turns can leave resistance looking healthy while inductance has dropped — both checks catch different fault mechanisms.`},{mistake:`Treating any non-infinite insulation reading as an immediate failure.`,correction:`Compare against the applicable standard/healthy reference; very high but not literally infinite readings are normal, and a borderline low reading recovering after cleaning may indicate surface contamination rather than true breakdown.`},{mistake:`Skipping discharge after an insulation test.`,correction:`The winding can retain a stored charge from the high test voltage briefly afterward — discharge it as part of the standard safety sequence.`}],demo:{title:`Run the winding test procedure`,text:`Work through the transformer's resistance and insulation test procedure.`,link:e.proc(`Winding resistance and insulation test`,`transformer`,`xfmr-winding`)},activities:[e.proc(`Transformer winding test`,`transformer`,`xfmr-winding`),e.proc(`Inductor L and DCR test`,`inductor`,`inductor-lcr`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A winding reading OL on a resistance check has...`,options:[`an open (broken) winding`,`shorted turns`,`perfectly healthy insulation`,`excessive inductance`],answer:0,explain:`No continuity at all indicates an open winding.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Shorted turns are best detected by...`,options:[`a reduced inductance reading, even with healthy resistance`,`resistance alone`,`colour of the winding`,`core temperature alone`],answer:0,explain:`Resistance can look normal while inductance drops from shorted turns.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An insulation test uses a high DC test voltage mainly to...`,options:[`reveal insulation breakdown that a low-voltage check would not stress enough to show`,`melt any faulty insulation`,`charge the winding permanently`,`measure turns ratio`],answer:0,explain:`Breakdown may only become apparent under realistic stress voltage.`}],summary:[`Winding resistance confirms basic continuity; an open winding reads OL (no path).`,`An inductance reading can reveal shorted turns even when resistance looks healthy.`,`Insulation testing (at a high DC test voltage) checks isolation between windings and between windings and the core/earth — a low reading is a genuine safety finding.`,`Discharge and isolation precautions apply to insulation testing, as the test itself produces several hundred volts.`],homework:{id:`F4.M3.L4.hw`,title:`Winding test interpretation`,brief:`Classify winding faults from combined readings.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A winding reads a healthy resistance AND a healthy inductance, but a low insulation resistance to the core. The fault is best classified as...`,options:[`an insulation breakdown, separate from the winding's own electrical integrity`,`an open winding`,`shorted turns`,`no fault at all`],answer:0,explain:`The winding itself is electrically sound; the fault is in its insulation to the core.`}],written:[{prompt:`A transformer passes its resistance and inductance checks but fails its insulation test. Explain why it would be incorrect to conclude the transformer is "basically fine, just needs an insulation top-up", and what the correct next step is.`,model:`Insulation breakdown is not something that can be "topped up" — it reflects actual physical degradation of the insulating material between windings or to the core, and once broken down it does not self-repair. Even though the winding's own electrical properties (resistance, inductance) remain healthy, the compromised insulation is a genuine safety hazard: it undermines the galvanic isolation the transformer is relied on to provide, and could allow unexpected voltages to appear where they should not. The correct next step is to treat the transformer as failed and replace it, not to continue using it while"monitoring" the insulation.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The saturation knee on a B-H curve marks...`,options:[`where further magnetising force produces little additional flux`,`the point of zero flux`,`the melting point of the core`,`maximum winding resistance`],answer:0,explain:`The curve flattens once the core runs out of flux capacity.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`HF transformers are typically smaller than LF transformers of equal power mainly because...`,options:[`higher frequency needs less flux swing per cycle for the same voltage`,`they carry less current`,`they use no magnetic core`,`they operate at lower voltage only`],answer:0,explain:`Faraday's law links EMF to frequency × flux swing.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Galvanic isolation from a transformer means...`,options:[`no direct conductive path between the two sides`,`a 1:1 voltage ratio`,`no magnetic core present`,`the windings touch directly`],answer:0,explain:`Energy crosses only magnetically, not via a direct conductive path.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Corner frequency (Hz, to nearest whole number) for L=2 mH, C=8 µF?`,answer:1259,tol:.02,unit:`Hz`,explain:`f_c=1/(2π√(0.002×8e-6))≈1259 Hz.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Shorted turns in a winding are best detected by...`,options:[`a reduced inductance reading, even with healthy resistance`,`resistance alone`,`visual inspection only`,`core colour`],answer:0,explain:`Resistance can look normal while inductance drops.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A low insulation-resistance reading on a transformer indicates...`,options:[`a genuine safety-relevant insulation breakdown`,`a perfectly healthy part`,`excessive turns ratio`,`normal behaviour at high humidity only`],answer:0,explain:`A significantly low reading is a genuine finding requiring action, not a normal variation.`}]},assignment:{id:`F4.M3.assignment`,title:`Magnetic component reasoning and testing`,brief:`Apply saturation, filter design and winding-test reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Corner frequency (Hz, to nearest whole number) for L=1.5 mH, C=15 µF?`,answer:1061,tol:.03,unit:`Hz`,explain:`f_c=1/(2π√(0.0015×15e-6))≈1061 Hz.`},{id:`a2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A winding reading OL on resistance has...`,options:[`an open winding`,`shorted turns`,`healthy insulation`,`excessive inductance`],answer:0,explain:`No continuity at all.`},{id:`a3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An air gap in a core trades...`,options:[`inductance per turn for a higher saturation current`,`cost for colour`,`frequency for voltage`,`nothing, it has no effect`],answer:0,explain:`A standard inductor-design technique.`}],written:[{prompt:`A technician measures a suspect output inductor's DC resistance and finds it normal, concludes the part is healthy, and refits it — but the circuit continues to show excessive output ripple. Explain what check was skipped and why it matters here.`,model:`A healthy DC resistance reading only confirms the winding has continuity; it does not rule out shorted turns or a change in effective inductance, which would not necessarily show up as a resistance change but would directly affect the filter's corner frequency and hence its ripple performance. An inductance check (compared against the marked/healthy value) was skipped, and is exactly the test that would reveal a reduced effective inductance causing the continued excessive ripple, even with normal winding resistance.`}]},practicals:[{id:`F4.M3.P1`,title:`Diagnose an output inductor fault in the Repair Workshop`,description:`Confirm, on a real circuit-grounded fault, that you can apply the winding-test reasoning from this module.`,link:e.repair(`Output inductor winding open`,`rsc-11`),evidence:{kind:`troubleshoot`,project:`rsc-11`}}]},{id:`F4.M4`,title:`Protection and switching devices`,summary:`Fuses and circuit breakers, relays and contactors, and how electromechanical switching compares with the semiconductor switching of F4.M2.`,lessons:[{id:`F4.M4.L1`,title:`Fuses: I²t, breaking capacity`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a fuse interrupts fault current and why its I²t rating matters.`,`Define breaking capacity and explain why it is a separate check from current rating.`,`Select an appropriately-rated fuse for a stated continuous and fault current.`],introduction:`Every circuit this platform teaches you to build, test or repair depends on protection devices to limit damage when something goes wrong. This lesson covers the simplest and most widely used of them: the [[fuse|fuse]].`,lecture:`### How a fuse works

A fuse is a deliberately thin, calibrated conductor element that heats up in proportion to I²R as current flows through it. Under normal (rated) current, this heating is modest and the element survives indefinitely; under fault current (far above rated), the heating rises sharply (because it scales with the SQUARE of current) and the element melts open within a predictable time, interrupting the circuit. A fuse is a one-shot, sacrificial device — once it has opened, it must be replaced, never simply reset.

### I²t: energy let-through

A fuse's **[[i2t|I²t rating]]** describes the energy it lets through before fully clearing a fault — critical for coordinating protection with whatever downstream component (a switching device, F4.M2) the fuse is meant to protect: if the protected device would be destroyed by less energy than the fuse lets through before clearing, the fuse is not actually protecting it, however correct its current rating looks on paper. Faster-acting fuses have lower I²t for the same current rating, at some cost in nuisance tripping on brief, harmless current surges (such as inrush at switch-on).

### Breaking capacity: a separate check from current rating

A fuse's [[breaking-capacity|breaking capacity]] states the maximum fault current it can safely interrupt without itself failing destructively — a fuse correctly rated for the circuit's normal and fault current can still be the WRONG choice if its breaking capacity is below the circuit's actual prospective fault current (a battery bank's very low internal resistance, F1.M2, can produce prospective fault currents far higher than many people intuitively expect). Current rating and breaking capacity answer two different questions — "does it carry normal load without nuisance tripping" and "can it survive interrupting the worst fault this circuit can produce" — and both must be checked.

### Selecting a fuse

A fuse is selected against TWO figures: the circuit's normal continuous current (it must not nuisance-trip or degrade under normal, rated load) and the circuit's worst-case fault current (it must interrupt it fast enough, and with low enough let-through energy, to protect whatever it is meant to protect, while its breaking capacity exceeds that fault current too). A rating too low causes nuisance tripping under normal load; a rating too high, or an inadequate breaking capacity, fails to protect the downstream component or the fuse itself during an actual fault.`,technical:`**Fuse speed classifications.** Fast-acting, time-delay (slow-blow) and semiconductor-protection (ultra-fast) fuse types trade nuisance-trip tolerance against let-through energy differently — a semiconductor-protection fuse, specifically designed for the very low I²t tolerance of power MOSFETs and IGBTs, is a deliberately different product from a general-purpose fuse of the same current rating.

**Voltage rating.** A fuse also carries a voltage rating, separate from its current rating — it must be able to safely extinguish the arc that forms as its element melts open at the circuit's actual voltage; using a fuse below its rated voltage does not relax this requirement, but using one rated below the circuit voltage risks the arc failing to clear, a hazard distinct from an inadequate breaking capacity.`,diagrams:[{id:`protection-chain`,caption:`A fuse's I²t let-through energy against a protected switching device's own withstand capability — the coordination check this lesson explains.`}],formulas:[{name:`Fuse let-through energy`,expr:`I²t = ∫ i²(t) dt (over the clearing time)`,where:[[`I²t`,`let-through energy`,`A²s`],[`i(t)`,`instantaneous fault current during clearing`,`A`]],note:`A protected component's withstand rating must exceed the fuse's I²t let-through for genuine protection.`}],examples:[{title:`Checking breaking capacity against prospective fault current`,problem:`A battery bank can deliver a prospective short-circuit current of 2000 A at its terminals. A fuse rated for the circuit's normal current has a breaking capacity of only 1000 A. Is this fuse suitable?`,steps:[`Breaking capacity (1000 A) must exceed the prospective fault current (2000 A) the fuse could actually see`,`1000 A < 2000 A`],answer:`No — this fuse is unsuitable despite having the correct current rating for normal load; a higher breaking-capacity device is required, or additional upstream current-limiting must be added.`},{title:`Why a correctly-rated fuse can still fail to protect a MOSFET`,problem:`A fuse is correctly rated for a MOSFET's continuous current, but its I²t let-through is higher than the MOSFET's own short-circuit withstand rating. Explain the consequence.`,steps:[`The fuse will eventually clear the fault, interrupting the circuit`,`But the MOSFET may absorb more energy during the fuse's clearing time than it can withstand, before the fuse finishes opening`],answer:`The MOSFET can be destroyed by the fault BEFORE the fuse finishes clearing it, even though the fuse itself is correctly rated for continuous current — current rating alone does not guarantee adequate protection; I²t coordination against the protected device's withstand rating must also be checked.`}],applications:`Every Build Your Own Inverter project's fuse sizing, and the Repair Workshop's blown-fuse scenario, rest on exactly this selection and coordination reasoning.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Selecting a fuse on current rating alone.`,correction:`Breaking capacity (against prospective fault current) and I²t let-through (against the protected device's withstand rating) must also be checked.`},{mistake:`Resetting a blown fuse by replacing it with a higher-rated one "to stop it blowing".`,correction:`A fuse blowing under normal conditions signals an underlying problem to diagnose; simply up-rating it removes the protection the original rating was providing.`},{mistake:`Assuming all fuses of the same current rating behave identically.`,correction:`Fast, time-delay and semiconductor-protection fuse types differ substantially in I²t let-through and nuisance-trip tolerance at the same current rating.`}],demo:{title:`See fuse ratings`,text:`Compare I²t and breaking-capacity figures for the Component Library's fuse entry.`,link:e.lib(`Fuse`,`fuse`)},activities:[e.lib(`Fuse`,`fuse`),e.proc(`Fuse check`,`fuse`,`fuse-check`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse interrupts a fault by...`,options:[`its element heating (I²R) until it melts open`,`an electromechanical trip mechanism that can be reset`,`reversing current flow`,`raising its own resistance smoothly without melting`],answer:0,explain:`A fuse is a sacrificial, heat-based device.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse's breaking capacity must be checked against...`,options:[`the circuit's prospective fault current`,`only its own price`,`the ambient room temperature only`,`the colour of its casing`],answer:0,explain:`A device with insufficient breaking capacity can fail destructively instead of safely interrupting the fault.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse correctly rated for continuous current but with I²t let-through exceeding a protected MOSFET's withstand rating...`,options:[`can still fail to protect the MOSFET during a fault`,`always protects the MOSFET perfectly`,`never blows`,`increases the MOSFET's voltage rating`],answer:0,explain:`I²t coordination against the protected device is a separate check from current rating.`}],summary:[`A fuse is a one-shot, sacrificial device that melts open from I²R heating under fault current.`,`I²t let-through energy must be checked against the withstand rating of whatever the fuse protects.`,`Breaking capacity must exceed the circuit's actual prospective fault current, separately from current rating.`,`A fuse is selected against both normal continuous current and worst-case fault current, not current rating alone.`],homework:{id:`F4.M4.L1.hw`,title:`Fuse selection practice`,brief:`Apply rating-selection reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse's I²t rating matters mainly because it describes...`,options:[`the energy let through to the protected device before the fuse fully clears`,`the fuse's physical size`,`its voltage rating only`,`how long the fuse lasts in storage`],answer:0,explain:`Let-through energy is what the protected device must actually withstand.`}],written:[{prompt:`A maintenance log shows a circuit's fuse has been replaced three times in six months, each time with an identical-rated fuse, with the circuit "working fine" immediately after each replacement. Explain why this pattern should concern a technician more than a single blown fuse would, and what it suggests about root cause versus symptom.`,model:`A fuse blowing once could plausibly be a one-off transient event, but blowing repeatedly at the same rating strongly suggests a recurring underlying condition — an intermittent overload, a developing fault elsewhere in the circuit, or a marginal design that occasionally exceeds the fuse's rating — rather than coincidence. Each replacement only restores the SYMPTOM (an open circuit) to working order without addressing whatever is repeatedly pushing current past the fuse's rating; continuing to simply replace the fuse risks eventually replacing it with something less protective (a slower-blow or higher-rated type, "to stop the nuisance") specifically because the underlying cause was never investigated. The repeated pattern itself is diagnostic information and should prompt root-cause investigation, not just another replacement.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M4.L2`,title:`Circuit breakers: thermal, magnetic`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a thermal-magnetic circuit breaker detects and interrupts overcurrent.`,`Distinguish the thermal and magnetic trip mechanisms and the fault conditions each responds to best.`,`Compare a breaker's resettable behaviour with a fuse's one-shot behaviour.`],introduction:`F4.M4.L1 covered the fuse's sacrificial, one-shot protection. This lesson covers the [[breaker|circuit breaker]]: a resettable alternative built around two distinct trip mechanisms, each suited to a different kind of overcurrent.`,lecture:`### Two trip mechanisms in one device

A common thermal-magnetic breaker combines two separate sensing mechanisms behind one switching mechanism:

- **Thermal trip**: a bimetallic strip heats (from I²R, carrying the circuit's own current) and bends as it heats, mechanically triggering the trip latch once it has heated enough — inherently SLOWER (it takes time for the strip to heat) and better suited to detecting a sustained, moderate overload that builds up gradually.
- **Magnetic trip**: a solenoid coil, carrying the same current, produces a magnetic field strong enough at HIGH current to directly pull a trip armature nearly instantaneously — much FASTER, and suited to a sudden, severe short-circuit current rather than a gradual overload.

Together, these give a breaker a trip-time characteristic that is slow at currents just above its rating (thermal-dominated) and very fast at currents far above its rating (magnetic-dominated) — exactly the shape needed to tolerate brief, harmless surges (motor starting, inrush) at moderate overcurrent while still responding quickly to a genuine short circuit.

### Why a breaker can be reset

Unlike a fuse's melted element, a breaker's trip mechanism is purely mechanical (a bent bimetallic strip returns to shape on cooling; a pulled armature can be re-latched) — once the fault is cleared, the breaker can simply be switched back on, without replacing any part. This makes breakers far more convenient for circuits expected to trip occasionally under normal operating conditions (nuisance overloads, accidental short exposure during maintenance), at a higher unit cost and, for a given size/cost, typically a somewhat higher let-through energy than an equivalently rated fast fuse.

### Selecting a breaker

As with a fuse, a breaker is selected against continuous current (avoiding nuisance tripping), fault current (tripping fast enough to protect the circuit) and breaking capacity (surviving the actual interruption) — the same three checks from F4.M4.L1, applied to a device with two trip mechanisms instead of one melting element.`,technical:`**Trip curve classes.** Breakers are commonly sold in trip-curve classes (such as B, C and D in common standards) that set how many times rated current the magnetic trip needs before acting nearly instantaneously — a class chosen to tolerate a specific load's normal inrush (a motor needs a higher-tolerance class than a purely resistive heater) without nuisance tripping.

**Electronic trip units.** Larger or more sophisticated breakers replace the bimetallic-strip and solenoid mechanism with an electronic sensing and tripping unit, offering more precisely configurable trip curves and additional protection functions — a different implementation of the same underlying thermal/magnetic trip philosophy, not a different protection concept.`,diagrams:[{id:`protection-chain`,caption:`A thermal-magnetic breaker's two trip mechanisms: a slower bimetallic-strip response to sustained overload, and a fast magnetic-solenoid response to a severe short circuit.`}],formulas:[{name:`Breaker trip-time behaviour (concept)`,expr:`Trip time falls sharply as fault current rises, thermal-dominated near rated current and magnetic-dominated at high multiples of rated current`,where:[],note:`A qualitative time-current relationship, formally captured in the breaker's published trip curve.`}],examples:[{title:`Identifying which trip mechanism responds`,problem:`A circuit draws a gradually rising overload that settles at 1.3× the breaker's rated current for several minutes before it trips. Which trip mechanism most likely responded?`,steps:[`1.3× rated current is a moderate, sustained overload, not a sudden severe fault`,`The breaker took several minutes to respond, consistent with a bimetallic strip slowly heating`],answer:`The thermal trip mechanism — its characteristic slow response to a moderate, sustained overload.`},{title:`Comparing breaker and fuse recovery after a fault`,problem:`A breaker and a fuse of similar rating both interrupt the same brief overload. Compare what is needed to restore the circuit afterward.`,steps:[`A fuse's element has melted and cannot conduct again`,`A breaker's trip mechanism is purely mechanical and has not been consumed by the event`],answer:`The breaker can simply be reset (switched back on); the fuse must be physically replaced — the defining practical difference between the two device types.`}],applications:`The circuit breaker test procedure in the Component Library, and breaker selection in any Build Your Own Inverter project, apply exactly this thermal/magnetic trip reasoning.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a breaker responds at a single, fixed overcurrent threshold regardless of how far above rated current the fault is.`,correction:`A thermal-magnetic breaker's response time varies sharply with fault current — slow near rated current, fast at high multiples of it.`},{mistake:`Believing a breaker never needs replacing, since it can be reset.`,correction:`Repeated severe trips can still mechanically wear or damage a breaker's contacts and trip mechanism over its service life, even though a single reset after a normal trip is expected.`},{mistake:`Selecting a breaker's trip-curve class without considering the load's normal inrush behaviour.`,correction:`A motor or other inrush-heavy load needs a higher-tolerance trip-curve class than a purely resistive load, to avoid nuisance tripping on normal starting current.`}],demo:{title:`See breaker ratings`,text:`Compare a thermal-magnetic breaker's trip behaviour in the Component Library.`,link:e.lib(`Circuit breaker`,`circuit-breaker`)},activities:[e.lib(`Circuit breaker`,`circuit-breaker`),e.proc(`Breaker check`,`circuit-breaker`,`breaker-check`),e.cmp(`Fuse vs breaker`,`fuse-vs-breaker`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A breaker's thermal trip responds to...`,options:[`a moderate, sustained overload, relatively slowly`,`only an instantaneous short circuit`,`low voltage only`,`reversed polarity`],answer:0,explain:`The bimetallic strip heats gradually under sustained overcurrent.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A breaker's magnetic trip responds to...`,options:[`a severe short-circuit current, nearly instantaneously`,`a slow, gradual overload`,`low voltage`,`normal rated current`],answer:0,explain:`The solenoid's field is only strong enough to trip directly at high current.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Compared with a fuse, a breaker's key practical advantage is...`,options:[`it can be reset rather than replaced after a normal trip`,`it always has lower let-through energy`,`it is always cheaper`,`it never nuisance-trips`],answer:0,explain:`Resettability is the breaker's main convenience advantage.`}],summary:[`A thermal-magnetic breaker combines a slow bimetallic thermal trip (sustained overload) with a fast magnetic trip (severe short circuit).`,`This combination gives a trip-time characteristic that tolerates brief harmless surges while still responding quickly to a genuine fault.`,`A breaker's mechanical trip mechanism allows it to be reset after a normal trip, unlike a fuse's melted element.`,`Breaker selection checks continuous current, fault current and breaking capacity, exactly as fuse selection does.`],homework:{id:`F4.M4.L2.hw`,title:`Breaker trip-mechanism practice`,brief:`Classify which trip mechanism responds to a given fault.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A dead short produces a fault current of 15× the breaker's rating, and the breaker trips within a few milliseconds. Which mechanism responded?`,options:[`the magnetic trip`,`the thermal trip`,`neither, this is too fast for any breaker`,`both equally and simultaneously by design`],answer:0,explain:`A high-multiple short-circuit current triggers the fast magnetic trip.`}],written:[{prompt:`A breaker nuisance-trips every time a particular motor starts, even though the motor's continuous running current is well within the breaker's rating. Explain, using this lesson's ideas, the most likely cause and the most appropriate fix, rather than simply fitting a higher-current-rated breaker.`,model:`Motor starting (inrush) current is typically several times the motor's continuous running current for a brief period — if the breaker's trip-curve class is too sensitive to tolerate this brief, normal inrush, its thermal (or in some designs, an overly sensitive instantaneous) trip can activate even though the motor's steady running current is well within the breaker's rating. Simply fitting a higher-current-rated breaker would reduce protection during an actual sustained overload or fault, since the new breaker would also tolerate more sustained overcurrent than the circuit should allow. The more appropriate fix is selecting a breaker with a trip-curve class suited to the motor's actual inrush profile, keeping the same current rating but changing how quickly it responds to a brief overcurrent.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M4.L3`,title:`Relays and contactors`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain how an electromagnetic relay switches a circuit using a separate control signal.`,`Distinguish a light-duty relay from a heavier-duty contactor by typical current rating and application.`,`Explain contact bounce and contact wear, and why they matter for reliability.`],introduction:`This lesson introduces a third protection/switching device family: the [[relay|relay]], which switches a circuit using an electromagnetically-actuated mechanical contact, controlled by a separate, often much smaller, signal — a different switching mechanism from the fuses and breakers of F4.M4.L1-L2, and from the semiconductor devices of F4.M2, compared directly in F4.M4.L4.`,lecture:`### Electromagnetic switching

A relay's coil, energised by a control signal, creates a magnetic field that pulls a mechanical armature, closing (or opening) a separate set of contacts carrying the switched circuit's current — completely electrically isolated from the control coil (aside from the deliberate magnetic coupling), which is a genuine advantage where the control and switched circuits must not share a conductive path, similar in spirit to the isolation a transformer provides (F4.M3.L2) but via a mechanical rather than magnetic-flux mechanism.

### Relay versus contactor

A small, light-duty relay and a larger, heavier-duty [[contactor|contactor]] work on the identical electromagnetic principle, differing mainly in current/voltage rating and construction robustness: contactor-class devices are built for the higher currents and more frequent switching cycles found in motor control and heavier power-distribution applications, often with features (arc chutes, multiple contact sets) a small relay does not need.

### Contact bounce and contact wear

When a mechanical contact closes, it typically does not settle cleanly on first touch — it physically bounces briefly, opening and reclosing several times within milliseconds ([[contact-bounce|contact bounce]]) before settling, which can cause brief current/voltage glitches a control system must sometimes be designed to tolerate or filter. Over many switching cycles, especially switching significant current, contacts also wear and pit from the small arcs that occur at each make/break — a genuine wear-out mechanism (unlike a solid-state switch, F4.M2, which has no moving parts to wear in this way) that eventually limits a relay or contactor's service life and is why switching-cycle life is a quoted rating, not an afterthought.`,technical:`**Coil suppression.** A relay or contactor coil is itself an inductor (F4.M1.L3); abruptly interrupting its drive current induces a large voltage spike (back-EMF) that can damage the driving circuit if not suppressed — a flyback diode (for DC coils) or an RC snubber (for AC coils) across the coil is standard practice, directly paralleling the freewheeling-diode reasoning already met in F4.M2.L2.

**Contact arcing at switch-off.** Interrupting current through an inductive load's contacts at switch-off can draw a small arc across the opening gap, which is part of why contacts wear and why some relays/contactors specify a derated current limit for inductive loads versus purely resistive ones.`,diagrams:[{id:`isolation-flow`,caption:`A relay's coil-actuated contact mechanism, showing the electrical isolation between the control coil circuit and the switched contact circuit.`}],formulas:[{name:`Coil back-EMF (concept)`,expr:`V_spike ∝ L · (di/dt) at coil de-energisation`,where:[],note:`The same inductive-kick reasoning as a freewheeling MOSFET load (F4.M2.L2) — a suppression diode or snubber limits it.`}],examples:[{title:`Choosing relay versus contactor`,problem:`A control circuit needs to switch a small 24 V, 200 mA signal relay coil versus a 50 A, 400 V three-phase motor. Which device suits each, and why?`,steps:[`The 200 mA signal load is well within a small relay's typical rating`,`The 50 A, 400 V motor load needs a heavy-duty device built for high current/voltage and frequent cycling — a contactor's typical application`],answer:`A relay for the small signal load; a contactor for the motor load — matching device robustness and rating to the actual switched current/voltage and expected duty cycle.`},{title:`Why a flyback diode matters when driving a relay coil from a MOSFET`,problem:`A MOSFET directly drives a relay coil with no suppression diode across the coil. Explain the risk when the MOSFET turns off.`,steps:[`The coil's stored magnetic energy (an inductor, F4.M1.L3) must go somewhere when current is interrupted`,`With no freewheeling path, this energy drives a large voltage spike across the now-open MOSFET`],answer:`The spike can exceed the MOSFET's voltage rating and destroy it — exactly the freewheeling-diode reasoning from F4.M2.L2, applied here to a relay coil rather than a motor or inductor load.`}],applications:`Battery-disconnect contactors and auxiliary relays appear throughout real inverter installations (S2, S3) for exactly the isolation and high-current switching reasons this lesson covers.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating relays and contactors as interchangeable regardless of current rating.`,correction:`Match the device's current/voltage rating and duty-cycle robustness to the actual switched load — a relay undersized for a motor-starting current will fail quickly.`},{mistake:`Omitting a flyback diode or snubber across a relay/contactor coil driven by a semiconductor switch.`,correction:`The coil's own inductive back-EMF can destroy the driving switch without suppression — the same reasoning as a freewheeling diode for any inductive load.`},{mistake:`Assuming a mechanical relay/contactor has unlimited switching-cycle life, like a solid-state device.`,correction:`Contact wear from repeated arcing is a genuine, quoted, finite-life wear-out mechanism — directly relevant to F4.M4.L4's comparison with semiconductor switches.`}],demo:{title:`See the relay entry`,text:`Compare a relay's coil and contact ratings in the Component Library.`,link:e.lib(`Relay`,`relay`)},activities:[e.lib(`Relay`,`relay`),e.proc(`Relay coil and contact test`,`relay`,`relay-coil-contacts`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A relay switches its contact circuit by...`,options:[`an electromagnetically-actuated mechanical contact, isolated from the control coil`,`direct electrical connection between control and switched circuit`,`a PN junction`,`heating a fusible element`],answer:0,explain:`Magnetic coupling, not a direct conductive path, links control and switched circuits.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Contactors differ from relays mainly in...`,options:[`current/voltage rating and construction robustness for heavier-duty switching`,`using a completely different switching principle`,`having no coil at all`,`being solid-state devices`],answer:0,explain:`Same principle, built for higher current and more frequent heavy-duty cycling.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A flyback diode across a relay coil exists to...`,options:[`suppress the back-EMF spike when coil current is interrupted`,`increase switching speed`,`reduce coil resistance`,`prevent contact bounce`],answer:0,explain:`The same inductive-kick suppression reasoning as a freewheeling diode.`}],summary:[`A relay or contactor switches a circuit via an electromagnetically-actuated mechanical contact, electrically isolated from its control coil.`,`Relays and contactors share the same principle, differing mainly in current/voltage rating and robustness for heavier-duty use.`,`A coil driven by a semiconductor switch needs flyback suppression, exactly as any other inductive load does.`,`Contact bounce and contact wear are real, quoted limitations of mechanical switching that solid-state devices do not share — the subject of the next lesson's comparison.`],homework:{id:`F4.M4.L3.hw`,title:`Relay and contactor reasoning`,brief:`Apply device-selection and coil-suppression reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A relay coil is directly driven by a microcontroller output with no suppression diode. The main risk at coil de-energisation is...`,options:[`a voltage spike that can damage the microcontroller output`,`the relay running backwards`,`the coil gaining extra inductance`,`no risk at all`],answer:0,explain:`Back-EMF from the collapsing coil field needs a suppression path.`}],written:[{prompt:`A battery-disconnect contactor in an inverter installation is reported to click audibly several times before settling into either state. Explain why this is very likely normal behaviour rather than a fault, referring to this lesson's ideas.`,model:`Mechanical contacts commonly exhibit contact bounce — a brief, rapid series of make-and-break events before settling into a stable closed or open state — as the moving contact physically settles after being actuated. An audible multiple-click sound on actuation is consistent with this well-understood, normal mechanical behaviour rather than a developing fault, provided the contactor does settle reliably into the correct final state each time and the clicking does not develop into chattering that fails to settle at all (which would be a genuine concern worth investigating separately).`}]}}},{id:`F4.M4.L4`,title:`Relays versus semiconductor switches`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Compare switching speed, isolation, wear and power loss between a relay/contactor and a semiconductor switch.`,`Explain why some designs use both switching technologies together, each for the job it suits best.`,`Select the more appropriate switching technology for a stated application.`],introduction:`This closing lesson brings F4.M2's semiconductor switches and F4.M4.L1-L3's electromechanical devices together, comparing them directly on the properties that actually decide which to use for a given job.`,lecture:`### Switching speed

A relay or contactor's mechanical armature takes milliseconds to physically move; a MOSFET or IGBT switches in nanoseconds to microseconds — a difference of several orders of magnitude. Any application needing fast, repeated switching (PWM at kilohertz frequencies, F4.M2.L7) is only practical with a semiconductor switch; a relay simply cannot physically move fast enough, and would wear out almost immediately if driven at anything close to PWM switching rates.

### Isolation

A relay/contactor provides genuine galvanic isolation between its control coil and switched contacts (F4.M4.L3) with no direct conductive path at all. A MOSFET or IGBT provides no such isolation on its own — its gate and its switched drain-source (or collector-emitter) path share a common reference, and achieving isolation in a semiconductor-switched design needs a deliberately added isolated gate driver (F4.M2.L6) or an isolation transformer elsewhere in the circuit. Where simple, robust isolation matters more than switching speed, a relay's isolation comes "for free" as part of its basic mechanism.

### Wear, reliability and power loss

A relay/contactor has a finite mechanical switching-cycle life from contact wear (F4.M4.L3); a semiconductor switch has no moving parts and, within its electrical ratings, does not wear out from switching cycles at all — a genuine reliability advantage for high-cycle-count applications. Conversely, a relay/contactor's closed contact resistance is typically extremely low (milliohms), giving very low conduction loss once closed, while a semiconductor switch's R_DS(on) or V_CE(sat) (F4.M2.L4-L5) represents a real, continuous conduction loss that a mechanical contact simply does not have.

### Using both together, each for the job it suits

Many real designs use both technologies deliberately, each where it is the better fit: a contactor for a slow, infrequent, high-isolation function (battery disconnect, main power switching) where its low conduction loss and strong isolation matter and its slow speed and finite cycle life are not limiting; semiconductor switches for the fast, frequent, PWM-rate switching inside the power conversion itself, where only they are fast enough and cycle-count durability is the governing requirement. Neither technology is universally superior — the right choice (or combination) depends on the specific switching speed, isolation, loss and reliability requirements of each specific function in the design.`,technical:`**Solid-state relays (SSRs).** An SSR packages a semiconductor switch together with an isolated (commonly optical) input stage, aiming to combine a relay's simple isolated control interface with a semiconductor's switching speed and absence of mechanical wear — a deliberate hybrid, at the cost of still having some conduction loss (unlike a mechanical contact's near-zero closed resistance) and generally a lower surge-current tolerance than an equivalent mechanical contactor.

**Contact rating versus semiconductor current rating.** A relay/contactor's current rating for inductive loads is commonly derated relative to its rating for purely resistive loads, reflecting the extra contact wear from inductive switching arcs (F4.M4.L3) — a consideration with no direct semiconductor-switch equivalent, since a properly protected semiconductor switch (with correct freewheeling-diode handling, F4.M2.L2) does not experience an equivalent switching arc.`,diagrams:[{id:`protection-chain`,caption:`Relay/contactor switching and semiconductor switching compared on speed, isolation, wear and conduction loss — each suited to different roles within the same design.`}],formulas:[{name:`Qualitative comparison`,expr:`Relay/contactor: slow, isolated, wears mechanically, very low closed-contact loss. Semiconductor: fast, not inherently isolated, no mechanical wear, real conduction loss (I²R_DS(on) or V_CE(sat)·I).`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Choosing a switching technology for a battery disconnect`,problem:`A design needs to disconnect a battery bank completely, perhaps a few times a day at most, with strong isolation and minimal standing conduction loss. Which switching technology fits better?`,steps:[`Switching frequency required is very low (a few times a day) — well within a contactor's mechanical speed`,`Strong isolation and minimal standing loss both favour the contactor's properties over a semiconductor switch's`],answer:`A contactor fits this application well — its slow switching speed is not a limitation here, and its isolation and low conduction loss are genuine advantages for this specific role.`},{title:`Why a relay cannot replace a MOSFET in a PWM bridge leg`,problem:`Explain why a relay could not be substituted for a MOSFET switching at 20 kHz in an inverter's bridge leg.`,steps:[`20 kHz switching means a new switching event roughly every 50 microseconds`,`A relay's mechanical armature takes milliseconds to move — orders of magnitude too slow to track this switching rate`,`Even if it somehow kept up, the contact wear from millions of switching cycles per second-equivalent would destroy it almost instantly`],answer:`A relay is fundamentally too slow (by several orders of magnitude) and would wear out essentially immediately at this switching rate — this role is only achievable with a semiconductor switch.`}],applications:`This exact comparison — fast, lossy, wear-free semiconductor switching for PWM versus slow, isolated, low-loss mechanical switching for disconnects — explains the mix of device families you will see across every real inverter design and Build Your Own Inverter project.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating one switching technology as universally better than the other.`,correction:`Each has genuine advantages (speed and no wear for semiconductors; isolation and low conduction loss for relays/contactors) suited to different roles.`},{mistake:`Assuming a relay could substitute for a semiconductor switch in a high-frequency PWM application.`,correction:`Mechanical switching speed is orders of magnitude too slow for PWM-rate switching, and would wear out almost immediately if attempted.`},{mistake:`Assuming a semiconductor switch provides isolation "for free" like a relay does.`,correction:`A semiconductor switch needs a deliberately added isolated gate driver or transformer to achieve isolation; it is not inherent to the device the way it is for a relay.`}],demo:{title:`Compare the two switching families directly`,text:`Use the comparison guide to see relay and semiconductor switching properties side by side.`,link:e.cmp(`Relay vs semiconductor switch`,`relay-vs-semiconductor`)},activities:[e.cmp(`Relay vs semiconductor switch`,`relay-vs-semiconductor`),e.lib(`MOSFET`,`mosfet`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A relay cannot be used for kilohertz-rate PWM switching mainly because...`,options:[`its mechanical switching speed is orders of magnitude too slow`,`it has no coil`,`it conducts in only one direction`,`it requires no control signal`],answer:0,explain:`Millisecond mechanical movement versus microsecond switching needs.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A relay/contactor provides isolation...`,options:[`inherently, as part of its basic electromagnetic mechanism`,`only with an added isolated driver`,`never, under any circumstance`,`only in AC circuits`],answer:0,explain:`No direct conductive path exists between coil and contacts.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A semiconductor switch, once fully on, has...`,options:[`a real, continuous conduction loss (I²R_DS(on) or V_CE(sat)·I)`,`exactly zero conduction loss`,`infinite conduction loss`,`a mechanical wear-out mechanism identical to a relay's`],answer:0,explain:`Unlike a near-zero-resistance closed mechanical contact.`}],summary:[`A relay/contactor switches far more slowly than a semiconductor switch, but provides inherent isolation and very low closed-contact conduction loss.`,`A semiconductor switch has no mechanical wear-out mechanism and can switch orders of magnitude faster, but has real conduction loss and no inherent isolation.`,`Neither technology is universally better; many real designs deliberately use both, each for the role it suits.`,`Solid-state relays attempt to combine properties of both, trading some of each technology's pure advantages for a hybrid compromise.`],homework:{id:`F4.M4.L4.hw`,title:`Switching technology selection practice`,brief:`Choose the appropriate switching technology for stated applications.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An application needs to switch a load a few times per hour, with strong isolation and no concern for switching speed. The better-suited technology is...`,options:[`a relay or contactor`,`a MOSFET switching at 50 kHz`,`an IGBT switching at 20 kHz`,`neither device family is appropriate`],answer:0,explain:`Low switching rate plus isolation strongly favours electromechanical switching.`}],written:[{prompt:`A design uses a contactor for the main battery disconnect and MOSFETs for the PWM bridge leg within the same inverter. Explain why this is a deliberate, sound engineering choice rather than an inconsistency, using this lesson's comparison.`,model:`The two switching functions have genuinely different requirements that favour different technologies: the battery disconnect switches rarely, needs strong isolation for safety, and benefits from the contactor's very low closed-contact conduction loss standing in the main current path continuously; the PWM bridge leg must switch at a rate many orders of magnitude beyond any mechanical device's capability, where only a semiconductor switch is fast enough and durable against the resulting switching-cycle count. Using the technology best suited to each specific role — rather than picking one technology and forcing it to do both jobs — is exactly the reasoning this lesson's comparison supports, not an inconsistency in the design.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse interrupts a fault by...`,options:[`its element melting open from I²R heating`,`an electromechanical trip that resets`,`reversing polarity`,`raising voltage`],answer:0,explain:`A sacrificial, heat-based mechanism.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse's breaking capacity must exceed...`,options:[`the circuit's prospective fault current`,`only its own continuous current rating`,`the ambient temperature`,`the supply frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Otherwise it can fail destructively instead of interrupting safely.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A breaker's magnetic trip mechanism responds to...`,options:[`a severe short-circuit current, nearly instantaneously`,`a slow, gradual overload`,`low voltage`,`reversed polarity`],answer:0,explain:`The solenoid's field is only strong enough to trip directly at high current.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A relay/contactor coil needs flyback suppression because...`,options:[`its collapsing magnetic field induces a damaging voltage spike on de-energisation`,`it has no inductance`,`it always runs on DC only`,`it cannot be driven by semiconductors`],answer:0,explain:`The same inductive-kick reasoning as any inductive load.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A relay cannot be used for kilohertz-rate PWM switching mainly because...`,options:[`its mechanical switching speed is orders of magnitude too slow`,`it conducts in only one direction`,`it has no coil`,`it requires isolation`],answer:0,explain:`Millisecond mechanical movement versus microsecond switching needs.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A semiconductor switch, unlike a relay/contactor, does not inherently provide...`,options:[`galvanic isolation between control and switched circuit`,`any switching function at all`,`any conduction loss`,`any current rating`],answer:0,explain:`Isolation needs a deliberately added isolated driver or transformer.`}]},assignment:{id:`F4.M4.assignment`,title:`Protection and switching device reasoning`,brief:`Apply fuse/breaker selection and switching-technology comparison reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse correctly rated for continuous current but with excessive I²t let-through for its protected device will...`,options:[`potentially fail to protect that device during a fault`,`always protect it perfectly`,`never blow`,`increase the device's voltage rating`],answer:0,explain:`I²t coordination is a separate, necessary check.`},{id:`a2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A breaker's thermal trip, compared with its magnetic trip, responds...`,options:[`more slowly, to a moderate sustained overload`,`faster, to any current level`,`only to reversed polarity`,`only above its breaking capacity`],answer:0,explain:`Thermal response time is inherently slower than the magnetic trip's.`},{id:`a3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A relay/contactor provides isolation and low conduction loss but is unsuited to PWM-rate switching mainly because of...`,options:[`its mechanical switching speed`,`its voltage rating`,`its coil resistance`,`its contact material colour`],answer:0,explain:`Mechanical movement cannot track PWM-rate switching.`}],written:[{prompt:`A technician proposes replacing a worn relay, used to switch a load roughly once per second in a control application, with a MOSFET "to eliminate the wear problem". Explain what would be gained and what would need to be added to preserve the relay's other properties.`,model:`Switching once per second is well within a semiconductor switch's capability, and the substitution would indeed eliminate the mechanical contact-wear issue, since a MOSFET has no moving parts to wear from switching cycles. However, the relay's inherent galvanic isolation between its control signal and switched load would be lost, since a MOSFET's gate and drain-source path share a common reference — an isolated gate driver (or some other isolation method) would need to be deliberately added to preserve the isolation the original relay provided "for free". The substitution is reasonable, but it is not simply a drop-in replacement; the isolation requirement must be explicitly re-addressed, not assumed to carry over automatically.`}]},practicals:[{id:`F4.M4.P1`,title:`Diagnose a blown fuse in the Repair Workshop`,description:`Confirm, on a real circuit-grounded fault, that you can apply the continuity-test and root-cause reasoning from this module.`,link:e.repair(`Blown fuse`,`rsc-02`),evidence:{kind:`troubleshoot`,project:`rsc-02`}}]},{id:`F4.M5`,title:`Control, sensing and thermal`,summary:`PWM controllers and microcontrollers, voltage and current sensing, heat sinks and thermal resistance, and the PCBs, connectors and terminals that carry it all.`,lessons:[{id:`F4.M5.L1`,title:`PWM controllers and microcontrollers`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain the role of a dedicated PWM controller versus a general-purpose microcontroller in generating switching signals.`,`Describe closed-loop feedback control at a conceptual level: measuring the output and adjusting the switching pattern to correct it.`,`Explain why a control fault (lost feedback, frozen firmware) can be just as serious as a power-stage fault.`],introduction:`Every switching device and gate driver this module has covered needs a source of switching commands. This lesson covers where those commands come from: a dedicated [[pwm-controller|PWM controller]] or a [[microcontroller|microcontroller]], and the [[feedback|feedback]] loop that lets either one correct the output rather than simply running open-loop.`,lecture:`### Dedicated PWM controllers

A dedicated PWM controller IC (such as the SG3525 family referenced in the Component Library) is a purpose-built analog/mixed-signal chip that generates PWM switching signals from a reference and feedback input, with built-in features (soft-start, under-voltage lockout, dead-time generation) specifically for power conversion — simple to apply, robust, but limited to the control strategy its fixed internal architecture supports.

### Microcontroller-based control

A general-purpose microcontroller instead runs FIRMWARE (software) that computes the switching pattern, reads feedback via its analog-to-digital converter, and can implement essentially any control algorithm the firmware author writes — far more flexible (supporting sophisticated control strategies, communication, protection logic and user interfaces all in one chip) at the cost of needing correctly-written and maintained firmware, and introducing software as a new class of potential fault (a firmware bug or a frozen process can misbehave in ways a simpler dedicated PWM IC's fixed hardware logic cannot).

### Closed-loop feedback: measuring and correcting

Either approach typically operates in a **closed loop**: a sensor (F4.M5.L2) measures the actual output (voltage, current), the controller compares this measurement against the DESIRED (reference) value, and adjusts the switching pattern to reduce the difference — continuously correcting for load changes, input voltage variation, and component tolerance that an open-loop (fixed, uncorrected) switching pattern could not compensate for. This feedback loop is what keeps an inverter's output voltage and frequency accurate under varying load, not the switching devices or transformer alone.

### A control fault is a real fault, not a lesser one

Because the controller determines every switching decision, a fault in the control path — lost or corrupted feedback, a frozen firmware process, a damaged PWM controller IC — can be just as serious as a fault in the power stage itself: a controller that loses its feedback signal might drive the output to an uncontrolled, potentially damaging voltage, just as surely as a shorted MOSFET can damage downstream equipment. Diagnosing "no output" or "wrong output" fault symptoms genuinely requires considering the control path, not only the power devices, as a candidate cause.`,technical:`**Digital vs analog control.** A microcontroller's digital control loop runs at a finite sample rate and with finite numerical resolution — fast and precise enough for virtually all inverter applications with modern hardware, but a genuinely different set of design considerations (sampling, quantization, computation delay) from a dedicated analog PWM IC's continuous-time operation.

**Watchdog timers.** Microcontroller-based designs commonly include a watchdog timer: a separate hardware mechanism that resets the microcontroller if its firmware fails to "check in" periodically, specifically to recover from a frozen or hung firmware process without needing a human to intervene — a deliberate design response to the software-fault risk this lesson raises.`,diagrams:[{id:`feedback-loop`,caption:`A closed feedback loop: a sensor measures the actual output, the controller compares it against the desired reference, and adjusts the switching pattern to correct any difference.`}],formulas:[{name:`Closed-loop correction (concept)`,expr:`switching pattern ← f(reference − measured feedback)`,where:[],note:`A qualitative relationship: the controller adjusts output based on the error between desired and measured values, not a fixed formula applicable to every control scheme.`}],examples:[{title:`Reasoning about a lost-feedback fault`,problem:`An inverter's output voltage rises well above its normal regulated value shortly after a voltage-sensing connector becomes loose. Explain the likely mechanism.`,steps:[`With the feedback connector loose, the controller no longer receives an accurate measurement of the actual output voltage`,`A controller that reads an artificially LOW feedback value (consistent with an open/disconnected sensing path) would interpret this as "output too low" and increase switching duty to compensate`],answer:`The controller, misled by the lost feedback signal into believing the output is too low, drives the switching pattern harder, actually raising the real output voltage well above normal — a classic lost-feedback runaway, and a genuine control-path fault, not a power-stage fault.`},{title:`Dedicated PWM IC vs microcontroller trade-off`,problem:`A simple, fixed-function battery charger needs reliable, predictable control with minimal software risk. Which control approach better fits this requirement?`,steps:[`A dedicated PWM controller IC's behaviour is fixed by its internal hardware design, with no firmware to write, debug or potentially hang`,`A microcontroller would add flexibility this simple, fixed-function application does not need, along with firmware-related fault risk it does not need either`],answer:`A dedicated PWM controller IC is often the better fit here — simplicity and predictability matter more than the flexibility a microcontroller would add, for this specific, fixed-function application.`}],applications:`The PWM controller and microcontroller test procedures in the Component Library, and any Repair Workshop scenario involving "no output" or "output out of regulation" symptoms, depend on exactly this control-path reasoning.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming an output fault must be in the power stage (switches, transformer) rather than the control path.`,correction:`A lost feedback signal or a firmware fault can produce symptoms (wrong, unstable or absent output) that look identical to a power-stage fault — the control path must be considered too.`},{mistake:`Treating a microcontroller as strictly superior to a dedicated PWM IC.`,correction:`A microcontroller adds flexibility and software-fault risk together; a fixed-function, simplicity-first application can genuinely be better served by a dedicated PWM IC.`},{mistake:`Believing an inverter's output accuracy comes from the switching devices alone, without feedback.`,correction:`Feedback is what corrects for load changes, input variation and component tolerance — an open-loop design without it would drift under these changes.`}],demo:{title:`See the PWM controller entry`,text:`Compare the PWM controller and microcontroller entries' roles in the Component Library.`,link:e.lib(`PWM controller`,`pwm-controller`)},activities:[e.lib(`PWM controller`,`pwm-controller`),e.lib(`Microcontroller`,`microcontroller`),e.cmp(`PWM controller vs microcontroller`,`pwm-vs-mcu`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A dedicated PWM controller IC, compared with a microcontroller, is typically...`,options:[`simpler and more predictable, but less flexible`,`always more flexible`,`incapable of generating PWM at all`,`only usable in digital circuits`],answer:0,explain:`Fixed hardware behaviour trades flexibility for predictability.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A closed feedback loop corrects the switching pattern based on...`,options:[`the difference between the desired reference and the measured actual output`,`the ambient room temperature only`,`a fixed, pre-programmed pattern that never changes`,`the switching frequency alone`],answer:0,explain:`Error (reference minus measurement) drives the correction.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A lost or corrupted feedback signal can cause...`,options:[`the output to run away toward an incorrect, potentially damaging value`,`no effect, since feedback is optional`,`the switching devices to automatically shut down safely in every case`,`only a cosmetic display error`],answer:0,explain:`The controller may drive the output based on a false reading, with no inherent limit.`}],summary:[`A dedicated PWM controller IC offers simple, predictable, fixed-function control; a microcontroller offers flexible, firmware-defined control with added software-fault risk.`,`Closed-loop feedback measures the actual output and corrects the switching pattern based on its difference from the desired reference.`,`A control-path fault (lost feedback, frozen firmware) can produce symptoms as serious as a power-stage fault.`,`Watchdog timers are a deliberate hardware response to the risk of a hung microcontroller firmware process.`],homework:{id:`F4.M5.L1.hw`,title:`Control path reasoning`,brief:`Reason about feedback and control-path faults.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An inverter's output voltage is found to be unstable and oscillating, with all power-stage devices testing healthy out of circuit. The most important remaining area to check is...`,options:[`the feedback/control path`,`the enclosure paint finish`,`the AC output terminal labelling`,`nothing further, the fault cannot exist`],answer:0,explain:`With power devices confirmed healthy, the control path is the natural remaining candidate.`}],written:[{prompt:`A technician, finding an inverter's output badly out of regulation, immediately replaces several power-stage MOSFETs without testing them first, assuming the power stage must be at fault. The output remains out of regulation after the replacement. Explain what reasoning error was made, and what should have been checked first.`,model:`The technician assumed the fault's LOCATION (power stage) without first gathering evidence to support that assumption — replacing parts is not a diagnostic step, and doing so before confirming where the fault actually lies risks exactly this outcome: the real fault (which this lesson suggests could plausibly be in the feedback or control path, not the power stage at all) remains unaddressed, and the replaced parts were likely never faulty. The correct approach is to test the suspect devices out of circuit (or the control path's feedback signals) BEFORE replacing anything, letting the evidence identify the fault's actual location rather than guessing and replacing speculatively.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M5.L2`,title:`Voltage and current sensing`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Compare resistive-divider voltage sensing with shunt and Hall-effect current sensing.`,`Explain why a sensor's own accuracy and scaling must be verified, not merely its presence.`,`Explain how a sensing fault can masquerade as a power-stage or load fault.`],introduction:`F4.M5.L1's feedback loop is only as trustworthy as the sensors feeding it. This lesson covers the two measurements every control loop in this platform depends on: [[voltage-sensor|voltage sensing]] and [[current-sensor|current sensing]].`,lecture:`### Voltage sensing

Measuring a voltage the controller can safely read (commonly a low-voltage ADC input, 0–3.3 V or 0–5 V) from a much higher real circuit voltage is most simply done with a resistive voltage divider (F2's divider principle), scaled so the real operating voltage maps to a safe ADC range. Isolated voltage sensing (where the measurement circuit must not share a direct conductive path with the high-voltage circuit, for safety or noise-isolation reasons) uses other techniques (isolation amplifiers, voltage-sensing transformers) beyond a simple divider.

### Current sensing: shunt versus Hall-effect

A **shunt resistor** (a precision, very low-value resistor placed in the current path) senses current by measuring the small voltage drop across it (V=IR) — simple, accurate and inexpensive, but NOT isolated (it sits directly in the current path) and introduces a small additional series resistance and associated power loss (F4.M1.L1). A [[hall-effect|Hall-effect]] current sensor instead measures the magnetic field around the conductor without breaking the circuit or adding series resistance, giving genuine galvanic isolation between the sensed circuit and the measurement electronics, at typically somewhat lower accuracy and higher cost than a well-designed shunt.

### Verifying scaling and accuracy, not just presence

A sensor that is electrically connected and producing SOME signal is not necessarily producing a CORRECT signal: a voltage-divider ratio that has drifted (from a resistor aging out of tolerance, F4.M1.L1), a shunt whose resistance has changed, or a Hall-effect sensor whose zero-current offset has drifted can each cause the controller to act on a subtly WRONG measurement while appearing, superficially, to be working — exactly the scenario the Component Library's sensing test procedures (vsense-scale, hall-zero) are designed to catch.

### Sensing faults can masquerade as other faults

A drifted voltage or current sensor feeding bad information into the feedback loop of F4.M5.L1 can cause the controller to drive the power stage incorrectly, producing symptoms (wrong output voltage, apparent overcurrent tripping under normal load) that look exactly like a power-stage or load fault — correctly diagnosing this requires checking the SENSOR'S OWN accuracy, independent of the power stage, not just assuming the power stage must be the cause because that is where the symptom is observed.`,technical:`**Sensor bandwidth.** A current sensor's bandwidth (how fast it can track a changing current) matters for protection functions expected to respond quickly to a fast-developing fault — a sensor too slow to track a rapidly rising short-circuit current can delay the very protection response it is meant to trigger, a genuine design consideration beyond steady-state accuracy alone.

**Common-mode rejection.** An isolated sensing technique's ability to reject noise or voltage common to both its inputs (common-mode rejection) matters particularly in high-voltage, high-dV/dt switching environments, where a poorly isolated or poorly filtered sensing path can pick up switching noise that corrupts the measurement the control loop relies on.`,diagrams:[{id:`feedback-loop`,caption:`Voltage and current sensors feeding the same closed feedback loop that F4.M5.L1 introduced, each needing independent accuracy verification.`}],formulas:[{name:`Shunt current sensing`,expr:`I = V_shunt / R_shunt`,where:[[`I`,`measured current`,`A`],[`V_shunt`,`voltage drop across the shunt`,`V`],[`R_shunt`,`shunt resistance`,`Ω`]]}],examples:[{title:`Interpreting a shunt measurement`,problem:`A 10 mΩ shunt shows a 0.18 V drop. What current does this represent?`,steps:[`I = V/R`,`= 0.18/0.01`],answer:`= 18 A.`},{title:`Diagnosing a masquerading sensing fault`,problem:`A circuit repeatedly trips its overcurrent protection under what the load clearly indicates is a normal, moderate load. Power-stage devices test healthy out of circuit. Suggest where to look next.`,steps:[`A normal-seeming load producing an apparent overcurrent trip suggests the MEASUREMENT, not necessarily the actual current, may be wrong`,`A drifted current sensor (shunt resistance changed, or a Hall-effect sensor's zero-offset drifted) could report a falsely high current to the controller even though the real current is normal`],answer:`The current sensor's own accuracy (its scaling/zero-offset against a known reference) should be checked next — a sensing fault reporting falsely high current would exactly produce this symptom while leaving the power stage genuinely healthy.`}],applications:`The voltage-sensor scaling check and the Hall-effect zero-offset check in the Component Library are exactly the verification this lesson describes, applied on the bench.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a sensor reading something is the same as the sensor reading correctly.`,correction:`Scaling drift or zero-offset drift can leave a sensor producing a plausible-looking but wrong signal — verify against a known reference, not just presence of a signal.`},{mistake:`Treating a shunt and a Hall-effect sensor as simply interchangeable.`,correction:`A shunt is simpler and more accurate but not isolated and adds series loss; a Hall-effect sensor is isolated and lossless in the current path but typically less accurate and more costly.`},{mistake:`Diagnosing an apparent overcurrent trip by assuming the power stage must be drawing too much current.`,correction:`A drifted current sensor can report a false overcurrent reading while the actual current is normal — check sensor accuracy independently.`}],demo:{title:`Run a sensor scaling check`,text:`Verify a voltage sensor's scaling against a known reference voltage.`,link:e.proc(`Voltage sensor scaling check`,`voltage-sensor`,`vsense-scale`)},activities:[e.proc(`Voltage sensor scaling`,`voltage-sensor`,`vsense-scale`),e.proc(`Hall-effect zero check`,`current-sensor`,`hall-zero`),e.cmp(`Voltage vs current sensing`,`voltage-vs-current-sensing`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Current (A) from a 20 mΩ shunt showing 0.24 V?`,answer:12,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`I=0.24/0.02=12 A.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A shunt resistor, compared with a Hall-effect sensor, is typically...`,options:[`simpler and more accurate, but not isolated and adds series loss`,`isolated and lossless but less accurate`,`always more expensive`,`incapable of measuring DC current`],answer:0,explain:`A direct series resistance measurement, not isolated.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A current sensor with drifted zero-offset can cause...`,options:[`a false overcurrent (or undercurrent) reading, even with genuinely normal load current`,`no effect on the control loop`,`the load to physically draw more current`,`the power stage to overheat regardless of actual current`],answer:0,explain:`A wrong measurement feeds a wrong decision into the control loop.`}],summary:[`Voltage sensing commonly uses a resistive divider; current sensing uses a shunt (simple, accurate, not isolated) or a Hall-effect sensor (isolated, lossless, less accurate).`,`A sensor producing some signal is not the same as producing a correct one — scaling and zero-offset must be independently verified.`,`A drifted sensor can feed wrong information into the feedback loop, producing symptoms that look like a power-stage or load fault.`,`Diagnosing such symptoms requires checking the sensor's own accuracy, not only the power stage where the symptom appears.`],homework:{id:`F4.M5.L2.hw`,title:`Sensing fault reasoning`,brief:`Apply shunt calculations and masquerading-fault reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Current (A) from a 5 mΩ shunt showing 0.15 V?`,answer:30,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`I=0.15/0.005=30 A.`}],written:[{prompt:`A battery charger's controller reports a battery voltage reading that is consistently 2 V higher than a trusted handheld meter measures at the same terminals. Explain the likely fault category, and why simply recalibrating the displayed number in software (without further investigation) would be a risky fix.`,model:`A consistent, fixed offset between the controller's reading and a trusted reference strongly suggests a voltage-sensing scaling or offset fault (for example, a divider resistor drifted out of tolerance) rather than an actual battery voltage discrepancy, since the true battery voltage is the same regardless of which instrument reads it. Simply adjusting the software's displayed number to match the trusted meter would mask the symptom without fixing the underlying sensing fault, and critically, the CONTROLLER's feedback loop (F4.M5.L1) uses the raw, uncorrected sensor reading to make its actual charging decisions — a software display offset would not fix the wrong value the control loop itself is acting on, leaving the charger still making decisions based on an inaccurate measurement even though the displayed number now looks right.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M5.L3`,title:`Thermal management: heat sinks and fans`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Apply thermal resistance to estimate a device's junction temperature from its total loss and ambient temperature.`,`Explain how forced-air cooling (a fan) changes the thermal resistance a heat sink achieves.`,`Explain derating and why running a device below its absolute maximum rating is standard, not conservative, practice.`],introduction:`F4.M2.L7 showed every switching device dissipates real power as heat. This lesson covers where that heat actually goes: [[thermal-resistance|thermal resistance]], [[heat-sink|heat sinks]] and [[airflow|forced-air cooling]], and the [[derating|derating]] discipline that ties them together.`,lecture:`### Thermal resistance: a circuit analogy

Heat flows from a device's junction, through its package, through a heat sink, and finally to the surrounding air — and this path can be modelled, very usefully, exactly like an electrical circuit: temperature difference plays the role of voltage, heat flow (power) plays the role of current, and [[thermal-resistance|thermal resistance]] (°C/W) plays the role of electrical resistance. Junction temperature rise above ambient is then simply total power loss multiplied by the TOTAL thermal resistance of the whole path (junction-to-case, case-to-sink, sink-to-ambient, summed in series exactly like series electrical resistances, F4.M1.L1's series-resistor reasoning applied to heat instead of current).

### Heat sinks and forced air

A heat sink increases the surface area in contact with the surrounding air, lowering the sink-to-ambient thermal resistance compared with the bare device package alone. Adding a fan (forced-air cooling) lowers this thermal resistance FURTHER, by continuously replacing the warmed air near the heat sink's surface with cooler air rather than relying on natural convection alone — a heat sink's rated thermal resistance is typically quoted for BOTH natural convection and one or more specific airflow rates, since the same heat sink genuinely performs very differently under each condition.

### Derating: margin is standard practice, not caution for its own sake

Running a device meaningfully below its absolute maximum rated junction temperature (and below its maximum current/voltage ratings) — [[derating|derating]] — is standard engineering practice, not excessive caution: component tolerance, ambient temperature variation, airflow degradation over the product's life (dust accumulation, fan wear), and the accuracy limits of the thermal model itself all consume some of the nominal margin between normal operation and the absolute maximum rating. A design that runs right at the edge of a device's maximum rating under ideal, freshly-manufactured conditions has effectively no margin left for any of these real-world variations, and is considered poor practice precisely because of this, not merely "less safe than ideal".`,technical:`**Thermal runaway risk.** Because several semiconductor parameters (leakage current, F4.M2.L9; R_DS(on), F4.M2.L4) worsen with rising temperature, inadequate cooling can create a positive feedback loop — [[thermal-runaway|thermal runaway]] — where rising temperature increases loss, which raises temperature further, potentially destroying the device well before its nominal steady-state loss calculation alone would suggest, which is exactly why cooling design margin matters more than a single steady-state calculation might imply.

**Fan reliability as a wear-out component.** A cooling fan is itself a mechanical, wearing component (bearings, moving blades) with a finite service life — a fan failure silently removes the forced-air cooling a thermal design was relying on, which is why some designs include fan-fault detection (tachometer feedback, airflow sensing) rather than assuming the fan will simply always be running.`,diagrams:[{id:`thermal-network`,caption:`The series thermal-resistance path from a device's junction, through its case and heat sink, to the surrounding ambient air — modelled exactly like a series electrical circuit.`}],formulas:[{name:`Junction temperature rise`,expr:`T_j = T_a + P · R_th(total)`,where:[[`T_j`,`junction temperature`,`°C`],[`T_a`,`ambient temperature`,`°C`],[`P`,`total device power loss`,`W`],[`R_th(total)`,`total junction-to-ambient thermal resistance`,`°C/W`]]}],examples:[{title:`Estimating junction temperature`,problem:`A device dissipates 10 W with a total thermal resistance of 3 °C/W, at an ambient of 35 °C. Estimate junction temperature.`,steps:[`T_j = T_a + P × R_th`,`= 35 + 10×3`],answer:`= 65 °C.`},{title:`Checking margin against a maximum rating`,problem:`The same device has a maximum rated junction temperature of 150 °C. Comment on the design's margin, and on what happens if ambient rises to 50 °C on a hot day with the same loss and thermal resistance.`,steps:[`At 35 °C ambient: T_j = 65 °C, which is 85 °C below the 150 °C maximum — a generous margin`,`At 50 °C ambient: T_j = 50 + 10×3 = 80 °C, still 70 °C below maximum`],answer:`The design retains a comfortable margin even at the higher ambient — exactly the kind of margin derating is meant to preserve against real-world variation, rather than designing to only just meet the rating at the lowest expected ambient.`}],applications:`The heat-sink/fan test procedures and the Build Your Own Inverter projects' thermal sizing both depend directly on this series thermal-resistance model and derating discipline.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Calculating junction temperature using only the heat sink's thermal resistance, ignoring junction-to-case resistance.`,correction:`All series thermal-resistance stages (junction-to-case, case-to-sink, sink-to-ambient) must be summed for an accurate total, exactly as series electrical resistances are summed.`},{mistake:`Assuming a heat sink's rated thermal resistance applies regardless of whether a fan is present.`,correction:`The same heat sink has a meaningfully different (higher) thermal resistance under natural convection alone than with forced air — use the rating that matches the actual cooling condition.`},{mistake:`Designing to just meet the maximum junction temperature rating under ideal, fresh conditions.`,correction:`Derating margin accounts for component tolerance, ambient variation and cooling degradation over the product's life — running at the theoretical edge leaves no real-world margin.`}],demo:{title:`Test a heat sink and fan`,text:`Run the heat-sink isolation check and the fan bench test.`,link:e.proc(`Heat sink isolation check`,`heat-sink`,`heatsink-isolation`)},activities:[e.proc(`Heat sink isolation check`,`heat-sink`,`heatsink-isolation`),e.proc(`Fan bench test`,`cooling-fan`,`fan-bench`),e.cmp(`Heat sink vs fan`,`heatsink-vs-fan`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Junction temperature (°C) for P=8 W, R_th=4 °C/W, T_a=30 °C?`,answer:62,tol:.02,unit:`°C`,explain:`30+8×4=62 °C.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Adding forced-air cooling (a fan) to a heat sink...`,options:[`lowers its effective thermal resistance compared with natural convection alone`,`has no effect on thermal resistance`,`always raises thermal resistance`,`only matters for AC circuits`],answer:0,explain:`Continuously replacing warmed air improves heat removal.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Derating a device below its absolute maximum rating is considered...`,options:[`standard practice, preserving margin for real-world variation`,`unnecessary caution with no real justification`,`only relevant to overvoltage, never temperature`,`a sign of a poor design`],answer:0,explain:`Margin accounts for tolerance, ambient variation and degradation over life.`}],summary:[`Heat flow from junction to ambient can be modelled as a series thermal-resistance circuit, directly analogous to electrical resistance.`,`Junction temperature rise equals total power loss times total junction-to-ambient thermal resistance.`,`Forced-air cooling lowers effective thermal resistance compared with natural convection alone, for the same heat sink.`,`Derating (running below absolute maximum ratings) preserves margin for component tolerance, ambient variation and cooling degradation over the product's life.`],homework:{id:`F4.M5.L3.hw`,title:`Thermal calculation practice`,brief:`Apply the junction-temperature formula and margin reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Junction temperature (°C) for P=12 W, R_th=2.5 °C/W, T_a=40 °C?`,answer:70,tol:.02,unit:`°C`,explain:`40+12×2.5=70 °C.`}],written:[{prompt:`An inverter that has run reliably for two years begins shutting down on thermal protection during hot summer afternoons, with no change to its load. Using this lesson's ideas, suggest a plausible explanation that does not require assuming a sudden component failure.`,model:`A design that originally had adequate derating margin under its original ambient and cooling conditions can lose that margin gradually over time without any single component failing outright: dust accumulation on the heat sink and fan blades raises the effective thermal resistance of the cooling path, and a fan's bearings wearing over two years of service can reduce its airflow below its original rating — both effects raise junction temperature for the SAME power loss and ambient temperature as before. Combined with naturally higher ambient temperatures on hot summer afternoons, a design that had comfortable margin at installation could gradually lose enough of that margin to begin tripping thermal protection, without any individual power component having failed — cleaning the heat sink/fan and checking fan performance would be a reasonable first investigation before suspecting a power-stage fault.`}]}}},{id:`F4.M5.L4`,title:`PCBs, connectors and terminals`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a PCB trace, a connector and a high-current terminal can each independently develop a high-resistance fault.`,`Apply a voltage-drop-under-load test to find a high-resistance joint.`,`Explain why a loose or corroded connection is often more dangerous, not less, than a dead short.`],introduction:`This closing lesson covers the humble, easily-overlooked components that carry every signal and every amp of current between all the others F4 has covered: the [[pcb|PCB]] and its [[connector|connectors]] and terminals — collectively one of the most common real-world sources of an inverter fault.`,lecture:`### PCB traces: thin copper, real limits

A printed circuit board carries both power and signal connections as thin copper tracks etched onto an insulating board. A trace is rated for a specific current (set by its width and copper thickness) and, like any conductor, dissipates I²R heat — a trace that is too narrow for the current it carries, or one that has been weakened by age, corrosion, or an earlier overcurrent event, can develop a high-resistance "hot spot" that heats, discolours the board, and in a severe case chars and opens completely. Repeated thermal cycling (heating under load, cooling when idle) can also crack a trace or a solder joint over time, even without ever exceeding the trace's rated current in any single event.

### Connectors and terminals: where most real joints actually fail

A [[connector|connector]] or a bolted/crimped terminal joins two conductors mechanically, and this mechanical joint — not the conductors themselves — is overwhelmingly the most common real-world point of failure in an otherwise healthy circuit: a connector that has loosened (from vibration, thermal cycling, or simply not being torqued correctly when installed) or corroded (from moisture, or dissimilar metals in contact) develops increased contact resistance at exactly the joint, which heats under load (I²R again) in a way that a simple no-load continuity check will completely miss, since the joint can look perfectly continuous with no current flowing through it.

### Finding a high-resistance joint: voltage drop under load

The standard test for exactly this fault is a voltage-drop measurement ACROSS the joint WHILE IT IS CARRYING LOAD CURRENT: a healthy joint shows only a few millivolts of drop even at substantial current; a degraded joint shows a much larger drop, directly revealing its elevated resistance — and, combined with the current flowing, the real power being dissipated AT that joint (P=I×V_drop), which is often the more useful number for judging how serious the fault actually is.

### Why a loose connection can be more dangerous than a dead short

A dead short is usually caught quickly by protection devices (F4.M4) sized for exactly that fault current. A loose or corroded connection, by contrast, often draws CURRENT WITHIN NORMAL RANGE for the circuit (the connection still passes current, just with excess resistance) — meaning no fuse or breaker trips, while the joint itself quietly heats, sometimes for a long time, as a genuinely dangerous, undetected fire and failure risk that normal overcurrent protection is not designed to catch at all.`,technical:`**Dissimilar-metal corrosion.** Joining two different metals (such as copper and aluminium) directly, without a compatible anti-corrosion compound or plating, can accelerate galvanic corrosion at the joint in the presence of moisture — a specific, well-known cause of connector/terminal degradation beyond simple age or vibration.

**Torque specifications.** Bolted terminals (battery terminals, DC input/output lugs) commonly specify a torque value for a reason: under-torquing leaves excess contact resistance from the start; over-torquing can deform or crack a lug or a PCB pad — both ends of the torque range cause the same underlying fault (a poor mechanical/electrical joint), for different reasons.`,diagrams:[{id:`circuit-loop`,caption:`A voltage-drop-under-load measurement across a suspect connector or terminal joint, revealing elevated resistance that a no-load continuity check would miss entirely.`}],formulas:[{name:`Joint heating from contact resistance`,expr:`P_joint = I · V_drop`,where:[[`P_joint`,`power dissipated at the joint`,`W`],[`I`,`current flowing through the joint`,`A`],[`V_drop`,`voltage drop measured across the joint under that load`,`V`]]}],examples:[{title:`Interpreting a voltage-drop reading`,problem:`A battery terminal shows a 4 mV drop at 70 A (healthy reference) versus a 180 mV drop at the same 70 A on a suspect terminal. Compute the power dissipated at the suspect joint and compare.`,steps:[`Healthy: P = 70 × 0.004 = 0.28 W`,`Suspect: P = 70 × 0.18 = 12.6 W`],answer:`The suspect joint dissipates roughly 45 times more power than the healthy reference at the same current — a genuinely dangerous, localised heating fault that a simple continuity check (both would read as "continuous") would never reveal.`},{title:`Why a loose connection evades normal overcurrent protection`,problem:`Explain why a fuse correctly sized for a circuit's normal current does not protect against a loose connection in that same circuit.`,steps:[`The circuit's actual CURRENT remains within the fuse's normal operating range — the load still draws its usual current through the (resistive) joint`,`The fuse only responds to current magnitude, not to where in the circuit that current is being dissipated as unwanted heat`],answer:`The fuse sees nothing abnormal in terms of current and has no way to detect that some of the circuit's normal current is being wastefully (and dangerously) dissipated as heat at a bad joint rather than reaching the load — a fundamentally different fault signature from the overcurrent conditions fuses and breakers are designed to catch.`}],applications:`The battery-terminal, DC-input-lug, AC-output-terminal and main-PCB test procedures in the Component Library are exactly this voltage-drop-under-load technique, applied to the specific joints most likely to degrade in a real inverter installation.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Confirming a connector or terminal is healthy with a no-load continuity check alone.`,correction:`A degraded joint can still read as fully continuous with no current flowing — test voltage drop UNDER LOAD to reveal elevated resistance.`},{mistake:`Assuming a connection fault will be caught by the circuit's normal overcurrent protection.`,correction:`A loose or corroded joint typically does not raise the circuit's overall current beyond normal, so fuses and breakers do not respond to it at all — a fundamentally different fault type.`},{mistake:`Over-torquing a terminal "to be extra safe" against loosening.`,correction:`Over-torquing can itself damage the lug or PCB pad, causing the same class of high-resistance fault from the opposite direction — torque to the specified value, not beyond it.`}],demo:{title:`Run a terminal voltage-drop check`,text:`Measure voltage drop across a battery terminal under load.`,link:e.proc(`Terminal voltage-drop check`,`battery-terminals`,`terminal-drop`)},activities:[e.proc(`Battery terminal check`,`battery-terminals`,`terminal-drop`),e.proc(`PCB trace inspection`,`main-pcb`,`pcb-inspect`),e.proc(`AC output terminal check`,`ac-output`,`ac-terminals`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A degraded (loose or corroded) connector most reliably shows up as...`,options:[`elevated voltage drop across the joint when tested under load`,`an open circuit on a simple no-load continuity check`,`a change in colour visible without disassembly`,`a change in the circuit's overall current draw`],answer:0,explain:`No-load continuity can look perfectly healthy even with significant contact resistance.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power dissipated (W) at a joint with 50 mV drop at 60 A?`,answer:3,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P=I×V=60×0.05=3 W.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A loose connection typically evades normal fuse/breaker protection because...`,options:[`the circuit's overall current stays within normal range, even though heat is wasted at the joint`,`fuses specifically detect loose connections`,`loose connections always draw zero current`,`breakers trip instantly on any resistance increase`],answer:0,explain:`Protection devices respond to current magnitude, not to where heat is being wastefully dissipated.`}],summary:[`PCB traces and connectors/terminals can each independently develop high-resistance faults from aging, corrosion, vibration or incorrect torque.`,`A voltage-drop measurement across a joint while it carries load current reveals elevated resistance that a no-load continuity check completely misses.`,`Joint heating power (P=I×V_drop) is often the more useful figure than resistance alone for judging how serious a connection fault is.`,`A loose or corroded connection typically evades normal overcurrent protection, since it does not raise the circuit's overall current — making it a genuinely dangerous, often-undetected fault type.`],homework:{id:`F4.M5.L4.hw`,title:`Connector and terminal fault practice`,brief:`Apply voltage-drop-under-load reasoning.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power dissipated (W) at a joint with 120 mV drop at 40 A?`,answer:4.8,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P=40×0.12=4.8 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`A battery terminal tests as fully continuous (0 Ω-range reading) with the battery disconnected and no load applied, yet the inverter it supplies intermittently shuts down under heavy load. Explain why the no-load test did not catch the fault, and what test should be run instead.`,model:`A no-load continuity test only confirms that SOME conductive path exists across the joint; it cannot reveal a joint whose resistance has increased from a healthy near-zero value to something still low enough to read as "continuous" on a basic meter range, but high enough to cause significant I²R heating and voltage drop once real load current flows through it. This is exactly the fault signature a loose or lightly corroded terminal produces — fine at no load, problematic under the higher current that heavy load demands. A voltage-drop-under-load test (measuring the actual millivolt drop across the joint while the inverter is delivering its heavy load current) is the correct test, since it directly reveals the elevated resistance that only becomes significant once real current is flowing.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A closed feedback loop corrects the switching pattern based on...`,options:[`the difference between desired reference and measured output`,`ambient temperature only`,`a fixed pattern that never changes`,`switching frequency alone`],answer:0,explain:`Error-driven correction is the basis of closed-loop control.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Current (A) from a 10 mΩ shunt showing 0.3 V?`,answer:30,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`I=0.3/0.01=30 A.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A current sensor with drifted zero-offset can cause...`,options:[`a false over/undercurrent reading despite normal real current`,`no effect on the system`,`the load to change its real current`,`the fuse to change rating`],answer:0,explain:`The control loop acts on the wrong measurement.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Junction temperature (°C) for P=15 W, R_th=2 °C/W, T_a=25 °C?`,answer:55,tol:.02,unit:`°C`,explain:`25+15×2=55 °C.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Derating below absolute maximum ratings is...`,options:[`standard practice preserving real-world margin`,`unnecessary caution`,`only relevant to voltage, never temperature`,`a sign of poor design`],answer:0,explain:`Margin accounts for tolerance, ambient variation and degradation.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A degraded connector or terminal is most reliably found by...`,options:[`a voltage-drop measurement across the joint under load`,`a no-load continuity check alone`,`visual inspection only`,`checking the circuit's overall current draw`],answer:0,explain:`No-load continuity can look healthy even with significant contact resistance.`}]},assignment:{id:`F4.M5.assignment`,title:`Control, sensing and thermal integration`,brief:`Apply sensing, thermal and fault-tracing reasoning together.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Current (A) from a 15 mΩ shunt showing 0.45 V?`,answer:30,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`0.45/0.015=30 A.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Junction temperature (°C) for P=6 W, R_th=5 °C/W, T_a=32 °C?`,answer:62,tol:.02,unit:`°C`,explain:`32+6×5=62 °C.`},{id:`a3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A healthy power stage with a collapsed near-zero output most likely indicates...`,options:[`a control/feedback fault driving duty cycle incorrectly low`,`a shorted MOSFET`,`a blown fuse`,`an oversized heat sink`],answer:0,explain:`A control-path explanation fits better than a power-stage one here.`}],written:[{prompt:`A system exhibits intermittent shutdowns that correlate with both high ambient temperature AND high load, but never with either alone. Using this module's ideas, explain why this specific combination (not either factor alone) is diagnostically meaningful, and what subsystem it points toward.`,model:`Junction temperature rise is the product of power loss and thermal resistance, added to ambient temperature (T_j = T_a + P·R_th) — both high ambient AND high load independently push this sum higher, so a design with just enough margin to handle EITHER condition alone comfortably could still exceed its thermal protection threshold only when BOTH combine, exactly matching the reported pattern. This points toward thermal management (F4.M5.L3) being at or near its design margin — rather than a sensing, control or power-stage component fault, which would typically not depend on this specific combined-condition pattern — and suggests investigating cooling performance (heat sink condition, fan function, derating margin) rather than immediately suspecting a failed component.`}]},practicals:[{id:`F4.M5.P1`,title:`Thermal and sensing calculation set`,description:`Apply shunt current sensing and junction-temperature calculations together. All answers must be correct.`,task:{id:`f4-thermal-sensing`,kind:`calc`,title:`Sensing and thermal calculations`,brief:`A MOSFET dissipates 9 W total loss with a junction-to-ambient thermal resistance of 3.5 °C/W at a 30 °C ambient. Its current is measured by an 8 mΩ shunt showing a 0.24 V drop.`,questions:[{id:`c1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Junction temperature (°C)?`,answer:61.5,tol:.02,unit:`°C`,explain:`30+9×3.5=61.5 °C.`},{id:`c2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Measured current (A) from the shunt?`,answer:30,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`0.24/0.008=30 A.`},{id:`c3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`If the shunt's true resistance has drifted up by 20% without recalibration, the controller will believe current is...`,options:[`lower than it actually is`,`higher than it actually is`,`unaffected`,`exactly zero`],answer:0,explain:`I=V/R: a higher R for the same V reads as a lower computed current, so the controller under-reads the true current.`}],explain:`Thermal margin and sensing accuracy are independent checks that both feed into whether a system runs safely and is correctly controlled — this task exercises both calculations together.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`f4-thermal-sensing`}},{id:`F4.M5.P2`,title:`Diagnose a gate-driver / control-path fault in the Repair Workshop`,description:`Confirm, on a real circuit-grounded fault, that you can apply the cross-subsystem fault-tracing reasoning from this module.`,link:e.repair(`Gate-driver signal lost`,`rsc-10`),evidence:{kind:`troubleshoot`,project:`rsc-10`}}]}],exam:{pass:.8,minutes:60,attempts:3,cooldownHours:24,questions:[{id:`e1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power dissipated (W) by a 22 Ω resistor carrying 0.5 A?`,answer:5.5,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P=I²R=0.25×22=5.5 W.`},{id:`e2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A capacitor's ESR mainly causes...`,options:[`self-heating from ripple current, worsening with age`,`an increase in capacitance over time`,`reverse polarity protection`,`a decrease in voltage rating`],answer:0,explain:`ESR dissipates I²·ESR as heat; it typically rises as electrolytic capacitors age.`},{id:`e3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An inductor driven beyond its saturation current rating...`,options:[`loses most of its inductance, and current rises sharply`,`gains additional inductance`,`becomes a perfect open circuit`,`converts to a capacitor`],answer:0,explain:`The core can no longer contribute additional flux once saturated.`},{id:`e4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A PN junction conducts easily when...`,options:[`forward biased beyond its barrier voltage`,`reverse biased`,`at absolute zero temperature`,`disconnected`],answer:0,explain:`Forward bias shrinks the depletion barrier.`},{id:`e5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`MOSFET conduction loss (W) for R_DS(on)=15 mΩ at 20 A RMS?`,answer:6,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`20²×0.015=400×0.015=6 W.`},{id:`e6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`IGBT conduction loss is best modelled as...`,options:[`P ≈ V_CE(sat) × I`,`P = I²R`,`P = V²/R`,`zero, always`],answer:0,explain:`A nearly constant saturation voltage, unlike a resistance.`},{id:`e7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Dead time in a bridge leg exists mainly to prevent...`,options:[`shoot-through (both switches on at once)`,`excessive gate charge`,`reverse polarity`,`ESR rise`],answer:0,explain:`Both switches on at once would short the DC bus.`},{id:`e8`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Switching loss (W) for (E_on+E_off)=40 µJ at 20 kHz?`,answer:.8,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`40e-6×20000=0.8 W.`},{id:`e9`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A device reading 0.00 V in both diode-test directions, where one should show a diode drop, has failed...`,options:[`short`,`open`,`with normal leakage`,`within tolerance`],answer:0,explain:`No blocking behaviour in either direction.`},{id:`e10`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Before fitting a non-identical replacement MOSFET, which rating is it safe to skip checking?`,options:[`None — voltage, current, on-state loss and gate characteristics should all be checked`,`Current rating`,`Voltage rating`,`Gate characteristics`],answer:0,explain:`Every relevant rating should be checked; none is safe to skip.`},{id:`e11`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`HF transformers are typically much smaller than LF transformers of equal power rating mainly because...`,options:[`higher frequency needs less flux swing per cycle for the same induced voltage`,`HF transformers carry less power`,`HF transformers use no core material`,`HF transformers operate at lower voltage only`],answer:0,explain:`Faraday's law links EMF to frequency × flux swing.`},{id:`e12`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`LC filter corner frequency (Hz, to nearest whole number) for L=2 mH, C=10 µF?`,answer:1126,tol:.02,unit:`Hz`,explain:`f_c=1/(2π√(0.002×10e-6))≈1126 Hz.`},{id:`e13`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Shorted turns in a winding are best detected by...`,options:[`a reduced inductance reading, even with healthy resistance`,`resistance alone`,`visual inspection only`,`core colour`],answer:0,explain:`Resistance can look normal while inductance drops.`},{id:`e14`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A fuse's breaking capacity must exceed...`,options:[`the circuit's prospective fault current`,`only its own continuous current rating`,`the ambient temperature`,`the supply frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Otherwise it can fail destructively instead of interrupting safely.`},{id:`e15`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A breaker's magnetic trip mechanism responds to...`,options:[`a severe short-circuit current, nearly instantaneously`,`a slow, gradual overload`,`low voltage`,`reversed polarity`],answer:0,explain:`The solenoid's field is only strong enough to trip directly at high current.`},{id:`e16`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A relay cannot be used for kilohertz-rate PWM switching mainly because...`,options:[`its mechanical switching speed is orders of magnitude too slow`,`it conducts in only one direction`,`it has no coil`,`it requires isolation`],answer:0,explain:`Millisecond mechanical movement versus microsecond switching needs.`},{id:`e17`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A closed feedback loop corrects the switching pattern based on...`,options:[`the difference between desired reference and measured output`,`ambient temperature only`,`a fixed pattern that never changes`,`switching frequency alone`],answer:0,explain:`Error-driven correction is the basis of closed-loop control.`},{id:`e18`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Current (A) from a 10 mΩ shunt showing 0.25 V?`,answer:25,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`I=0.25/0.01=25 A.`},{id:`e19`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Junction temperature (°C) for P=10 W, R_th=3 °C/W, T_a=30 °C?`,answer:60,tol:.02,unit:`°C`,explain:`30+10×3=60 °C.`},{id:`e20`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Derating below absolute maximum ratings is...`,options:[`standard practice preserving real-world margin`,`unnecessary caution`,`only relevant to voltage, never temperature`,`a sign of poor design`],answer:0,explain:`Margin accounts for tolerance, ambient variation and degradation.`},{id:`e21`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A degraded connector or terminal is most reliably found by...`,options:[`a voltage-drop measurement across the joint under load`,`a no-load continuity check alone`,`visual inspection only`,`checking the circuit's overall current draw`],answer:0,explain:`No-load continuity can look healthy even with significant contact resistance.`},{id:`e22`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A near-immediate repeat failure of a like-for-like replacement semiconductor most strongly suggests...`,options:[`an unaddressed external root cause, not coincidence`,`a bad batch of parts, nothing more`,`the component family is universally unreliable`,`the voltage rating was too high`],answer:0,explain:`Root-cause investigation, not just replacement, is needed.`}]}};export{t as F4};